window.VOCAB = [{"w": "abate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-BAYT", "also": "abatement (noun)", "d1": "Reduce, diminish", "sent": "Her stress over spending so much money on a house <b>abated</b> when the real estate broker told her about the property’s 15 year tax <b>abatement</b>.", "related": "Tax abatement is used in the same way as tax “relief”—that is, a partial discount. Subside is another word for lessening (a storm could abate or subside).", "moreinfo": "Abate comes from an Old French word for “beat, cast down” that also gives us batter (beat severely) and abattoir (slaughterhouse).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "a-BATE: let the debate abate, the shouting dies down and cools off.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "abdicate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "AB-di-cayt", "also": "abdication (noun)", "d1": "Formally give up the throne (or some other power or responsibility)", "sent": "King Edward VIII of England famously <b>abdicated</b> the throne in order to marry an American divorcée.\nParents can be charged with neglect for <b>abdicating</b> their responsibilities towards their children.", "related": "Don’t confuse abdicate with dethrone and depose, which refer to forcing a leader from power. Abdication is voluntary.", "moreinfo": "Abdicate comes from the root “ab” (away) and “dic/dict” (proclaim), the latter of which also appears in dictator, dictionary, dictate, dictum, and indict.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "ab (away) + dictate: the king who dictated walks AWAY from the throne.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "aberrant", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AB-er-int", "also": "aberration (noun)", "d1": "Abnormal, deviant", "sent": "The teen’s <b>aberrant</b> behavior made his family suspect that he was using drugs.\nLosing rather than gaining weight over the holidays is certainly an aberration.", "related": "Anomaly (deviation from the norm, inconsistency), Outlier (a person on the fringe; a data point that lies outside the main pattern of data)", "moreinfo": "The prefix “ab” means “away”—in this case, away from what’s normal.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "ab (away) + errant (erring): strayed away from what is normal.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "abhor", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ab-HORE", "also": "", "d1": "Detest, regard with disgust", "sent": "“Go out with you?” she replied. “I <b>abhor</b> you! I would rather stab myself with a rusty bread knife than be your girlfriend!”", "related": "Loathe, Abominate (synonyms), Antipathy (instinctive repugnance or aversion)", "moreinfo": "The prefix “ab” means “away”—if you abhor (or abominate) something, you want to get as far away from it as possible.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "ab-HORROR: you view it with pure horror, you cannot stand it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "abjure", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ab-JOOR", "also": "", "d1": "Give up, renounce; repudiate, recant, or shun (especially formally or under oath)", "sent": "To become a citizen of the United States, you must <b>abjure</b> loyalty to the nation of your birth.\nSince enrolling in that nutrition class, she has <b>abjured</b> sugar and saturated fats.", "related": "Forswear (reject or renounce under oath; swear falsely in court), Eschew (shun, avoid, abstain from)", "moreinfo": "Abjure can be used in the same way as renounce. You renounce worldly pleasures, a religion or family member, membership in a group, etc. It can also be used in the same way as repudiate, which is more often used with ideas, as in “Galileo repudiated the belief that the Sun revolves around the Earth.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "ab (away) + jure (swear, as in jury): you swear it away, formally renounce it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "abrasive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "uh-BRAY-siv", "also": "abrade (verb)", "d1": "Rough, suitable for grinding or polishing (such as sandpaper); causing irritation or annoyance", "sent": "Could the inside of this mascot costume be any more <b>abrasive</b>? It’s rubbing my skin raw! I have some seriously <b>abrasive</b> remarks for whoever designed this thing.", "related": "Caustic (capable of burning or corroding; extremely critical or sarcastic), Excoriate (to rub the skin off of; to criticize very harshly)", "moreinfo": "Like caustic and excoriate, abrasive can be used literally or metaphorically. You scrub a dirty pan with something abrasive, such as steel wool. Harsh criticism is abrasive, like being scrubbed with steel wool.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "sounds like \"a bruise\": sandpaper, or a rude person, rubs you raw.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "abridge", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-BRIDGE", "also": "abridged (adj)", "d1": "Reduce or lessen; shorten by omitting parts throughout while retaining the main idea", "sent": "Our romantic vacation was <b>abridged</b> when the babysitter called to say that the kids were sick and we should come home.\nAudio books are almost always <b>abridged</b>, since few people want to listen to a 200-hour book.", "related": "Truncate (shorten by cutting off a part) Memory Trick: When you abridge a book, you cut out sections from all over (hopefully without being too obvious)—that is, you create a bridge from the last part you kept in to the next part you kept in, so people don’t notice the missing bits. An abridged book still tells the whole story; a truncated book is missing the ending!", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "an a-BRIDGE cuts straight across, skipping the long way; a shortened version.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "abstain", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ab-STAIN", "also": "abstemious (adj.)", "d1": "Hold back, refrain (especially from something bad or unhealthy); decline to vote", "sent": "The church board voted on whether to hold an abstinence rally to encourage young people not to become sexually active; while most members voted in favor, one voted against and two <b>abstained</b>, with one <b>abstainer</b> commenting that, as far as she knew, the church’s teens were pretty abstemious already.", "related": "Temperance (moderation, holding back), Teetotaler (a person who abstains from alcohol), Forbear (hold back or abstain from)", "moreinfo": "Abstain is usually followed by “from” (vegetarians forbear meat or abstain from meat).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "abs + stain: keep your abs clean, hold back from the bad stuff.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "acme", "pos": "noun", "pron": "ACK-mee", "also": "", "d1": "Summit, peak, highest point", "sent": "The <b>acme</b> of my vacation was when I finally climbed to the <b>acme</b> of the mountain and enjoyed the gorgeous vista.", "related": "Summit, Pinnacle (synonyms), Apex (vertex, tip, point), Apogee (high point, point at which the moon is furthest from the Earth)", "moreinfo": "Acme, Summit, Pinnacle, and Apex are all popular names for businesses: Apex Locksmith, Pinnacle Home Security, etc. (Acme is especially popular, since it occurs early in the phone book).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "ACME, the top brand Wile E. Coyote orders, the very highest point.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "activism", "pos": "noun", "pron": "ACK-ti-vizm", "also": "activist (noun)", "d1": "The practice of pursuing political or other goals through vigorous action, often including protests and demonstrations", "sent": "Lindsay’s parents had a hard time accepting that, after incurring $100,000 in student loans, their daughter had decided to enter the low-paying field of environmental <b>activism</b>.", "related": "Advocacy (pleading for, recommending), Champion (one who defends or supports, as a cause)", "moreinfo": "Activism is often considered noble, but judicial activism—going beyond interpreting existing laws to actually using court decisions to create new public policies—is often controversial.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "active-ism: taking vigorous active action, protests and marches.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "adhere", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ad-HERE", "also": "", "d1": "Stick (to), such as with glue, or to a plan or belief", "sent": "I have a message board that <b>adheres</b> to my refrigerator with magnets; on it, I’ve written some affirmations to help me <b>adhere</b> to my diet plan.", "related": "Abide by (follow, conform to), Cohere (become united, hold together as part of the same mass)", "moreinfo": "Use adhere for attaching two different kinds of things together, and cohere for things of the same kind (good cookie dough coheres instead of crumbles).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "add-HERE: glue sticks it right here, or you stick to a plan.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "admonish", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ad-MAHN-ish", "also": "admonition (noun)", "d1": "Mildly scold; caution, advise, or remind to do something", "sent": "She was an exacting boss who upbraided an employee for jamming the copier, yet she merely <b>admonished</b> her five-year-old for the same offense.", "related": "Reprove, upbraid, reprimand, and rebuke are all harsher forms of criticism than admonish.", "moreinfo": "You can also admonish someone to do something, as in “The GRE instructor admonished her students to study vocabulary every day.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "a gentle \"a-MONk-ish\" warning to behave, a mild scolding.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "adverse", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AD-verss", "also": "adversity (noun)", "d1": "Opposing, harmful", "sent": "Pioneer women persevered despite <b>adverse</b> circumstances, even when fording a river—baby in one arm, leading a horse with the other—against an <b>adverse</b> current.", "related": "Antagonistic (hostile, acting in opposition)", "moreinfo": "Adverse appears in “adverse criticism,” although that expression is a bit redundant. Adverse is also related to adversary (an opponent, foe).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "an advert working against you: harmful, opposing conditions.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "advocate", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "AD-voh-kayt (v), AD-voh-kit (n)", "also": "", "d1": "Speak or argue in favor of (verb); a person who pleads for a cause or on behalf of another person (noun)", "sent": "I cannot possibly vote for a candidate who <b>advocates</b> oil drilling in federally protected nature preserves.\nChildren often have <b>advocates</b> appointed to represent them in court.", "related": "Proponents, Exponents, or Champions are all people who advocate for a cause.", "moreinfo": "Advocate contains the root “voc,” meaning “to call.” This root also appears in vocal, invoke, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "add-vocal: add your voice in favor of a cause.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "aesthetic", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "ess-THEH-tick", "also": "aesthete (noun)", "d1": "Concerning the appreciation of beauty or good taste, pertaining to the science of what is beautiful (adj); a sense of beauty and taste of a particular time and place (noun)", "sent": "The twins were so different—one possessed a truly baroque <b>aesthetic</b>, preferring golden, gilded decor. The other lived in a world of pure logic, untouched by any <b>aesthetic</b> sense whatsoever; art did not move him, his house was bare, and he married his wife solely because she was a master of calculus.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The meaning of aesthetic can range from the academic study of beauty (aesthetics) to performing manicures, the work of an aesthetician. Don’t confuse aesthetic with ascetic (pertaining to a simple, austere life with no luxuries, such as that of a monk; a person who leads such a life).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "think aesthetics at a salon, all about beauty and good looks.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "affable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AFF-uh-bull", "also": "affability (noun)", "d1": "Warm and friendly, pleasant, approachable", "sent": "The professional wrestler played at belligerence in the ring, but in real life, he was quite an <b>affable</b> fellow—sociable, easy-going, and always ready to lend a hand.", "related": "Amiable, Cordial, and Genial are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "The opposite of affable could be standoffish, discourteous, or distant.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "able to be spoken to, easy to befriend: warm and approachable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "affectation", "pos": "noun", "pron": "aff-eck-TAY-shun", "also": "affected (adj)", "d1": "Fake behavior (such as in speech or dress) adopted to give a certain impression", "sent": "I’m annoyed whenever Americans move to England and suddenly start speaking with an affected British accent; such <b>affectations</b>, when practiced by celebrities, are only likely to alienate their fans.", "related": "Artifice (trickery, especially as part of a strategy)", "moreinfo": "In slang, some people call affected behavior “being a poser” (or poseur).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "an affected pose, a put-on act adopted to impress.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "aggrandize", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-GRAND-ize", "also": "self-aggrandizing (adj)", "d1": "Make greater; exaggerate", "sent": "I can’t stand when my coworker <b>aggrandizes</b> her role in our group projects.\nStop it with your constant self-<b>aggrandizing</b>—we don’t care how many automobiles you own!", "related": "Augment (to make larger)", "moreinfo": "An opposite of self-aggrandizing could be self-deprecating or self-effacing.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "a-GRAND-ize: make it grand, puff it up bigger.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "aggregate", "pos": "verb, adj", "pron": "AG-gruh-gayt (v), AG-gruh-git (n)", "also": "", "d1": "Gather together, amount to (verb); constituting a whole made up of constituent parts (adj)", "sent": "While some of the company’s divisions did better than others, in <b>aggregate</b>, we made a profit.\nConcrete is created when crushed rock or glass is <b>aggregated</b> with cement; in <b>aggregate</b>, concrete is stronger than cement alone.", "related": "Agglomerate (collect into a mass), Consolidate (unite, combine, firm up—you can consolidate loans or consolidate power)", "moreinfo": "Aggregate can be used in the same sense as a gross amount. Gross or aggregate sales are the total amount from all sources.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "a-GREG-ate: Greg gathers everyone into one combined total.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "alacrity", "pos": "noun", "pron": "uh-LACK-rit-tee", "also": "", "d1": "Cheerful or speedy willingness", "sent": "Any boss wants an employee to respond with <b>alacrity</b> to his or her requests, handling them promptly and with enthusiasm.", "related": "Brisk (speedy, energetic, vigorous), Sprightly (lively, full of spirit)", "moreinfo": "Alacrity has an adjective form, alacritous, but phrases like “full of alacrity” or “with alacrity” are much more common.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "\"a-LACK-of-delay\": cheerful, speedy eagerness.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "alienate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "AY-lee-en-ayt", "also": "inalienable (adj)", "d1": "Cause to become unfriendly, hostile, or distant", "sent": "The talk-show host was trying to help, but only <b>alienated</b> her viewers when she suggested that they cope with a tough economy by checking themselves into a spa.", "related": "Disaffect (cause to lose affection or loyalty), Estrange (make hostile or indifferent —“He hasn’t spoken to his estranged son in a decade.”)", "moreinfo": "In law, alienate means to transfer property to another owner. An inalienable right is one that you cannot give away or sell (for instance, it is not possible for a person to sell himself into slavery).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "alien-ate: treat someone like an alien, push them away.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "alleviate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-LEE-vee-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Lessen, make easier to endure", "sent": "The stimulus package has <b>alleviated</b> the pangs of the Great Recession, but times are still tough.", "related": "Assuage (alleviate, satisfy, or pacify—“assuage someone’s fears”), Ameliorate (make better), Extenuate (to make seem less serious—“His crime was extenuated by his mental illness”), Palliate (to alleviate or extenuate)", "moreinfo": "The over-the-counter painkiller “Alleve” was undoubtedly named with the word alleviate in mind.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "a-LEVIate: lift the weight off, ease the pain (like Advil relieves).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ambiguous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "am-BIG-yoo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Not clear, hard to understand, open to having several meanings or interpretations", "sent": "The meaning of this ancient text is <b>ambiguous</b>—either we are missing some cultural context, or else the writer actually wanted to be mysterious.", "related": "Equivocal (allowing for several different meanings; undecided or having mixed feelings), Opaque (hard to understand; dull or stupid; literally, not allowing light to pass through)", "moreinfo": "The prefix “ambi-“ means “both” or “all.” Don’t confuse ambiguous with ambivalent, which means “having mixed feelings, undecided.” People are ambivalent; writing, speech, and other forms of communication are ambiguous.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "ambi (both) + guous: it could go both ways, unclear.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ambivalent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "am-BIV-uh-lent", "also": "ambivalence (noun)", "d1": "Uncertain; unable to decide, or wanting to do two contradictory things at once", "sent": "I’ve been accepted to two amazing graduate programs, one inexpensive and close to home, and one in a big, exciting city. I’m <b>ambivalent</b>—I don’t know how I’m going to decide.\nWhen I asked you if you thought we’d get married some day, your ambivalence hurt my feelings.", "related": "Equivocal (allowing for several different meanings; undecided or having mixed feelings), Vacillate (waver in decision or opinion, be indecisive)", "moreinfo": "The prefix “ambi-“ means “both” or “all” and also occurs in ambiance and ambidextrous. Don’t confuse ambivalent with ambiguous, which means “vague, open to multiple meanings.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "ambi (both) + valent (feelings): pulled two contradictory ways at once.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "analogous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "uh-NAL-uh-guss", "also": "", "d1": "Comparable, corresponding in some particular way (making a good analogy)", "sent": "In the U.S., whenever opponents of a war want to suggest that the war is unwinnable, they point out all the ways in which the war is <b>analogous</b> to the Vietnam War.", "related": "Tantamount (equivalent, as in “What he did is tantamount to murder.”)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 1", "setNum": 1, "hook": "it makes a good analogy: parallel, comparable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "anarchy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "ANN-ark-ee", "also": "anarchism (noun), anarchist (noun)", "d1": "Absence of law or government; chaos, disorder", "sent": "Once the dictator was assassinated, the country fell into total <b>anarchy</b>, as none of the opposition groups were strong enough to seize power.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Anarchism can sometimes mean the political philosophy that the absence of all direct government is ideal and that voluntary associations of individuals would be able to govern themselves.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "an (without) + archy (ruler): no ruler, pure chaos.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "anoint", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-NOINT", "also": "anointed (adj)", "d1": "Rub or sprinkle oil on; make sacred, such as by a ceremony that includes applying oil to someone", "sent": "After Principal Smitters raised test scores over 60% at her school, it was only a matter of time before she was <b>anointed</b> superintendent by a fawning school board.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Anoint shares a root with ointment, an oily substance added to the skin. Anointing occurs repeatedly in the Bible; in that time, people rubbed oil on themselves medicinally and for refreshment, and as a means of showing hospitality to guests.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "a-N-OINTment: rub oil or ointment on to bless and make sacred.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "anomaly", "pos": "noun", "pron": "uh-NOM-ah-lee", "also": "anomalous (adj)", "d1": "Deviation from what is common; inconsistency", "sent": "While the cosmetics division of this company has many female executives, it is an <b>anomaly</b>— in the rest of the company, sadly, only 4% of management positions are filled by women.", "related": "Aberration (departing from normal; mental irregularity or deviation in morality)", "moreinfo": "The word “anomaly” sometimes pops up in discussions of the supernatural. The show X- Files was about the investigation of anomalies.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "\"a-NOM-aly\" = not normal, the odd one out of the pattern.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "antagonize", "pos": "verb", "pron": "an-TAG-uh-nize", "also": "antagonistic (adj)", "d1": "Make hostile or unfriendly", "sent": "“Josie! Stop <b>antagonizing</b> your little brother! Give him back that firetruck and tell him you’re sorry for pulling his hair!”", "related": "Provoke (anger, enrage, stir up, incite to action)", "moreinfo": "When discussing literature or films, the protagonist is the main character and the antagonist is the main adversary (The Joker is Batman’s antagonist).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "the antagonist in a movie provokes and needles the hero.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "antipathy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "an-TIP-uh-thee", "also": "", "d1": "Deep dislike, aversion, or repugnance, sometimes without reason", "sent": "As an environmentalist, Mr. Subramanian had nothing but <b>antipathy</b> for the mining company drilling in and polluting his hometown.", "related": "Loathing (strong aversion, dislike, or disgust)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse antipathy with apathy, which means “indifference, not caring.” Antipathy is the opposite of sympathy (note that the root “path” means “feeling”).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "anti (against) + pathy (feeling): a feeling set against, deep dislike.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "apathy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "APP-uh-thee", "also": "apathetic (adj)", "d1": "Not caring; absence of feeling; lack of interest or concern", "sent": "Many parents of teenagers are concerned by their indolent teens’ <b>apathy</b> about the future. Few teens are totally apathetic, however—many get quite excited about video games, flirting, or trips to the mall.", "related": "Indifferent (apathetic or impartial), Lukewarm (moderately warm; having little enthusiasm)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse apathy with antipathy, which means “deep dislike, aversion, or repugnance.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "a (without) + pathy (feeling): no feeling, you just do not care.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "apocryphal", "pos": "adj", "pron": "uh-POCK-riff-ull", "also": "Apocrypha (noun)", "d1": "Of questionable authenticity; false", "sent": "I’m sorry, but this putative letter from George Washington that you found at a garage sale is clearly <b>apocryphal</b>—it is riddled with anachronisms (for instance, Washington was long dead by the time silent films were invented), and also, Washington most certainly didn’t refer to Martha Washington as “hey baby.”", "related": "Ersatz (artificial, synthetic, serving as a substitute), Faux (fake, imitation, as in “faux fur”), Specious (pleasing to the eye but deceptive)", "moreinfo": "The word “Apocrypha” often refers to books that have been rejected for inclusion in (various versions of) the Bible, either due to dubious authenticity or because the Church considered them useful, but not divinely inspired. Obviously, different authorities disagree about what exactly is included in the Apocrypha.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "like the Apocrypha, disputed writings: of doubtful authenticity, probably false.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "appease", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-PEEZ", "also": "", "d1": "Pacify, satisfy, relieve; concede to belligerent demands, sometimes at the expense of principles", "sent": "My mother is so angry she wasn’t the first person we called when the baby was born—I’m hoping to <b>appease</b> her by spending Christmas at her house this year.", "related": "Placate, Mollify, and Assuage are near-synonyms", "moreinfo": "“Appeasement” is commonly associated with British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain’s policy towards Germany between 1937–1939—as it turned out, giving the Sudetenland to Hitler didn’t actually create “Peace in Our Time.” Today, appeasement is associated with cowardice and giving in to bullies.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "a-PEACE: bring peace and calm them, often by giving in.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "appreciable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "uh-PREESH-yuh-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Enough to be perceived, considerable", "sent": "She ruefully concluded that the “Pot Pie Diet” was a scam and had made no <b>appreciable</b> difference in her appearance—after four weeks, she had lost three-quarters of a pound.", "related": "A near-antonym is Negligible—both words can refer to small amounts, but an appreciable amount or difference is large enough to matter, and a negligible amount or difference is so small it can safely be ignored.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "enough that you can appreciate/notice it: considerable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "arbitrary", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AR-bi-trayr-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Based entirely on one’s discretion; capricious, unreasonable, or having no basis", "sent": "The judge’s rulings were truly <b>arbitrary</b>—one defendant got community service for stealing a television, and another got three years for the same crime.\nIt doesn’t really matter which brand of baking soda you buy—just arbitrarily pick one so we can get out of this grocery store before dinnertime.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Arbitrary looks a lot like arbiter for good reason—originally, something arbitrary was something that was uncertain because it depended on an arbiter’s decision. As indicated by the definition above, arbitrary can be neutral or negative.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "an arbiter deciding on a whim, with no real reason.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "arcane", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ar-CANE", "also": "arcanum (noun)", "d1": "Known or understood by only a few; obscure, secret", "sent": "To win at Jeopardy, you must be full of <b>arcane</b> knowledge.\nThe wizard’s shop was full of <b>arcanum</b>, from Latin-to-Ancient Greek dictionaries to entire books on magic spells relating only to elephants.", "related": "Esoteric, Abstruse", "moreinfo": "Arcane comes from the Latin “arca,” for “box”—arcanum is information that is metaphorically shut up in a box.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "arcane \"arcana\": secret wizardly knowledge only a few understand.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "archaic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ar-CAY-ick", "also": "archaism (noun)", "d1": "Characteristic of an earlier period, ancient, primitive", "sent": "The school’s <b>archaic</b> computer system predated even floppy disks—it stored records on tape drives!\nSometimes, when you look a word up in the dictionary, certain definitions are marked “<b>archaic</b>”—unless you are a Shakespeare scholar, you can safely ignore those archaisms.", "related": "Obsolete (out of date, no longer used), Antediluvian (extremely old, before the Biblical flood)", "moreinfo": "“Archaic” is especially used to describe language common to an older time but rare today, such as “thou” or “forsooth.” If a word appears in a dictionary with “archaic” next to a secondary meaning, that meaning is unlikely to be tested on the GRE.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "arch- (old, as in archaeology): ancient, outdated.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "arduous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AR-dyoo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Very difficult, strenuous; severe, hard to endure", "sent": "The <b>arduous</b> hike up rocky terrain was all worth it once the hikers reached the summit.\nIt was an <b>arduous</b> winter on the prairie; the family barely survived.", "related": "Grueling (very difficult and exhausting)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse arduous with ardor, which means passion.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "sounds like \"hard ordeal\": strenuous and exhausting.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "articulate", "pos": "adj, verb, verb", "pron": "ar-TICK-yoo-lit (adj), ar-TICK-yoo-layt (v)", "also": "", "d1": "Using language in a clear, fluent way (adj); speak distinctly or give clarity to an idea (verb)", "sent": "She’s so <b>articulate</b> that I’m sure she’ll make a good lawyer.\nThe group voted on who would be the best spokesperson, able to <b>articulate</b> their needs to the panel.", "related": "Eloquent (speaking in a fluent, powerful, appropriate way)", "moreinfo": "As an adjective, the last syllable is pronounced “lit.” As a verb, it is pronounced “late.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "hits every article of speech clearly: well-spoken and precise.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "artifact", "pos": "noun", "pron": "AR-ti-fact", "also": "", "d1": "Any object made by humans, especially those from an earlier time, such as those excavated by archaeologists", "sent": "The archaeologists dug up countless <b>artifacts</b>, from simple pottery shards and coins to complex written tablets.\nThe girl’s room was full of the <b>artifacts</b> of modern teenage life: Justin Bieber posters, Twilight books, and a laptop open to Facebook.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Artifact contains the same root (“fact,” meaning “to make”) as in factory and manufacture.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "art + fact: a made object, a relic dug up from the past.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "artless", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ART-less", "also": "", "d1": "Free of deceit or craftiness, natural, genuine; lacking skill or knowledge, crude, uncultured", "sent": "Children can be so <b>artless</b> that, when you try to explain war to them, they say things like, “But isn’t that mean?”/ His <b>artless</b> attempt at negotiating a raise began with “I need more money, please” and ended with “Okay, sorry I asked.”", "related": "Guileless, Ingenuous (synonyms)", "moreinfo": "Don’t think of artless as a lack of art—think of it as a lack of artifice, or artificiality. Artless can be either positive (free of deceit) or negative (lacking skill).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "art-less: no artifice, natural and genuine (or lacking skill).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ascertain", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ass-er-TAYN", "also": "", "d1": "Find out with certainty", "sent": "Hopefully, the investigation will allow us to <b>ascertain</b> who is at fault here.", "related": "Discern (perceive or recognize; tell two or more things apart), Descry (discover, see by looking carefully)", "moreinfo": "Ascertain is easy to remember—it means “make certain” and has the word “certain” contained inside it.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "a-CERTAIN: make it certain, find out for sure.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ascetic", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "uh-SET-ick", "also": "asceticism (noun)", "d1": "abstinent or austere in lifestyle (adj); a person who leads an austere and simple life without material pleasures, esp. someone who does this for religious reasons", "sent": "<b>Ascetics</b> such as monks actually take vows of poverty.\nThe graduate student lived an <b>ascetic</b> existence, her apartment containing only a futon couch and a single bowl and set of chopsticks, which she used to eat ramen noodles every night.", "related": "Hermit or Anchorite (person who lives away from society, esp. for religious reasons), Recluse (person who lives in solitude)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse ascetic with aesthetic, which means “pertaining to beauty or good taste.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "an \"a-SET-ic\" monk denies himself all pleasure, living bare and austere.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "assuage", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-SWAY-zh", "also": "", "d1": "Make milder, relieve; soothe, pacify, or calm", "sent": "After losing a million-dollar account, he tried to <b>assuage</b> his furious boss by pointing out that he was close to winning a new account worth at least as much.", "related": "Placate, Mollify, and Appease are near-synonyms", "moreinfo": "Assuage shares a Latin root (meaning “sweet”) with suave, which today means smoothly agreeable or polite.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "a-SWAGE, like \"a soothe\": a massage eases and calms the pain.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "audacious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "aw-DAY-shuss", "also": "", "d1": "Very bold or brave, often in a rude or reckless way; extremely original", "sent": "He <b>audaciously</b> asked for a raise after working at the company for less than two months!", "related": "Insolent (bold in a rude way), Brazen (shameless, contemptuously bold)", "moreinfo": "Barack Obama wrote a book called The Audacity of Hope. The title suggests that Americans should be bold enough to have hope even in bad circumstances. Audacious can be good (audacious explorers) or bad, as in the employee in the sentence above.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "pure audacity: so bold it borders on rude or reckless.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "augment", "pos": "verb", "pron": "awg-MENT", "also": "", "d1": "Make larger", "sent": "If you memorize the definitions on all of these flashcards, you will have notably <b>augmented</b> your vocabulary!", "related": "Aggrandize (make greater; exaggerate)", "moreinfo": "Augment shares a root (“augere,” to increase) with august, meaning “majestic, inspiring reverence.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "augment reality adds more onto it: enlarge, increase.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "austere", "pos": "adj", "pron": "aw-STEER", "also": "austerity (noun)", "d1": "Severe in manner or appearance; very self-disciplined, ascetic; without luxury or ease; sober or serious", "sent": "Her design sense was so minimalist as to be <b>austere</b>; all-white walls, hard, wooden furniture, not a single picture, throw pillow, or cozy comfort anywhere.\nThe graduation speaker delivered an <b>austere</b> message: the economy is bad, and academic success alone isn’t enough to succeed in the job market.", "related": "Ascetic (pertaining to a simple, austere life with no luxuries, such as that of a monk; a person who leads such a life)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "aw-STERN: stern, bare, and disciplined, without any luxury.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "autonomous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "aw-TAH-nuh-muss", "also": "autonomy (noun)", "d1": "Self-governing, independent", "sent": "As leader of an <b>autonomous</b> region, the newly-elected president was received as a peer by some world leaders, although he was not entitled to send a representative to the United Nations.\nIt is normal for young people to desire greater autonomy as they grow up.", "related": "Fiat (dictate or authoritative order, as in “The king rules by fiat.”), Hegemony (domination, authority; influence by one country over others socially, culturally, economically, etc.)", "moreinfo": "An autonomous region, such as Iraqi Kurdistan, is independent, but not considered its own country.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "auto (self) + nomous (rule): it governs and rules itself.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "aver", "pos": "verb", "pron": "uh-VER", "also": "", "d1": "Declare or affirm with confidence", "sent": "“Despite your insistence that ethics are completely situational,” said the philosophy professor, “I <b>aver</b> that the existence of natural rights inevitably leads to certain immutable ethical boundaries.”", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Aver contains the root “ver” (truth), which also appears in verify, veracious, and verisimilitude.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "a-VERify: state something as true, with full confidence.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "avid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AV-id", "also": "avidity (noun)", "d1": "Enthusiastic, dedicated, passionate; excessively desirous", "sent": "An <b>avid</b> cyclist, she was on her bike every weekend, and even bought the same bike that Lance Armstrong last used in the Tour de France.\n<b>Avid</b> of power, the young Senator compromised every principle to gain the support—and money—of large corporations.", "related": "Ardent (very passionate), Zealous (full of fervor or dedicated enthusiasm for a cause, person, etc.)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 2", "setNum": 2, "hook": "an avid fan is \"a-VID,\" vivid with enthusiasm.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "balk", "pos": "verb", "pron": "BALK", "also": "", "d1": "Refuse to proceed or to do something", "sent": "At the company retreat, he reluctantly agreed to participate in the ropes course, but <b>balked</b> at walking over hot coals as a “trust exercise.”", "related": "Demur (show reluctance or object, especially for moral reasons, as in, “His colleagues wanted him to tell the client that their sales would double, but he demurred.”)", "moreinfo": "Balk comes from a word for a beam or ridge—when a horse or mule balks, it stops short and refuses to proceed. Occasionally, balk is used as a noun for an impediment, much like a beam or ridge, or a defeat.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "balk rhymes with block: you stop short and refuse to go.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "base", "pos": "adj", "pron": "BASE", "also": "", "d1": "Morally low, mean, dishonorable; of little or no value; crude and unrefined; counterfeit", "sent": "His philanthropy was underlied by truly <b>base</b> motives—he not only craved the fawning publicity his donations brought, but he was actually funneling drug money through the Children’s Defense Fund!\nThe supposed “gold bricks” were really <b>base</b> metals covered in a very thin layer of real gold.", "related": "Debase (lower or reduce in quality or dignity)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a \"base\" person has sunk to rock bottom, morally low.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "belie", "pos": "verb", "pron": "bih-LIE", "also": "", "d1": "Contradict or misrepresent", "sent": "The actress’s public persona as a perky “girl next door” <b>belied</b> her private penchant for abusing her assistants and demanding that her trailer be filled with ridiculous luxury goods.\nThe data <b>belie</b> the accepted theory—either we’ve made a mistake, or we have an amazing new discovery on our hands!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "This word is extremely common in GRE questions that ask you to select the missing word for a blank, as belie allows a sentence to “change directions,” as in the example above.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "be-LIE: it tells a lie about the truth, gives a false impression.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "benign", "pos": "adj", "pron": "bih-NINE", "also": "benignant (adj)", "d1": "Harmless; favorable; kindly, gentle, or beneficial; not cancerous", "sent": "He was relieved when the biopsy results came back, informing him that the growth was <b>benign</b>.\nHe’s a <b>benign</b> fellow. I’m sure having him assigned to your team at work will be perfectly pleasant, without changing the way you do things.", "related": "Innocuous (harmless, inoffensive)", "moreinfo": "Benign contains the root “bene,” meaning “good.” Its antonym is malign (“mal” means “bad”), which as an adjective means “evil, harmful, or malicious” (as a verb, it can also mean “slander or defame”). The variant benignant is used the same way as benign.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "bene (good): a benign tumor is the \"good,\" harmless kind.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "bogus", "pos": "adj", "pron": "BOH-guss", "also": "", "d1": "Fake, fraudulent", "sent": "The back of this bodybuilding magazine is just full of ads for <b>bogus</b> products—this one promises 22-inch biceps just from wearing magnetic armbands!", "related": "Spurious and Sham are synonyms", "moreinfo": "Bogus is of American origin, originally a device for making counterfeit money. “Bogus!” was also a slang term of the ‘80s (prominent in the 1989 film Bill and Ted’s Excellent Adventure), meaning “bad” or “unfair.” On the GRE, however, bogus means fake.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "bogus, plain slang for fake and fraudulent.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "bolster", "pos": "verb", "pron": "BOHL-ster", "also": "", "d1": "Strengthen or support", "sent": "The general requested reinforcements to <b>bolster</b> the defensive line set up at the border.\nMany people use alcohol to <b>bolster</b> their confidence before approaching an attractive person in a bar.", "related": "Buttress means a support against a building but can also be a verb meaning “strengthen or support.” Fortify is most associated with protecting against military attack but also means “strengthen or support.”", "moreinfo": "Bolster comes from an Old Norse noun for a long, supportive pillow, and can still mean this. Bolster has additional meanings in sailing, metalworking, and other fields, but in every case a bolster is some kind of specialized support.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a bolster is a firm pillow that props you up and supports you.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "boor", "pos": "noun", "pron": "BOO-er", "also": "boorish (adj)", "d1": "Rude, ill-mannered, or insensitive person; a peasant or country bumpkin", "sent": "Milton was such a <b>boor</b> that, when Jane brought him home to meet her parents, he laughed at their garden gnome and made fun of everyone’s hairstyles in old family photos. “Don’t be so <b>boorish</b>!” said his mortified girlfriend.", "related": "Churl is a synonym and can also be used in its adjective form, churlish. Bumpkin, Yokel, and Rustic are all words for an awkward, uncultured, simple person, generally from the country.", "moreinfo": "Boor is from a German word for farmer that also gives us the “Boers” in South Africa.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a boor is a bore with zero manners, crude and rude.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "buffer", "pos": "noun", "pron": "BUFF-er", "also": "", "d1": "Something that shields, protects, absorbs shock, or cushions", "sent": "During the colonial era, England wanted Georgia as a <b>buffer</b> between its original colonies and Spanish Florida.\nA railroad car has a <b>buffer</b> (similar to a bumper on a car) to absorb shock in case of contact with other cars.\nWhen Joel came out to his family, he used his mother as a <b>buffer</b>— he knew she would be supportive, so he allowed her to relay the news to everyone else, and to relay their responses back to him.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "A buffer can also be a device for polishing (cars, fingernails, etc.).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a buffer zone sits between two sides and absorbs the shock.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "bureaucracy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "byur-ROCK-cruh-see", "also": "bureaucratic (adj), bureaucrat (noun)", "d1": "Government characterized by many bureaus and petty administrators or by excessive, seemingly meaningless requirements", "sent": "Some nations have a worse reputation for <b>bureaucracy</b> than others—in order to get a visa, he had to file papers with four different agencies, wait for hours in three different waiting rooms, and, weeks later, follow up with some petty bureaucrat who complained that the original application should’ve been filed in triplicate.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "There is nothing wrong with bureaus (the Federal Bureau of Investigation, for instance), but bureaucracy takes the idea much too far. While, technically, bureaucracy can simply mean a form of government by bureaus, it is virtually always used in the negative sense of excessive “red tape.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a bureau full of clerks, petty rules, and endless red tape.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "burgeon", "pos": "verb", "pron": "BER-juhn", "also": "", "d1": "Grow or flourish rapidly; put forth buds or shoots (of a plant)", "sent": "The dictator was concerned about the people’s <b>burgeoning</b> discontent and redoubled his personal security.\nSpending an hour a day on vocabulary studies will soon cause your lexicon to <b>burgeon</b>.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "From Old French “burjon,” a shoot or bud. Mushroom is used in a similar metaphorical way, meaning “to spread out in all directions,” as a mushroom grows.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "buds burst open and flourish: grow rapidly.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "buttress", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "BUTT-ress", "also": "", "d1": "Support or encourage (verb); a support or prop, esp. projecting from and supporting the wall of a building (noun)", "sent": "A self-defense class really helped to <b>buttress</b> Elaine’s confidence.\nDavid used his Ph.D. as a <b>buttress</b> against criticism. “I have a doctorate,” he would say. “I just don’t think you can understand.”", "related": "Bolster originally was a type of pillow and now is also a verb meaning “support or strengthen.” Fortify is most associated with protecting against military attack but also means “strengthen or support.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a stone buttress props up a cathedral wall; to prop up or support.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "bygone", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "BYE-gahn", "also": "", "d1": "Past, former (adj); that which is in the past (usually plural noun)", "sent": "At the nursing home, the time to reminisce about <b>bygone</b> days was pretty much all the time.\nIt’s tempting to spend our whole high school reunion talking about <b>bygones</b>, but instead, let’s toast to the future!", "related": "Erstwhile (former), Quondam (former, sometime)", "moreinfo": "The expression “Let bygones be bygones” means to agree to let go of old disagreements.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "bygone days: times gone by, in the past.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "cacophony", "pos": "noun", "pron": "cah-CAW-fuh-nee", "also": "cacophonous (adj)", "d1": "Harsh, discordant, or meaningless mixture of sounds", "sent": "The first day of elementary school marching band practice was nothing but <b>cacophony</b>, as students who hadn’t learned to play their instruments at all nevertheless banged on or puffed air into them.", "related": "Din (loud, confused noise), Dissonance (harsh, inharmonious sound), Clamor (noisy uproar, as from a crowd)", "moreinfo": "“Caco/kako/kaki” is the Greek root for “bad,” occurring in such obscure but useful words as cacography (bad spelling or handwriting) and kakistocracy (rule by evil people). “Eu” is the Greek root for “good” and occurs in the antonym to cacophony, euphony.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "caca (poop) + phony: it sounds like crap, a harsh jumble of noise.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "candid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "CAN-did", "also": "candor (noun)", "d1": "Open, sincere, honest", "sent": "Allow me to be <b>candid</b>: you do look rather portly in those pants, and I think you should wear something else.\nYou have been really secretive about where you’ve been going after work; we could use a little more candor in this relationship.", "related": "Frank (direct, straightforward)", "moreinfo": "Candid photographs are photos “from life,” where the subjects are not posing. Candid Camera was a “hidden camera” style prank show that ran for decades.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a candid camera catches the honest, unposed truth.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "canonical", "pos": "adj", "pron": "can-NON-ick-ull", "also": "“the canon” (noun)", "d1": "Authorized, recognized; pertaining to the canon, or body of accepted rules, standards or artistic works", "sent": "School boards often start controversies when replacing <b>canonical</b> books in the curriculum with modern literature; while many people think students should read works more relevant to their lives, others point out that Moby Dick is part of the canon for a reason.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Canon comes from a Greek word similar to “measuring rod” and which also gives us cane.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "part of the \"canon\": the authorized, accepted official body.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "capricious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cap-REE-shuss", "also": "caprice (noun)", "d1": "Acting on impulse, erratic", "sent": "The headmaster’s punishments were <b>capricious</b>—break the rules one day, you get a warning; break them another day, you get expelled.\nWho needs a plan? A date is more fun with a little caprice —let’s just start driving and see what we find!", "related": "Whimsical (fanciful, eccentric), Lark (merry adventure), Mercurial (changing moods), Arbitrary (based on one’s discretion; capricious)", "moreinfo": "Capricious is generally (but not always) meant in a negative way, as acting on impulse is inappropriate in most contexts. Whimsical is a near-synonym, although generally used in a positive way, to describe using one’s imagination.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a goat (Capricorn) leaping rock to rock with no plan: impulsive, erratic.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "cartography", "pos": "noun", "pron": "car-TAH-grah-fee", "also": "", "d1": "Mapmaking", "sent": "The invention of better navigation tools had major effects on <b>cartography</b>—the more ships knew precisely where they were sailing, the better the world could be mapped.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "From the French “carte,” map.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "carto (chart) + graphy (drawing): the drawing of maps.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "castigate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "CASS-tigg-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Criticize severely; punish in order to correct", "sent": "At the grocery store, the mother attracted stares when she <b>castigated</b>—rather than merely admonished—her child for throwing a box of instant oatmeal.", "related": "Excoriate (berate severely; wear the skin off)", "moreinfo": "Castigate shares a root (meaning “pure”) with both “castrate” and the “caste system.” That should make it easy to remember that castigation is harsh indeed!", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a harsh \"CAST-a-gate\" slammed on you: severely scold and punish.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "catalyst", "pos": "noun", "pron": "CAT-uh-list", "also": "", "d1": "Causer of change", "sent": "The young manager was a <b>catalyst</b> at the stodgy old company—once he introduced employee laptops, telecommuting, and mobile workstations, even the most conventional of employees totally changed the way they worked.\nReaching 500 pounds on the scale was a <b>catalyst</b> for Marcus to really change his lifestyle.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "In chemistry, a catalyst causes or accelerates a chemical reaction but is not, itself, affected (the opposite is an inhibitor). Metaphorically, a catalyst is a person or event that causes a reaction.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a chemistry catalyst kicks off and speeds the reaction: a causer of change.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "caustic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "CAW-stick", "also": "", "d1": "Capable of corroding metal or burning the skin; very critical or sarcastic", "sent": "Wait, those chemicals are <b>caustic</b>! You need safety gloves and goggles before performing this experiment, or else you risk not only getting your skin burned off, but also some seriously <b>caustic</b> remarks from our chemistry teacher.", "related": "Acerbic (sour; harsh or severe), Acrid (sharp or biting, pungent), Astringent (caustic, biting, severe; a skin cleaning fluid that clears pores), Excoriate (to rub the skin off of; to criticize very harshly)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "caustic soda burns skin; a caustic remark stings just as bad.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "censure", "pos": "noun, verb, verb", "pron": "SEN-sher", "also": "", "d1": "Strong disapproval or official reprimand (noun); to issue such disapproval or reprimand (verb)", "sent": "The Senator was <b>censured</b> by the Senate for campaign fund improprieties—in fact, he narrowly avoided being expelled from office.", "related": "Don’t confuse censure with censor (to delete objectionable portions of a work).", "moreinfo": "In 1834, Andrew Jackson became the only U.S. President to ever be censured by the Senate. Since 1789, nine Senators have been censured; this act of formal disapproval does not remove them from office.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "like censor + \"sure\": official disapproval and reprimand.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "chauvinism", "pos": "noun", "pron": "SHOW-vin-izm", "also": "chauvinist (noun), chauvinistic (adj)", "d1": "Fanatical patriotism or blind enthusiasm for military glory; undue or biased devotion to any group, cause, etc.", "sent": "He’s such a chauvinist that he denies that any other nation could be better than ours at anything —he insists our wine is better than France’s, our ski slopes are better than Norway’s, and even that we grow more rice than China! Absurd.", "related": "Bigot (obstinately prejudiced person), Xenophobia (fear of foreigners), Jingoism (extreme chauvinism plus warlike foreign policy)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse chauvinism with sexism—a “male chauvinist” is just one kind. The original chauvinist was Nicholas Chauvin, a possibly fictional soldier wounded 17 times while serving in Napoleon’s army (he really loved Napoleon).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "named for soldier Chauvin, blindly and fanatically loyal to his group.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "chronological", "pos": "adj", "pron": "KRAH-noh-LODGE-ick-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Arranged in or relating to time order", "sent": "Joey, I’m afraid you’ve done the assignment wrong—the point of making a timeline is to put the information in <b>chronological</b> order. You’ve made an alphabetical-order-line instead!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The root “chron,” for “time,” also occurs in chronicle (a historical account in time order), chronic (ongoing, usually of an illness), and anachronism (thing out of its own time period). Chronometer is just a fancy word for clock.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "chrono (time): arranged in time order.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "clamor", "pos": "verb", "pron": "CLAM-er", "also": "", "d1": "Noisy uproar or protest, as from a crowd; a loud, continuous noise", "sent": "As soon as a scent of scandal emerged, the press was <b>clamoring</b> for details.\nThe mayor couldn’t even make herself heard over the <b>clamor</b> of the protestors.", "related": "Hubbub (loud noise, confusion), Cacophany (harsh, discordant, or meaningless mixture of sounds), Din (loud, confused noise), Dissonance (cacophony, harsh, inharmonious sound)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse with clamber, which means to climb awkwardly or scramble. Clamor comes from an Anglo-French root meaning “cry out,” a root that also gives us claim.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "a crowd clamps together and yells: a noisy uproar or protest.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "clinch", "pos": "verb", "pron": "KLIN-ch", "also": "", "d1": "Make final or settle conclusively; to fasten or hold together", "sent": "When their best player was benched, the team fell behind, but once he was allowed back in the game, the team was able to <b>clinch</b> the win.\nThese two pieces have been <b>clinched</b> together with a clamp while the glue dries.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "In sports, to clinch a championship is to gain so great a lead that it is impossible not to win. To clinch a contract is to lock it down. To clinch a nail is to flatten the part that sticks out—that is, to finish your work.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 3", "setNum": 3, "hook": "clinch the deal: lock it shut and settle it for good.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "coalesce", "pos": "verb", "pron": "coh-uh-LESS", "also": "", "d1": "Come together, unite; fuse together", "sent": "While at first everyone on the team was jockeying for power and recognition, eventually, the group <b>coalesced</b> and everyone was happy to share credit for a job well-done.\nEast and West Germany <b>coalesced</b> into a single country in 1990.", "related": "Agglomerate (collect into a mass), Aggregate (gather together), Consolidate (unite, combine, firm up—you can consolidate loans or consolidate power)", "moreinfo": "A coalition is a group that has coalesced (at least in theory).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "co (together) + alesce: separate coals merge into one mass.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "cogent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "COH-gent", "also": "", "d1": "Very convincing, logical", "sent": "Studying logic is an excellent way to improve at formulating <b>cogent</b> arguments.\nNurses who work in the Alzheimer’s ward must develop skills for communicating with people who are often not <b>cogent</b>.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Cogent comes from two roots meaning “together” and “drive”—a cogent argument allows you to “drive” your listener to your conclusion. The resemblance of cogent to cognition, cognitive, cogitation, and excogitate—words about thinking—is merely a coincidence, but a helpful one.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "a cogent argument clicks like a cog: tightly logical and convincing.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "commensurate", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cuh-MEN-sher-it", "also": "", "d1": "The same in size, extent, etc., equivalent; proportional", "sent": "According to the course catalog, you may take Advanced Japanese following Japanese III or <b>commensurate</b> experience with the language.", "related": "Tantamount (equivalent, as in “What he did is tantamount to murder.”)", "moreinfo": "The Latin “mensuratus” means “measure”—so commensurate means “measure together.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "co + measure: measured to match, proportional.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "complacent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cuhm-PLAY-sent", "also": "", "d1": "Self-satisfied, smug; overly content (and therefore lazy, neglectful, or some other bad quality)", "sent": "The coach gave a pep talk: “I know we’ve never won a championship before, but we do have an advantage over the six-time state champions we’re about to play—they have grown <b>complacent</b> with their success, and now they just assume they’ll win without having to sweat.”", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse complacent with complaisant, which means “eager to please.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "too com-PLACID and pleased to bother improving: smug.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "complementary", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cahm-pleh-MENT-uh-ree", "also": "", "d1": "Completing; fitting together well; filling mutual needs", "sent": "“That scarf really complements your outfit,” said Elle. “Thanks for the compliment,” said Danica.\nThe couple had <b>complementary</b> personalities—when Mark got overwhelmed with the details, Lee took care of everything, and when Lee got too introspective, Mark cheered him up with an insatiable zest for life.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Complementary angles (for instance, 35° and 55°) add to 90 degrees.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "with an e it comPLEtes the set, filling the missing gap.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "compliant", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cuhm-PLY-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Obeying, submissive; following the requirements", "sent": "Those who are not <b>compliant</b> with the regulations will be put on probation and possibly expelled.\nA <b>compliant</b> child, he never gave his parents any trouble.", "related": "Complaisant and Obliging are words describing people who comply with eagerness.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "you comply: obedient, going along with the requirements.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "concede", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-SEED", "also": "concession (noun)", "d1": "Give in, admit, yield; acknowledge reluctantly; grant or give up (such as giving up land after losing a war)", "sent": "The negotiations were pointless, with each side’s representatives instructed by their home countries to make no concessions whatsoever.\nQuebec was a French concession to Britain in the Treaty of Paris in 1763.\nI suppose I will have to <b>concede</b> the argument now that you’ve looked up evidence on Wikipedia.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The Latin “cedere” means “yield, go, withdraw” and also gives us cede (to yield, especially to give up land after losing a war), precede, and succeed.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "con + cede (yield): yield the point, admit and give in.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "conciliatory", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cuhn-SILL-ee-uh-tore-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Reconciling, appeasing, attempting to make the peace", "sent": "The hotel manager was horrified at how the guest had been treated, and approached him in a <b>conciliatory</b> manner, offering him numerous freebies and apologizing repeatedly.", "related": "Placate, Mollify, Appease, Pacify and Assuage are all verbs for attempting to calm or make peace.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "trying to reconcile: making peace, appeasing.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "concur", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-KER", "also": "", "d1": "Approve, agree", "sent": "John Locke wrote that justice is based on the social contract, and I <b>concur</b>—in fact, my latest book is all about contractual justice.", "related": "Assent (approval, agreement)", "moreinfo": "Concur is related to concurrent, meaning “occurring at the same time or acting over the same area; coordinating actions,” as in “We recommend that you take calculus before college physics, but it is possible to take them concurrently.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "con (together) + cur (run): minds run together, they agree.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "condone", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-DOHN", "also": "", "d1": "Overlook, tolerate, regard as harmless", "sent": "While underage drinking is illegal, at many universities, it is tacitly <b>condoned</b> by administrations that neglect to enforce anti-drinking policies.", "related": "Brook (suffer or tolerate), Countenance (as a noun: face or facial expression; as a verb: approve or tolerate)", "moreinfo": "Condone shares a root (meaning “give”) with donate. To condone is to give mild, sometimes tacit, approval.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "con-DONE: you treat the wrong as \"done and fine,\" letting it slide.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "confer", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-FER", "also": "", "d1": "Consult, compare views; bestow or give", "sent": "A Ph.D. <b>confers</b> upon a person the right to be addressed as “Doctor” as well as eligibility to pursue tenure-track professorship.\nExcuse me for a moment to make a call—I can’t buy this car until I <b>confer</b> with my spouse.", "related": "Vest can mean “grant an authority or right,” as in “The ownership of 51% of the stock vests her with the right to make decisions for the company.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "con (together) + fer (bring): bring heads together, or bestow a title.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "connoisseur", "pos": "noun", "pron": "cah-nuh-SOOR", "also": "", "d1": "Expert, especially in the fine arts; person of educated, refined tastes", "sent": "A chocolate <b>connoisseur</b>, Mom eschews grocery store brands and will only eat 80% -cocoa- or-higher artisanal chocolate that is less than a week old.", "related": "Epicure (person with discriminating tastes, esp. regarding food and wine)", "moreinfo": "Connoisseur comes, through French, from the Latin “cognoscere,” meaning “to know,” which also gives us cognition.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "a wine connoisseur truly \"knows\" quality, a refined expert.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "console", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "cuhn-SOHL (v), CAHN-sohl (n)", "also": "", "d1": "Lessen the suffering or grief of (verb); a control panel, or small table or cabinet (noun)", "sent": "I was unable to <b>console</b> Tina after she fell asleep at the <b>console</b> of her airplane and thereby failed her pilot’s exam.", "related": "Succor (comfort, provide relief)", "moreinfo": "As a verb, the word is pronounced “con-SOLE.” As a noun, it’s pronounced “CON-sole.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "you console a crying friend, easing their grief.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "consolidate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-SAH-lid-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Unite, combine, solidify, make coherent", "sent": "She <b>consolidated</b> her student loans so she would only have to make one payment per month.\nAs group leader, Muriel will <b>consolidate</b> all of our research into a single report.", "related": "Agglomerate (collect into a mass), Aggregate (gather together), Commix (mix together), Conglomerate (anything made up of different kinds of materials; blended; to bring together)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "con + solid: press it all into one solid whole.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "constrict", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-STRICT", "also": "", "d1": "Squeeze, compress; restrict the freedom of", "sent": "The children strongly disliked being gussied up in <b>constrictive</b> clothing for a formal wedding.\nTourism is now allowed in North Korea, but tourists must stay with official tour groups, and their movements are heavily <b>constricted</b>.", "related": "Constringe (cause to shrink)", "moreinfo": "A Boa constrictor is a snake that squeezes its prey to death.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "a boa constrictor squeezes tight, compressing and restricting.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "construe", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-STROO", "also": "", "d1": "Interpret or translate", "sent": "I don’t know how you <b>construed</b> my comment as an insult. All I said was, “Wow, I never knew you could sing.”", "related": "", "moreinfo": "To misconstrue is to misunderstand or misinterpret.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "how do you construe it: how do you read and interpret it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "contentious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cuhn-TENT-chuss", "also": "contend (verb), contention (noun)", "d1": "Controversial; prone to causing arguments, especially gratuitous or petty ones", "sent": "The death penalty is a <b>contentious</b> issue.\nMy uncle is so <b>contentious</b> that every attempt I made to introduce an uncontroversial topic met with failure—he ranted and raved about the weather, trees, math, and my daughter’s enjoyment of oatmeal.", "related": "Disputatious and quarrelsome are near-synonyms", "moreinfo": "A contention is simply a claim, often a thesis or statement that will then be backed up with reasons. To contend can be to argue or to vie for a prize, as in the famous quote from On the Waterfront: “I coulda been a contender.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "full of contention: controversial, quick to spark arguments.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "contextualize", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-TEKS-tchoo-ah-lize", "also": "", "d1": "Place in context, such as by giving the background or circumstances", "sent": "Virginia Woolf’s feminism is hard to truly understand unless <b>contextualized</b> within the mores of the highly restrained, upper-class English society of her time.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Context, from the Latin, can simply be thought of as “the text that comes with.” The root “text” itself is the same in both “textbook” and “textile”—it means “weave,” just as we would weave either cloth or a story.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "give it context: set the background and circumstances around it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "conundrum", "pos": "noun", "pron": "cah-NUHN-drum", "also": "", "d1": "Riddle, the answer to which involves a play on words; any mystery", "sent": "A classic <b>conundrum</b> is “What’s black and white and red all over?” The answer, of course, is a newspaper.\nHow to count the entire population accurately—including those without fixed addresses—is a bit of a <b>conundrum</b>.", "related": "Enigma (puzzle, mystery), Paradox (contradiction, or seeming contradiction that is actually true)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "a conundrum is a real head-scratcher puzzle or riddle.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "converge", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuhn-VERGE", "also": "", "d1": "Move towards one another or towards a point; unite", "sent": "I know we’re driving to the wedding from different states, but our routes ought to <b>converge</b> when each of us hits I-95—maybe we could <b>converge</b> at a Cracker Barrel for lunch!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The antonym of converge is diverge.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "con (together) + verge: the edges come together toward one point.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "conversant", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cuhn-VER-sint", "also": "", "d1": "Knowledgeable about or experienced with", "sent": "For an opera singer, she is unusually <b>conversant</b> in physics—she just explained to everyone the purpose of the Large Hadron Collider.", "related": "Abreast (keeping up with, staying aware of, or remaining equal in progress with)", "moreinfo": "Conversant does NOT mean talkative—it means having enough knowledge to be able to carry on an intelligent conversation about a topic.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "you can converse about it: knowledgeable, well-versed.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "conversely", "pos": "adverb", "pron": "cuhn-VER-slee", "also": "", "d1": "In an opposite way; on the other hand", "sent": "I am not here to argue that lack of education causes poverty. <b>Conversely</b>, I am here to argue that poverty causes lack of education.", "related": "The expression “to the contrary” is sometimes used in the same way as conversely.", "moreinfo": "In logic, the converse of a statement is a simple reversal, from “Bachelors are unmarried men” to “Unmarried men are bachelors.” The converse is not always true, such as in the case of “All schnauzers are dogs” and “All dogs are schnauzers.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "converse: turned the opposite way around, on the other hand.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "convoluted", "pos": "adj", "pron": "CAHN-vuh-loo-tid", "also": "", "d1": "Twisted; very complicated", "sent": "Your argument is so <b>convoluted</b> that I’m not even able to understand it enough to start critiquing it.\nTo get from the hotel room to the pool requires following a <b>convoluted</b> path up two staircases and down two others—to get to someplace on the same floor we started on!", "related": "Tortuous (twisting, not direct, deceitful), Labyrinthine (tortuous, resembling a labyrinth), Byzantine (complex or intricate; relating to the Byzantine Empire and its highly intricate art and architecture style)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "con + voluted (rolled): rolled up in knots, tangled and complex.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "copious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "COH-pee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Plentiful, bountiful", "sent": "Although she took <b>copious</b> notes in class, she found that she was missing a big picture that would have tied all the information together.\nThe fertile, <b>copious</b> land yielded a rich harvest.", "related": "Profuse (giving or done in a free, abundant way)", "moreinfo": "Copious shares a root with cornucopia, a “horn of plenty” (the symbol of the American Thanksgiving holiday).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "copious \"copies\" galore: plentiful, in abundance.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "corroborate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "cuh-ROBB-er-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Support, add evidence to", "sent": "You’re telling me you were thirty miles away riding a roller coaster when the school was vandalized? I have a hard time believing that—is there anyone who can <b>corroborate</b> your story?", "related": "Substantiate and verify also mean to prove true or provide evidence in favor of.", "moreinfo": "Corroborate shares a Latin root (“robur,” for oak or strength) with robust.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 4", "setNum": 4, "hook": "co + robur (strength): back it up, add strengthening evidence.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "cosmopolitan", "pos": "adj", "pron": "cahz-moh-PAH-lit-uhn", "also": "cosmopolite (noun)", "d1": "Belonging to the entire world, at home globally; free from local or national prejudices or attachments", "sent": "Trapped in a small town, he dreamed of a more <b>cosmopolitan</b> existence filled with world travel, exotic cuisine, and scintillating parties where he would meet famous authors and other cosmopolites.", "related": "Urbane (elegant, sophisticated, suave; suitable to social life in major cities)", "moreinfo": "The magazine Cosmopolitan was originally named to evoke the image of a wordly, sophisticated woman. A “cosmopolitan” is also a cocktail popularized by the show Sex and the City. Most New Yorkers do not think that drinking “cosmos” is cosmopolitan.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "cosmo (world) + politan (city): a citizen of the whole world, at home anywhere.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "countenance", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "COUNT-eh-nenss", "also": "", "d1": "Facial expression or face (noun); approve or tolerate (verb)", "sent": "Her <b>countenance</b> said it all—the look on her face was pure terror.\nI saw you cheating off my paper, and I can’t <b>countenance</b> cheating—either you turn yourself in or I’ll report you.", "related": "Brook (suffer or tolerate), Condone (overlook or tacitly approve)", "moreinfo": "Countenance shares a root with continence, meaning “self control.” The use of countenance to mean “approve or tolerate” makes sense when you think about a similar expression: “I cannot look you in the face after what you did.” (We would usually say “I cannot face you” when the speaker is the guilty party).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "keep your countenance (face) calm; or to countenance is to \"count as okay,\" to allow.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "counterintuitive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "count-er-in-TOO-it-iv", "also": "", "d1": "Against what one would intuitively expect", "sent": "Although it seems <b>counterintuitive</b>, for some extreme dieters, eating more can actually help them to lose weight, since the body is reassured that it is not facing a period of prolonged starvation.", "related": "Paradoxical (contradictory, or seemingly contradictory but actually true)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "counter to your intuition: the opposite of what you would guess.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "counterpoint", "pos": "noun", "pron": "COUNT-er-point", "also": "", "d1": "Contrasting item, opposite; a complement; the use of contrast or interplay in a work of art", "sent": "The play’s lighthearted, witty narrator provides a welcome <b>counterpoint</b> to the seriousness and grief expressed by the other characters.\nThe hot peppers work in <b>counterpoint</b> to an otherwise sweet dish.", "related": "Foil (a character in a drama, or any entity, that contrasts with another, as in “Fortinbras is a foil to Hamlet in that each wishes to avenge his father’s death, but Fortinbras acts decisively, which only serves to point out Hamlet’s weakness.”)", "moreinfo": "The humor publication The Onion is known for its amusing “Point/Counterpoint” articles in which two speakers disagree on a topic.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "a counter-point: the contrasting, opposite item.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "counterproductive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "count-er-proh-DUCK-tiv", "also": "", "d1": "Defeating the purpose; preventing the intended goal", "sent": "The candidate’s attempt to win swing votes in Ohio was actually <b>counterproductive</b> —following his speech in Toledo, his poll numbers actually went down 5%.", "related": "Feckless (ineffectual), Bootless (unsuccessful), Inimical (unfavorable, harmful, as in “Drinking unclean water is inimical to health.”)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "counter to being productive: it backfires and defeats the purpose.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "covert", "pos": "adj", "pron": "COH-vert", "also": "", "d1": "Secret, veiled, undercover", "sent": "The soldier trained to be part of <b>covert</b> operations, moving silently and remaining out of the enemy’s sight.", "related": "Clandestine, Surreptitious, and Furtive all mean secret, undercover.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "a covert op is \"covered,\" hidden and secret.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "crafty", "pos": "adj", "pron": "CRAFF-tee", "also": "", "d1": "Cunning, skillful in deception or underhanded schemes", "sent": "A <b>crafty</b> play in basketball is the “head-fake”—moving the head in one direction slightly prior to running in the other direction, to try to get a tiny head start on a disoriented pursuer.", "related": "Cunning, Guileful, and Wily are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "Crafty is connected to “arts and crafts” in the sense of being skilled. Of course, if you somehow used yarn to trick people, you’d be doubly crafty.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "crafty as a fox: cunning and skilled at deception.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "craven", "pos": "adj", "pron": "CRAY-ven", "also": "", "d1": "Very cowardly, lacking courage", "sent": "The nervous soldier feared he would turn <b>craven</b> in his first firefight, but he actually acted quite bravely.", "related": "Timorous (timid, fearful), Pusillanimous (cowardly), Dastardly (cowardly in a treacherous, sneaky way)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "a craven coward \"craves\" safety and runs.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "credibility", "pos": "noun", "pron": "credd-ih-BILL-it-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Believability, trustworthiness", "sent": "Many famous “experts” with “Dr.” before their names are not medical doctors at all. Any television “doctor” who turns out to have a Ph.D. in botany, for instance, ought to suffer a serious drop in <b>credibility</b>.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The root “cred,” meaning “believe” also gives us credit, creed, credo, incredulous, discredit, and accreditation. Don’t confuse credible with credulous, meaning gullible. Finally, while we often use incredible to mean “great,” it really means “not believable.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "cred (believe): how much belief and trust you have earned.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "credulous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "CREDD-joo-luss", "also": "", "d1": "Gullible; prone to believing or trusting too easily or without enough evidence", "sent": "“Did you know you can wash your ears by turning your head on its side in the shower and letting the water run straight through your head?” said the father to his five-year-old daughter. “I’ll try that!” said the <b>credulous</b> little girl.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The opposite of credulous is incredulous, which means skeptical. The root “cred,” meaning “believe” also gives us credit, creed, credo, discredit, and accreditation.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "cred (believe) + ulous: believes too easily, gullible.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "crescendo", "pos": "noun", "pron": "creh-SHEN-doh", "also": "", "d1": "Steady increase in force, intensity, or the loudness of a musical passage; a climactic moment or peak", "sent": "Mrs. Higgins did love the symphony, but she was also coming down with a migraine—by the time the music reached its towering <b>crescendo</b>, her head was pounding.\nThe administration tried to ignore the protest, but finally had to address the issue when the demonstration reached a <b>crescendo</b>.", "related": "Ascension (rising to the top), Culminate (reach a high point or final stage), Surge (strong forward movement, swelling wave)", "moreinfo": "Crescendo is originally a musical term; its antonym is decrescendo, although that word is less often used metaphorically.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "music crescendos, swelling louder toward a peak.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "culminate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "CULL-minn-ayt", "also": "culmination (noun)", "d1": "Reach the highest point or final stage", "sent": "A Ph.D. program generally <b>culminates</b> in a written dissertation and its defense to a committee.", "related": "Acme, Summit, Pinnacle, and Apex are all words for a high point or peak. Denouement means conclusion or ending, such as of a literary work, esp. one that “wraps up all the loose ends.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "culmen (summit): reach the summit, the final peak.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "cynical", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SIN-ick-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Thinking the worst of others’ motivations; bitterly pessimistic", "sent": "Shilpa was so <b>cynical</b> that even when her friends threw her a surprise party, she figured that they only did it so they wouldn’t feel so guilty about all the mean things they must be saying behind her back.", "related": "Misanthrope (hater of humankind)", "moreinfo": "The original Cynics were ancient Greek philosophers who held that self-control was the only virtue. They are rumored to have been quite contemptuous towards their philosophical opponents.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "a cynic sneers, expecting the worst of everyone's motives.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "daunt", "pos": "verb", "pron": "DAWNT", "also": "undaunted, dauntless (adj)", "d1": "Discourage, dishearten, lessen the courage of", "sent": "Amazingly undaunted after his accident, Devon vowed to complete a marathon in his wheelchair. Not even a dented rim on mile 19 could <b>daunt</b> him—he <b>dauntlessly</b> completed the race anyway.", "related": "Cow (intimidate, destroy the courage of)", "moreinfo": "Daunt shares a root with domesticate, “to tame.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "\"nothing daunting\" means nothing scary; to daunt is to intimidate and discourage.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "debase", "pos": "verb", "pron": "deh-BASE", "also": "debased (adj)", "d1": "Degrade; lower in quality, value, rank, etc.; lower in moral quality", "sent": "You have <b>debased</b> yourself by accepting bribes.\nMembers of the mainstream church argued that the fringe sect was practicing a <b>debased</b> version of the religion, twisting around its precepts and missing the point.\nI can tell from the weight that this isn’t pure gold, but rather some <b>debased</b> mixed metal.", "related": "Base (morally low, of little value, crude or fake), Adulterate (make impure), Debauch (corrupt morally), Vitiate (ruin, make faulty, corrupt)", "moreinfo": "To “debase a currency” is to lower its value, such as by a government wishing to print more money for its own ends, thereby lowering the value of the money held by citizens.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "de (down) + base: drag it down to a low, base level.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "debunk", "pos": "verb", "pron": "deh-BUNK", "also": "", "d1": "Expose, ridicule, or disprove false or exaggerated claims", "sent": "Galileo spent his last years under house arrest for <b>debunking</b> the widely held idea that the Sun revolved around the Earth.\nThe show MythBusters <b>debunks</b> pseudoscientific claims.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Debunk is an Americanism, deriving from bunkum, an old-fashioned word for nonsense or meaningless talk.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "de-BUNK: take the \"bunk\" (nonsense) out, expose it as false.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "decorous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "DECK-er-uss", "also": "decorum (noun)", "d1": "Behaving with propriety and good taste; polite", "sent": "Miss Etiquette writes an advice column about decorum. One writer asked, “What’s the most <b>decorous</b> way to tell guests exactly what I want for my wedding?” Miss Etiquette replied, “Young lady, there is no <b>decorous</b> way to solicit gifts, and even asking that question is entirely indecorous of you.”", "related": "Genteel (aristocratic, elegant), Seemly (proper or attractive)", "moreinfo": "Decorous shares a root (meaning “beauty, grace”) with decorate and decor (interior furnishings).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "decor + ous: proper and tasteful, fitting the decorum.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "deem", "pos": "verb", "pron": "DEEM", "also": "", "d1": "Judge; consider", "sent": "“You can take the black belt exam when I <b>deem</b> you ready, and not a moment before,” said the karate instructor.", "related": "Assay (examine, analyze, judge)", "moreinfo": "Deem, which shares an Old English root with doom, originally meant “to pronounce judgment”—in the 17th century, some judges were called “deemsters.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "deem it worthy: judge or consider it so.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "deface", "pos": "verb", "pron": "deh-FACE", "also": "", "d1": "Vandalize, mar the appearance of", "sent": "Ernest was charged with three counts of vandalism after being caught <b>defacing</b> a row of townhouses with spraypaint.", "related": "Depredate (plunder, lay waste to), Efface (wipe out, erase)", "moreinfo": "To deface can also be “to mark a check or monetary instrument” in order to make it invalid (“She defaced the check so it couldn’t be cashed.”)", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "de + face: ruin the face or surface of it, vandalize.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "defamatory", "pos": "adj", "pron": "deh-FAM-uh-tore-ee", "also": "defame (verb)", "d1": "Slanderous, injurious to someone’s reputation", "sent": "One interesting issue in free speech law is defamation.\nThe political blogs are filled with <b>defamatory</b> language; it seems anyone with a computer and an opinion can destroy a politician’s reputation these days.", "related": "Slander and Traduce also mean “to speak maliciously and falsely of.” Aspersions are damaging remarks or defamatory speech. Memory Trick: Someone who made up vicious lies about Lady Gaga’s hit album would have defamed “The Fame.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "de + fame: it destroys your good name and fame, slanderous.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "default", "pos": "noun, verb, verb", "pron": "DEE-fault (n), diff-FAULT (v)", "also": "", "d1": "Failure to act, neglect (noun); fail to fulfill an obligation, especially a financial one (verb)", "sent": "The government is cracking down on for-profit colleges where a large percentage of the graduates cannot use their degrees to gain employment and end up <b>defaulting</b> on their student loans.\nYou must elect a new health plan by December 31st or by <b>default</b> you will be re-enrolled in the plan you selected last year.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "In law, a “judgment by default” is when someone loses a case for failing to show up in court.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "de-FAULT: your fault for failing to act or pay; or the fall-back setting.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "deference", "pos": "noun", "pron": "DEFF-er-enss", "also": "defer (verb)", "d1": "Respectful submission; yielding to the authority or opinion of another", "sent": "In many cultures, young people are expected to show <b>deference</b> to older people at all times.\nI’m not an expert in databases—I’ll defer to our programmers on that decision.\nIngrid deferred her college admissions for a year so she could travel the world.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "you defer to elders: yield out of respect.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "deflect", "pos": "verb", "pron": "diff-FLECT", "also": "", "d1": "Cause to curve; turn aside, esp. from a straight course; avoid", "sent": "The purpose of a shield is to <b>deflect</b> arrows or bullets from an enemy./ Every time he was asked a difficult question, Senator Warrington <b>deflected</b> by changing the topic, saying he’d answer later, or even—insincerely, it seemed—calling for a moment of prayer.", "related": "Avert (prevent; turn away or aside, as in “The makeshift dam narrowly averted disaster” or “Avert your eyes—I’m changing my clothes!”)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "de + flect (bend): bend it away, turn it aside.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "deleterious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "dell-uh-TEER-ee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Harmful, unhealthful", "sent": "The Resident Assistant told the first-year students, “I think you will find not only that drugs are illegal and will result in expulsion, but also that drug abuse will have a <b>deleterious</b> effect on anyone’s grades.”", "related": "Pernicious (very harmful, fatal), Insalutary or Insalubrious (unhealthful)", "moreinfo": "Deleterious shares a root (“destroy”) with delete.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "it \"deletes\" your health: harmful, damaging.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "delineate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "deh-LINN-ee-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Mark the outline of; sketch; describe in detail", "sent": "I do need the cash, but I’m not signing up for this psychological experiment unless you <b>delineate</b> what’s going to happen.", "related": "Adumbrate (give a rough outline of)", "moreinfo": "Delineate, of course, shares a root with linear.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 5", "setNum": 5, "hook": "de + line: draw the lines of it, outline or describe in detail.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "denigrate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "DEN-igg-rayt", "also": "", "d1": "Belittle, attack the reputation of", "sent": "Many jokes in the Meet the Parents trilogy come from Robert De Niro’s character <b>denigrating</b> Ben Stiller’s character for being a male nurse.", "related": "Disparage is a synonym.", "moreinfo": "Denigrate comes from a Latin root meaning “to blacken.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "de + nigr (blacken): blacken someone's name, belittle them.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "denote", "pos": "verb", "pron": "deh-NOTE", "also": "denotation (noun)", "d1": "Be a name or symbol for", "sent": "The company’s brand <b>denotes</b> quality; the marketing team has done a fantastic job of associating the company’s image with fine service.\nThere’s nothing in the <b>denotation</b> of “crotchety” (grumpy, having strong and irrational preferences) that indicates any particular group of people, but due to the expression “crotchety old man,” the word connotes, for many people, an image of an especially unpleasant male senior citizen.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "A denotation is the literal meaning of a word; a connotation is the feeling that accompanies that word. For instance, many special color words, such as “lilac” or “cerulean” have a positive connotation.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "de + note: it notes or marks the meaning, it stands for.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "deride", "pos": "verb", "pron": "deh-RIDE", "also": "", "d1": "Mock, scoff at, laugh at contemptuously", "sent": "The manager really thought that <b>deriding</b> his employees as “stupid” or “lazy” would motivate them to work harder; instead, it motivated them to constantly hide his office supplies as an act of revenge.", "related": "Denigrate (belittle, attack the reputation of)", "moreinfo": "Deride contains the Latin root “ridere” for “laughter,” which also appears in risible, meaning “laughable.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "de + ride: \"ride\" someone hard, mock and scoff at them.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "derivative", "pos": "adj", "pron": "deh-RIV-uh-tiv", "also": "", "d1": "Derived from something else; not original", "sent": "The singer’s first album was a disappointment, <b>derivative</b> of several hit albums from the previous year, as though a management team had simply picked out the elements from other popular songs that they thought would make the most money.", "related": "Banal, Hackneyed, Inane, Insipid and Trite all mean “lacking freshness and originality, shallow.”", "moreinfo": "In finance, a derivative is a contract the value of which is derived from the value of underlying stocks, bonds, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "derived from something else: a copy, not original.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "desiccate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "DESS-ick-ayt", "also": "desiccated (adj)", "d1": "Thoroughly dried up, dehydrated", "sent": "The key to maintaining odor-free shoes is to <b>desiccate</b> the insole by placing a drying agent, such as a small pouch of baking soda, inside the shoe between wears.\nBeef jerky is a <b>desiccated</b> meat product.", "related": "Arid means very dry, like a desert.", "moreinfo": "Also from the Latin “siccare” (dry), a siccative is a drying agent. The same root appears in the Spanish word for dry (seco) and in the name of the Italian dry sparkling wine prosecco.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "desert-dry: bone dry, thoroughly dehydrated.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "detached", "pos": "adj", "pron": "dee-TATCH-t", "also": "", "d1": "Impartial, disinterested; unconcerned, distant, aloof", "sent": "He found her <b>detached</b> demeanor inappropriate for a funeral. It’s fine to politely ask how someone died, but it’s not appropriate to coldly question a relative on the medical history of the deceased.\nThe divorce proceeding was full of anger and recriminations, but the judge was able to make a <b>detached</b> decision.", "related": "Standoffish (cold, unfriendly)", "moreinfo": "A detached house is one that does not have a wall in common with another building.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "detached, unhooked from emotion: impartial, aloof.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "deterrent", "pos": "noun", "pron": "dee-TER-int", "also": "", "d1": "Something that restrains or discourages", "sent": "Some argue that the death penalty is a <b>deterrent</b> to crime—that is, the point is not just to punish the guilty, but to frighten other prospective criminals.", "related": "Balk (an impediment; refuse to proceed or to do something)", "moreinfo": "As a military policy, deterrence is building military strength or ability to retaliate (especially by stockpiling nuclear weapons, as in the Cold War) sufficient to deter enemies from attacking.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "a deterrent deters you, holding you back or discouraging.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "diatribe", "pos": "noun", "pron": "DIE-uh-TRIBE", "also": "", "d1": "Bitter, abusive attack or criticism; rant", "sent": "I’d stay out of the living room for awhile—Grandpa’s on another one of his <b>diatribes</b> about how it’s un-American to call a large coffee a “venti.” You can hear him ranting from here!", "related": "Tirade, Harangue, and Fulmination are all words for bitter, angry speeches or attacks.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "a bitter rant hurled through and at someone, harsh criticism.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "didactic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "die-DACK-tick", "also": "", "d1": "Intended to instruct; teaching, or teaching a moral lesson", "sent": "She might have been Teacher of the Year at work, but at home, her husband wished she would turn off her <b>didactic</b> personality. “Honey,” he said, “I really don’t need you to use everything as a learning opportunity.”\nThe child was disappointed when the storybook turned <b>didactic</b> in the end, with the teddy bears—and the reader—being admonished never to lie.", "related": "Pedagogical (pertaining to teaching), Pedantic (showy about learning, excessively concerned with details, as in “He’s so pedantic he corrects his friends’ grammar.”)", "moreinfo": "Didactic can be positive or negative. Any teacher is didactic, but a person who is lecturing or moralizing in an unwanted manner can also be called didactic.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "\"dida-teach\": intended to instruct or teach a moral lesson.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "digress", "pos": "verb", "pron": "die-GRESS", "also": "digression (noun)", "d1": "Go off-topic when speaking or writing", "sent": "Grandpa <b>digressed</b> quite a bit while you were in the kitchen—he was telling us an old war story, but somehow now he’s ranting about how nobody celebrates Arbor Day anymore. That <b>digression</b> could take awhile.", "related": "Divagate is a synonym. Diffuse as an adjective can mean off-topic (a diffuse speech).", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "di (aside) + gress (step): step aside from the topic.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "din", "pos": "noun", "pron": "DIN", "also": "", "d1": "Loud, confused noise, esp. for a long period of time", "sent": "This hotel was described as “near all the hot spots,” but I didn’t realize that I wouldn’t be able to sleep due to the all-night <b>din</b> from partygoers.", "related": "Cacophony (harsh, discordant, or meaningless mixture of sounds), Dissonance (harsh, inharmonious sound), Clamor (noisy uproar, as from a crowd)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "a constant DIN, like the roar of a crowded dinner hall.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disabuse", "pos": "verb", "pron": "diss-ah-BYOOZ", "also": "", "d1": "Free someone from a mistake in thinking", "sent": "Do you really believe that toilets flush one way in the Northern hemisphere and another way in the Southern? Any physicist would be happy to <b>disabuse</b> you of that silly notion.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Disabuse is almost always used in the pattern “to disabuse (person) of (idea).” Memory Trick: When someone disabuses you of a belief, they both “dis” and “abuse” your false ideas.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dis + abuse: strip away the mistaken belief someone is \"abusing.\"", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "discerning", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-ER-ning", "also": "", "d1": "Having good judgment or insight; able to distinguish mentally", "sent": "In an age in which we are bombarded with advertising, it’s important to be a <b>discerning</b> consumer. For instance, the term “all natural” is not federally regulated and doesn’t have to mean anything at all, so a smart shopper still reads ingredients.", "related": "Keen, Perceptive, and Perspicacious are all related to having good judgment or perception. Descry means to discover or see by looking carefully.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "able to discern differences: having sharp judgment and insight.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "discredit", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-CREH-dit", "also": "", "d1": "Injure the reputation of, destroy credibility of or confidence in", "sent": "Congresswoman Huffman’s opponent tried to use her friendship with a certain radical extremist to <b>discredit</b> her, even though the Congresswoman hadn’t seen this so-called “extremist” since sixth grade summer camp.", "related": "Slander, Traduce, and Defame all mean “to speak maliciously and falsely of.” Note, however, that slander is always wrong (slanderous statements are, by definition, lies), whereas it is possible (and sometimes for the greater good) to discredit someone by exposing the truth about that person—for instance, a lying political candidate, or a fake “expert” giving damaging advice.", "moreinfo": "The root “cred” means “belief” and also appears in credit, credible, creed, credo, credulous, incredulous, and accreditation.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dis + credit: take away someone's credit and trust.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "discrepancy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "diss-CREH-pen-see", "also": "discrepant (adj)", "d1": "Difference or inconsistency", "sent": "When there is a <b>discrepancy</b> between a store’s receipts and the amount of money in the register, the cashier’s behavior is generally called into question.", "related": "Incongruity (standing out), Discord or Discordance (harshness or inharmoniousness; disagreement)", "moreinfo": "Unlike a mere incongruity, which could be as innocent as one guy with a mohawk at an otherwise straight-laced gathering, a discrepancy usually indicates that someone has done something wrong. A discrepancy in your medical records means someone has made a mistake; a discrepancy in your story means you’re lying.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "a discrepancy is where things do not agree, a mismatch.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "discriminating", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-CRIM-in-ay-ting", "also": "", "d1": "Judicious, discerning, having good judgment or insight", "sent": "He is a man of <b>discriminating</b> tastes—all his suits are handmade in Italy, and I once saw him send back an entree when he complained that black truffle oil had been substituted for white. The chef was astounded.\nYou can tell a real Prada bag by the <b>discriminating</b> mark on the inside.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Many people automatically think of discriminating as bad, because they are thinking of racial discrimination. However, discriminating is simply telling things apart and can be an important skill—it is important to discriminate legitimate colleges from fraudulent diploma mills, for instance.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "a discriminating palate tells fine differences apart: judicious.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disingenuous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-in-JEN-yoo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Insincere, not genuine", "sent": "Christine used the fact that her mother spoke limited English as an opportunity to be <b>disingenuous</b>. When her mother asked, “Will there be boys at this sleepover?” she replied “There won’t not be boys there!”", "related": "Dissembling (misleading, concealing the truth, acting hypocritically), Prevaricating (misleading or lying)", "moreinfo": "Disingenuous appropriately describes misleading behavior that isn’t quite lying, like when you say “Let’s do the dishes!” when you really mean “Why don’t you do the dishes?” The opposite of disingenuous is ingenuous, or genuine.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dis + ingenuous (genuine): NOT genuine, insincere.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disinterested", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-INT-uh-rest-id", "also": "", "d1": "Unbiased, impartial; not interested", "sent": "Let’s settle this argument once and for all! We’ll get a <b>disinterested</b> observer to judge who can sing the highest note!", "related": "Dispassionate (unbiased, not having a selfish or personal motivation), Nonpartisan (impartial, not controlled by a political party)", "moreinfo": "Disinterested and uninterested have a confusing history. Some insist that disinterested only means “unbiased” and uninterested only means “not interested, apathetic.” However, throughout history, both words have been used to denote both meanings, so you’ll have to be guided by context in interpreting these words.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dis + interested: no personal stake in it, so unbiased.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disjointed", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-JOIN-tid", "also": "", "d1": "Disconnected, not coherent, jerky; having the joints separated", "sent": "The novel seemed <b>disjointed</b>, as though whole chunks of it were missing, or as though the author had tried to stitch together drafts of several different stories.\nTo begin the recipe, you’ll need a whole chicken that has been <b>disjointed</b>.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "In math, disjointed means having no common elements, as in “The set of all odd numbers and the set of all even numbers are disjointed.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dis + jointed: the joints come apart, choppy and disconnected.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dismiss", "pos": "verb", "pron": "diss-MISS", "also": "", "d1": "Allow to disperse or leave; fire from a job; put aside or reject, especially after only a brief consideration", "sent": "“Before I <b>dismiss</b> class,” said the teacher, “I want to remind you of the importance of <b>dismissing</b> biases in your research by ruling out or adjusting for factors other than the variable you are testing that may have led to your results.”", "related": "Discount as a verb can mean “disregard, ignore, or downgrade so as to compensate for exaggeration.”", "moreinfo": "To dismiss an idea is generally to choose to ignore it without having considered it seriously.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dismiss the class: send them off, or brush an idea aside.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dispassionate", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-PASH-shun-it", "also": "", "d1": "Unbiased, not having a selfish or personal motivation; calm, lacking emotion", "sent": "The defendant tearfully described how much her young child needed her at home, but the judge, who <b>dispassionately</b> sentenced her to ten years for selling drugs, was unmoved.", "related": "Disinterested (unbiased, impartial; not interested), Nonpartisan (impartial, not controlled by a political party)", "moreinfo": "Dispassionate could be positive or negative, depending on the context—a judge should be dispassionate, but if someone dispassionately broke up with you, you’d probably think that was pretty cold.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dis + passion: without passion, calm and neutral.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dispatch", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "DISS-patch (n), diss-PATCH (v)", "also": "", "d1": "Speed, promptness; send off or deal with in a speedy way", "sent": "So, you want to be a bike messenger? I need messengers who approach every delivery with alacrity, care, and <b>dispatch</b>—if the customers wanted their packages to arrive slowly, they’d use the post office.\nActing with all possible <b>dispatch</b>, emergency services <b>dispatched</b> a rescue squad to the scene.", "related": "Expediency (promoting a goal; advantageous, sometimes at the expense of morals or justice)", "moreinfo": "A dispatch can also be a report from a journalist or something delivered by messenger, or the act of sending a messenger. To dispatch a person can also mean to execute him or her— presumably, in a speedy manner.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dispatch the courier quickly: speed, or send off promptly.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disperse", "pos": "verb", "pron": "diss-PERSS", "also": "", "d1": "Scatter, spread widely, cause to vanish", "sent": "Because the demonstrators didn’t have a permit, the police showed up with megaphones, demanding loudly that the crowd <b>disperse</b>.\nGet the hose so I can <b>disperse</b> the dirt on our driveway.", "related": "Disseminate (scatter, spread about, broadcast), Diffuse (spread widely, disseminate; dispersed; or wordy and going off-topic)", "moreinfo": "Use disperse for something that spreads out and disappears (the crowd dispersed and went their separate ways); use disseminate for something that spreads out and remains significant (writers want to disseminate their ideas; disseminating seeds grows new plants).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "di + sperse (scatter): scatter widely, like seeds in the wind.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disposition", "pos": "noun", "pron": "diss-puh-ZISH-un", "also": "disposed (adj)", "d1": "A person’s general or natural mood; tendency", "sent": "She was possessed of a kind and helpful <b>disposition</b>—she wouldn’t just help you move, she’d bring home-baked muffins to the affair.\nI could really use some help in the kitchen, if you are so disposed.", "related": "Penchant and Predilection mean “tendency, preference” (a penchant for fast cards, a predilection to get angry easily).", "moreinfo": "Disposed and disposition are slightly old-fashioned words and occur in old-fashioned expressions: “possessed of a ____ disposition” and “so disposed” (meaning “inclined towards the kind of activities I just mentioned”).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "how you are \"disposed\": your natural, default mood.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disquieting", "pos": "adj", "pron": "diss-QUIET-ing", "also": "disquiet (noun or verb)", "d1": "Disturbing, causing anxiety", "sent": "Mr. Peters’ lack of emotion at his wife’s death was <b>disquieting</b>—so much so, in fact, that even his own family began to suspect he’d had something to do with it.\nHe was deeply disquieted by the racism he encountered in his new neighborhood.", "related": "Discomfiting (disconcerting, confusing, frustrating)", "moreinfo": "Think of disquiet not as the opposite of quiet, but more as an opposite to quiescent, meaning peaceful and calm.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 6", "setNum": 6, "hook": "dis + quiet: it unsettles your quiet, disturbing.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "disseminate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "diss-EM-in-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Scatter, spread about, broadcast", "sent": "Many plants use attractive fruits to <b>disseminate</b> their seeds—animals eat the fruit and excrete the seeds, allowing new plants to grow.\nIn the 1760s, revolutionary ideas were <b>disseminated</b> via pamphlets such as Thomas Paine’s “Common Sense.”", "related": "Disperse (scatter, spread widely, cause to vanish), Diffuse (spread widely, disseminate; dispersed; or wordy and going off-topic)", "moreinfo": "Disseminate contains the Latin root for “seed.” Use disperse for something that spreads out and disappears (the crowd dispersed); use disseminate for something that spreads out and remains significant (writers disseminate ideas; disseminating seeds grows new plants).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "dis + seminate (seed): scatter seeds and ideas everywhere.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dissent", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "diss-ENT", "also": "", "d1": "Disagree or take an opposing view, esp. in relation to a formal body such as a government, political party, or church; such a view", "sent": "Judge Antonin Scalia cast the only <b>dissenting</b> vote, explaining in his written decision why he thought all the other justices had it wrong.\nNot every country has a right to free speech (and thus to <b>dissent</b>), although nations that throw <b>dissenters</b> in jail are condemned by the international community at large.", "related": "Gainsay (deny, refute, oppose), Sedition (incitement of dissent against a government; promoting rebellion by speech or writing)", "moreinfo": "The variant dissidence tends to be a strong, longstanding, determined practice of dissenting.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "dis + sent (feel): a differing sentiment, disagreement.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dissonance", "pos": "noun", "pron": "DISS-uh-nenss", "also": "", "d1": "Harsh, inharmonious sound; cacophony; disagreement", "sent": "After allowing her sixth-grader’s heavy metal band to practice in her living room, Mrs. Rosen decided she’d better get used to <b>dissonance</b>.", "related": "Din (loud, confused noise), Clamor (noisy uproar, as from a crowd), Hubbub (loud noise, confusion)", "moreinfo": "An opposite to dissonance is assonance, primarily a poetry term, meaning sound resemblance or vowel rhyme. Harmony or euphony, meaning “good sound,” are more general antonyms.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "dis + sonance (sound): clashing, inharmonious sound; discord.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "distill", "pos": "verb", "pron": "diss-TILL", "also": "", "d1": "Purify; extract the essential elements of", "sent": "While traveling in certain countries, it is important to only drink <b>distilled</b> water so you don’t get sick.\nBob, it’s not necessary to read your entire Powerpoint presentation to us—can you just <b>distill</b> it down to the main point?", "related": "Gist and Pith can both be used to mean “essence, main idea,” as in, “This summary really distills the idea down to its pith, allowing me to get the gist without reading the whole book.”", "moreinfo": "Water is distilled through a process of vaporization and then condensation (when the water evaporates, impurities are left behind).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "distill liquor: boil it down to the pure essence.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "diverge", "pos": "verb", "pron": "die-VERGE", "also": "divergent (adj)", "d1": "Differ, deviate; branch off or turn aside, as from a path", "sent": "Go five miles until the old post office, then the road <b>diverges</b>—you want the branch that winds off to the left.\nThe high school sweethearts found that their paths <b>diverged</b> when they were accepted to different colleges.", "related": "Disparate (divergent, different)", "moreinfo": "The antonym of diverge is converge.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "di (apart) + verge: the paths branch apart, differ.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "divest", "pos": "verb", "pron": "die-VEST", "also": "", "d1": "Deprive or strip of a rank, title, etc., or of clothing or gear; to sell off holdings (opposite of invest)", "sent": "When she found out that the most profitable stock in her portfolio was that of a company that tested products on animals, she immediately <b>divested</b> by telling her broker to sell the stock.\nOnce his deception was exposed, he was <b>divested</b> of his position on the Board.", "related": "Arrogate (claim or take presumptuously or without right), Appropriate (as a verb, to set aside or authorize for a particular purpose; take for one’s own use)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "di + vest: take off the vest, strip away holdings (opposite of invest).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "divine", "pos": "verb", "pron": "div-VINE", "also": "", "d1": "Discover through divination or supernatural means; perceive by insight", "sent": "I’ve been poring over these quarterly reports all day, trying to <b>divine</b> whether I should buy or sell this stock.", "related": "Ascertain (find out with certainty), Perspicacity (acuteness of perception)", "moreinfo": "Of course, divine is most commonly an adjective, meanly “of or like a god; heavenly.” I read online reviews of the spa to divine whether it would really be as divine an experience was advertised.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "a diviner divines the hidden truth, perceiving it by insight.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "document", "pos": "verb", "pron": "DOCK-yoo-ment", "also": "", "d1": "Support with evidence, cite sources in a detailed way, create documentary evidence of", "sent": "Journalists embedded with military units are able to <b>document</b> a war as it happens.\nIt’s hard to deny her conclusion when her book is so well <b>documented</b>—she cites a relevant scientific study on practically every page.", "related": "Corroborate, substantiate and verify can all mean to prove true or provide evidence in favor of.", "moreinfo": "Of course, document can also be a noun (a factual printed item); this card focuses on the less common definition.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "document it: back it up with a detailed paper trail.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dogma", "pos": "noun", "pron": "DOG-muh", "also": "dogmatic (adj)", "d1": "A system of principles laid down by an authority; established belief", "sent": "It is part of the <b>dogma</b> of modern education that there are multiple intelligences that are equally valuable; try to suggest that some people just aren’t that smart, and you’ll find yourself a pariah.\n<b>Dogmatic</b> people will never change their minds, even in the face of evidence.", "related": "Doctrinaire (person who applies doctrine in an impractical or close-minded way)", "moreinfo": "Dogmatic is always meant in a negative way. However, dogma can be meant in a neutral or positive way as well; when people refer to their church’s dogma, they generally mean that they believe those tenets on the church’s authority.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "dogma, the fixed doctrine an authority says you must accept.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dormant", "pos": "adj", "pron": "DOR-muhnt", "also": "", "d1": "Asleep, inactive, on a break", "sent": "Some famous writers’ skills have lain <b>dormant</b> until quite late in life; Laura Ingalls Wilder didn’t publish Little House on the Prairie until she was 65.", "related": "Abeyance (temporary suspension, inactivity), Hiatus (break or gap in an activity), Deferment or Deferral (postponement)", "moreinfo": "If you speak Spanish or French, dormant will certainly remind you of the verb dormir, to sleep.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "dorm (sleep, as in dormitory): sleeping, inactive, on pause.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "dubious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "DOO-bee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Doubtful, questionable, suspect", "sent": "This applicant’s resume is filled with <b>dubious</b> qualifications—this is a marketing position, and this resume is mostly about whitewater rafting.", "related": "Apocryphal (of questionable authenticity, false), Ersatz (artificial, synthetic, serving as a substitute), Faux (fake, imitation, as in “faux fur”), Specious (pleasing to the eye but deceptive)", "moreinfo": "Dubious is related to the Latin word for “two,” perhaps in the sense of there being two explanations for something—the one presented, and the true one being hidden.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "dubious: full of doubt, questionable and sketchy.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "e.g.", "pos": "abbreviation for Latin “exempli gratia”", "pron": "ee-gee", "also": "", "d1": "For example, such as", "sent": "He was positively traumatized by the romantic comedies his girlfriend made him watch (<b>e.g.</b>, He’s Just Not That Into You).", "related": "i.e. is an abbrevation of Latin “id est,” or “that is” and means “that is to say, in other words,” as in “He finally nailed the lutz—i.e., a toepick-assisted figure skating jump with an entrance from the back outside edge.”", "moreinfo": "Use e.g. to introduce examples and i.e. to add a definition or clarification.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "e.g. = \"example given,\" for instance.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "eccentric", "pos": "adj", "pron": "eck-SENT-rick", "also": "", "d1": "Peculiar, odd, deviating from the norm esp. in a whimsical way", "sent": "The old woman was harmless but <b>eccentric</b>—not many senior citizens wear a train conductor’s uniform and carry a boom box.\nThe <b>eccentricity</b> of a planet’s orbit is the amount by which it deviates from a perfect circle; in general, comets are far more <b>eccentric</b> than known planets.", "related": "Idiosyncrasy (a peculiarity specific to a particular person)", "moreinfo": "Some have observed that only the rich are called eccentric; regular people are just weird.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "off-center (ec + centr): quirky, away from the middle of normal.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "eclectic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ick-CLECK-tick", "also": "", "d1": "Selecting the best of everything or from many diverse sources", "sent": "<b>Eclectic</b> taste is helpful in being a DJ—crowds love to hear the latest hip-hop mixed with ‘80s classics and other unexpected genres of music.\nThe restaurant features an <b>eclectic</b> menu—if you don’t like artisanal pasta or steak frites, try the chow mein!", "related": "Heterogeneous means “made up of diverse elements.” Hodgepodge, Medley, Farrago, Potpourri, Pastiche and Olio are all words for mixtures of diverse things.", "moreinfo": "The original Eclectics were Greek philosophers who chose what they thought were the best of other philosophers’ doctrines.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "an eclectic playlist picks the best from many different genres.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "eclipse", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "ick-CLIPS", "also": "", "d1": "The obscuring of one thing by another, such as the sun by the moon or a person by a more famous or talented person (noun); to obscure, darken, make less important (verb)", "sent": "During a solar <b>eclipse</b>, the moon <b>eclipses</b> the Sun.\nBilly Ray Cyrus, who had a hit song, “Achy Breaky Heart,” in the ‘90s, has long since found his fame <b>eclipsed</b> by that of his daughter, Miley.", "related": "Occult can mean “supernatural, mysterious, arcane,” but as a verb, can also mean “to hide from view or become hidden,” as in “The Pleiades, existing in deep sky, can be occulted by the moon when everything lines up just right” or “Other former members of NSYNC have felt their careers occulted (or eclipsed) by the stunning success of Justin Timberlake.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "an eclipse blocks out the sun; to overshadow or obscure.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "efficacy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "EFF-ih-cuh-see", "also": "", "d1": "The quality of being able to produce the intended effect", "sent": "Extensive trials will be necessary to determine whether the drug’s <b>efficacy</b> outweighs the side effects.\nI am having trouble cutting my steak with this butter knife; I’m going to ask the waiter for a more efficacious implement.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse efficacy with efficiency. Something efficacious gets the job done; something efficient gets the job done without wasting time or effort. Efficacity is a variant on efficacy and is identical in meaning.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "efficacy: how effective it is, whether it produces the intended effect.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "egalitarian", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ee-gal-it-TAIR-ee-uhn", "also": "", "d1": "Related to belief in the equality of all people, esp. in political, economic, or social spheres", "sent": "After moving to a more liberal part of the country, the couple was pleased to have neighbors who shared their views of <b>egalitarian</b> marriage—for instance, men and women could be found in equal proportions downshifting to part-time work to make time for childcare.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Egalitarian, of course, contains a root for “equal,” but comes into English through French, which explains the spelling (égal is French for “equal”).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "equal-itarian: believing everyone is equal.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "egregious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "igg-GREE-juss", "also": "", "d1": "Extraordinarily or conspicuously bad; glaring", "sent": "Your conduct is an <b>egregious</b> violation of our Honor Code—not only did you steal your roommate’s paper off his computer and turn it in as your own, you also sold his work to a plagiarism website so other cheaters could purchase it!", "related": "Flagrant (shamelessly bad or offensive, obvious, notorious)", "moreinfo": "Egregious shares a root with gregarious (sociable)—“greg” comes from a word for “herd, flock.” Think of egregious in the sense of standing out from the herd (in a bad way).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "e + greg (herd): it stands out from the herd, glaringly bad.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "elated", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ee-LAY-tid", "also": "", "d1": "Very happy, in high spirits", "sent": "I am <b>elated</b> that you flew my twin brother in from Australia to surprise me at my birthday party!", "related": "Ebullient (very enthusiastic, lively, excited), Blithe (joyous, merry; excessively carefree), Exultant (joyful, celebratory)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "elated: an \"elevated\" mood, on cloud nine.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "elevate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ELL-iv-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Raise, lift up; lift the spirits of; move up to a higher rank or status or raise up to a higher spiritual or intellectual plane", "sent": "After a year getting coffee and making copies, he hoped to be promoted to a more <b>elevated</b> position suitable for a law school graduate.\nOur goal here at Morality Publishing is not just to sell books, but to <b>elevate</b> our readers.", "related": "Lofty (in a high position, esp. in character, spirit, or rank), Edify (uplift, enlighten, instruct or improve in a spiritual or moral way)", "moreinfo": "Elevate shares the root “lev” (coming from a word meaning “to raise” or “light in weight”) with levitate, lever, and levity, meaning “lightness of personality or behavior, lack of seriousness.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "an elevator lifts you up: raise, lift higher.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "elicit", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ee-LISS-it", "also": "", "d1": "Call forth, bring out, evoke", "sent": "The touchdown <b>elicited</b> wild cheer from the crowd.\nWhile the death of Ellen’s elderly cat was indeed sad, Ellen’s constant, mournful looks whenever anyone mentioned any animal at all were nothing but a play to <b>elicit</b> sympathy.", "related": "Educe means “bring out, esp. something that has been dormant,” as in “The speech therapist finally educed (or elicited) a complete sentence from a boy who had been previously labeled ‘non-verbal.’”", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse with illicit, which is an adjective meaning “illegal.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "elicit a response: draw it out, evoke it (not \"illicit\").", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "eloquent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ELL-oh-kwent", "also": "", "d1": "Marked by forceful, fluid, apt speech; expressive, emotionally moving", "sent": "Wow, he’s such an <b>eloquent</b> speaker, he could sell snow to Antarcticans!\nWhen Mom suggested that everyone might enjoy a museum instead of the beach, she was met with the children’s <b>eloquent</b> looks of disgust.", "related": "Rhetorical and oratorical are words related to the art of public speaking. While a lawyer needs good rhetorical skills, sometimes rhetorical and oratorical mean only related to style and effect, and lacking substance. Eloquent, however, is always positive—even in the latter sentence above, the eloquent looks of disgust are very effective in expressing the children’s feelings.", "moreinfo": "The root “loq” means “speech” and also appears in loquacious (talkative) and interlocutor (participant in a dialogue; interrogator).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "e + loqu (speak): speaking fluently and movingly.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "embellish", "pos": "verb", "pron": "em-BELL-ish", "also": "", "d1": "Decorate, add ornamentation; enhance (a story) with fictional or fanciful details", "sent": "Every time she tells that story, she <b>embellishes</b> it quite a bit—at first, she was lost in the woods and then she was found. The part with the grizzly bear was added later.\nThe Christmas sweater was <b>embellished</b> with festive jingle bells!", "related": "Bedeck means “adorn or deck out,” as in “The church was bedecked with flowers for the wedding.” This is essentially the same word as in the Christmas carol “Deck the Halls.”", "moreinfo": "Embellish contains a root for beauty, “bell,” as in “belle of the ball.” (This can be a quite confusing root, though, as “bell” can also mean “war,” as in belligerent, bellicose, rebellion. In Latin, “bellus” meant “dress up” and “bellum” meant war).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "embellish a story: add fancy trimmings and ornament.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "eminent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "EM-in-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Prominent, distinguished, of high rank", "sent": "The undergraduate shocked everyone by asking the <b>eminent</b> old professor, “Really? What makes you such an expert?”\n“Your Eminence!” said the bishop to the Pope, “Don’t forget this copy of your speech!”", "related": "Venerable (worthy of deep respect, hallowed, dignified)", "moreinfo": "Eminent domain is the law allowing the government to appropriate private property, such as forcing people to move so a new highway can be built. And, while supposedly Marshall Mathers selected the pseudonym “Eminem” based on his initials (M&M), we like to think he was aware of the name’s strong resemblance to eminent.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "an eminent figure stands out, high and distinguished.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "empirical", "pos": "adj", "pron": "em-PEER-ick-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Coming from, based on, or able to be verified by experience or experimentation; not purely based on theory", "sent": "The Ancient Greeks philosophized about the nature of matter (concluding, for instance, that everything was made of earth, water, air, and fire) without any <b>empirical</b> evidence—that is, the very idea of conducting experiments hadn’t been invented yet.\nPeople always knew <b>empirically</b> that when something is dropped, it falls to the ground; the theory of gravity later explained why.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "In Ancient Greece, practitioners of medicine who relied on experience rather than theory were of the Empiric School; in contrast, the Dogmatic school followed the teachings of Hippocrates above all. In philosophy, a priori knowledge is that which can be deduced from logic alone, and a posteriori knowledge is empirical.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 7", "setNum": 7, "hook": "empirical: proven by experiment and experience, not just theory.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "emulate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "EM-yoo-layt", "also": "", "d1": "Copy in an attempt to equal or be better than", "sent": "The ardent Star Trek fan <b>emulated</b> Captain Kirk in every way possible—his brash and confident leadership might have gotten him somewhere, but the women he tried to impress weren’t so impressed.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Imitate merely means to copy; emulate means to copy in a positive, admiring way. A word for “copy” that has a somewhat negative connotation is ape (based on the idea of a large primate trying to copy humans), as in “While he tried to ape Hemingway’s style, the truth is, he simply wasn’t a very good writer.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "emulate your hero: imitate them to match or beat them.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "enervate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "EN-er-vayt", "also": "", "d1": "Weaken, tire", "sent": "After taking the SAT in the morning and playing in a soccer game in the afternoon, Trina was truly <b>enervated</b> before the prom even began. “You dance like a grandmother with osteoporosis,” said her date.", "related": "Sap (weaken, undermine, destroy in an underhanded way), Enfeeble (weaken, make feeble)", "moreinfo": "The “e” in enervate is a variant of the prefix “ex” and means “out,” while “nerv” means “nerve, sinew.” Today, the word doesn’t exactly mean “remove the nerves from,” although that certainly does sound like something that would weaken a person.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "it drains your \"energy\" out, leaving you weak (opposite of energize).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "enhance", "pos": "verb", "pron": "en-HANSS", "also": "", "d1": "Raise to a higher value, desirability, etc.", "sent": "The cosmetics industry stays in business because so many people want to <b>enhance</b> their appearances.", "related": "Embellish (decorate, add ornamentation), Aggrandize (make greater; exaggerate)", "moreinfo": "Enhance may come from the Old French “enhaucer,” meaning “to raise” and sharing an origin with haughty, or arrogant.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "enhance: raise the value, make it better.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "enigma", "pos": "noun", "pron": "en-IGG-muh", "also": "", "d1": "Puzzle, mystery, riddle; mysterious or contradictory person", "sent": "The enormous rock sculptures in Stonehenge, England are truly an <b>enigma</b>—were they created as part of a religious observance, or in deference to a great ruler? We may never know.", "related": "Paradox (contradiction, or seeming contradiction that is actually true), Conundrum (a riddle, the answer to which involves a play on words; any mystery)", "moreinfo": "Enigma comes from a Greek word for speaking in riddles, which itself comes from a word for “fable.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "an enigma is a baffling mystery or riddle.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "entitlement", "pos": "noun", "pron": "en-TIE-tull-ment", "also": "", "d1": "Having the right to certain privileges; believing, sometimes without cause, that one deserves or has a right to certain privileges", "sent": "Many bosses complain about young people’s sense of <b>entitlement</b>—raised on a steady diet of praise from parents and teachers, these young people are shocked to be expected to “pay their dues” at a new job.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Entitlement originally referred to investing a person with a title, as in “Charles Lennox was entitled Duke of Richmond in 1675.” An entitlement can also mean a government benefit, as in “Social Security payments and other entitlements.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "entitled: feeling owed special privileges.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "enumerate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ee-NOO-mer-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Count or list; specify one-by-one", "sent": "The Bill of Rights <b>enumerates</b> the basic rights held by every citizen of the United States.\nI don’t have time to <b>enumerate</b> all the steps involved in baking a cake—just find a recipe on the internet!", "related": "Reckon means “count” or “consider or regard as,” as in “The math teacher is reckoned the only person in the school who can reckon the number of primes under 500 in less than one minute.”", "moreinfo": "Enumerable means “countable,” not to be confused with innumerable, which means “numerous, a lot.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "e + numer (number): number them off one by one.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ephemeral", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ee-FEM-er-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Lasting only a short time, fleeting", "sent": "“Thank you for this jacket that says ‘Eugene’s Girl,’” said Marie, “but I fear that your love will prove to be <b>ephemeral</b>—over the last two years, I’ve seen four other girls in school with the same jacket. Do you buy them in six-packs?”", "related": "Evanescent and Fugacious are synonyms. Transient can mean “lasting only a short time, temporary” or “staying only a short time,” or can be a noun referring to homeless people, temporary workers, or others who move often.", "moreinfo": "Ephemeral comes from a Greek word for “day.” It originally meant—and sometimes still means—lasting only one day.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "the mayfly (Ephemera) lives one day: lasting a very short time.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "equitable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ECK-wit-uh-bull", "also": "equity (noun)", "d1": "Fair, equal, just", "sent": "As the university president was heavily biased towards the sciences, faculty in the liberal arts felt they had to fight to get an <b>equitable</b> share of funding for their departments.", "related": "Evenhanded (fair, impartial), Parity (equality or equivalence)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse equitable and equity with equanimity and equanimous; the first set is about equality, the second set about being even-tempered.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "equity is fairness: even, just, evenhanded.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "erratic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "er-RAT-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Inconsistent, wandering, having no fixed course", "sent": "When someone engages in <b>erratic</b> behavior, family members often suspect drug use or mental illness. However, sometimes the person is just building a top-secret invention in the garage!", "related": "Desultory (lacking consistency or order, disconnected, sporadic; going off topic)", "moreinfo": "Erratic shares a root with error and the verb err, which originally meant “to stray or wander”—that is, to be erratic—but now means “to make a mistake.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "erratic wanders like an \"errant\" path: inconsistent, no fixed course.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "erroneous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "er-ROH-nee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Mistaken, in error; improper, morally incorrect", "sent": "Hilda was completely unable to assemble her new desk chair after the instructions <b>erroneously</b> instructed her to screw the left armrest onto a small lever on the bottom of the seat.", "related": "Fallible (liable to be in error, capable of making mistakes)", "moreinfo": "Erroneous contains the root “err,” from a word for “stray or wander.” The related word errant can mean “incorrect” but can also mean “journeying or roving adventurously,” as in a medieval “knight-errant.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "erroneous: full of errors, mistaken.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "erudite", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AIR-yoo-dite", "also": "erudition (noun)", "d1": "Scholarly, knowledgeable; possessing deep, often systematic, knowledge", "sent": "Some have said that Americans dislike <b>erudite</b> leaders; while German leaders frequently have Ph.D.’s, even speaking a foreign language is considered pretentious by many American voters.", "related": "Learned is a synonym. Cerebral can mean “intellectual, esp. as opposed to instinctual or emotional.” Esoteric, recondite, and abstruse all mean “pertaining to difficult or obscure knowledge; known to only a few.”", "moreinfo": "Erudite comes from the Latin “erudire,” meaning “to polish.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "erudite: deeply learned and scholarly, well-read.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "eschew", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ess-CHOO", "also": "", "d1": "Shun, avoid, abstain from", "sent": "As a vegan, he <b>eschewed</b> not only meat and dairy but also anything made of leather.", "related": "Abjure (give up, renounce; repudiate, recant, or shun, especially formally or under oath), Forswear (reject or renounce under oath; swear falsely in court)", "moreinfo": "Pronounce this word just as it looks: “ess-CHEW.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "\"es-CHEW\" and spit it out: shun and avoid it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "esoteric", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ess-oh-TAIR-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Understood by or intended for only a few; secret", "sent": "In his first year of university-level physics, he felt he was merely memorizing information found in every textbook on the subject; by his fourth year, he spent his days poring over <b>esoteric</b> journal articles that few people had ever read or understood.", "related": "Arcane and Recondite are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "The “eso” in esoteric means “in”—in this case, in the sense of “inside knowledge.” An antonym is exoteric (“exo” = out), as in that which is “common knowledge.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "esoteric: inside secret knowledge meant for only a few.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "estimable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ESS-tim-uh-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Worthy of esteem, admirable; able to be estimated", "sent": "As the first Black president of the Harvard Law Review, Barack Obama presented an <b>estimable</b> resume when he ran for President in 2008.\nRiding a roller coaster is safer than driving on the highway, but there is still an <b>estimable</b> risk.", "related": "Calculable is a synonym for estimable as “able to be estimated.”", "moreinfo": "Inestimable generally means “so large it cannot be estimated,” as in “The Rosetta Stone was of inestimable value to scholars of Egyptian history.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "estimable: worthy of esteem, admirable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "eulogy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "YOU-luh-gee", "also": "", "d1": "Speech of praise or written work of praise, esp. a speech given at a funeral", "sent": "While it was hard for Xing to write a <b>eulogy</b> for his friend, he was pleased to be able to tell others at the funeral some wonderful things about him that they hadn’t known.\nThe review of the book was pure <b>eulogy</b>—usually, this publication runs more balanced articles.", "related": "Encomium (warm, glowing praise, esp. a formal expression of praise), Laudation (praise, tribute), Panegyric (formal, lofty, or elaborate praise), Paean (song of praise, triumph, or thanks)", "moreinfo": "“Eu” is a Greek root for “good,” and “log” or “logos” for “discourse.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "eu (good) + logy (speech): good words of praise, esp. at a funeral.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "exacerbate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "egg-ZASS-er-bayt", "also": "", "d1": "Make worse (more violent, severe, etc.), inflame; irritate or embitter (a person)", "sent": "Allowing your band to practice in our garage has greatly <b>exacerbated</b> my headache.", "related": "Aggravate is a synonym.", "moreinfo": "Exacerbate shares a root with acerbic, meaning “sour; harsh or severe.” The prefix “ex” means “out” but can also have the meaning of “thoroughly” or “utterly.” To acerbate is to make more acerbic; to exacerbate is to make utterly acerbic.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "\"ex-ACID-bate\": pour acid on it, make it worse.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "exacting", "pos": "adj", "pron": "egg-ZACK-ting", "also": "", "d1": "Very severe in making demands; requiring precise attention", "sent": "The boxing coach was <b>exacting</b>, analyzing Joey’s footwork down to the millimeter and forcing him to repeat movements hundreds of times until they were correct.", "related": "Meticulous (taking extreme care with details; fussy), Fastidious (excessively particular, hard to please)", "moreinfo": "Exaction is a word for extortion, or demanding money under threat. To exact as a verb can mean to extort money, or simply to demand, as in “to exact obedience from the soldiers.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "exacting: it demands exactness, strict and precise.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "exculpate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ECK-skull-payt", "also": "exculpatory (adj)", "d1": "Clear from guilt or blame", "sent": "The security camera footage showing Mr. Murphy to have been in a casino the entire night turned out to be just the evidence needed to <b>exculpate</b> him of robbing a bank fifty miles away.", "related": "Vindicate and Exonerate are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "“Ex” in this context means “out” and “culp” (as in culprit and culpable) means “blame.” Exculpate has a very rare antonym, inculpate, meaning “charge with fault, incriminate.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "ex (out) + culpa (guilt): take the guilt out, clear the blame.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "exhaustive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "egg-ZAW-stiv", "also": "", "d1": "Comprehensive, thorough, exhausting a topic or subject, accounting for all possibilities; draining, tending to exhaust", "sent": "The Standard Book of British Birds provides an <b>exhaustive</b> treatment of the subject—you will find that not a single British bird has been omitted.\nThe rebels finally surrendered after an <b>exhaustive</b> siege that left them without ammunition or even food.", "related": "Thoroughgoing (thorough, complete; zealous), Omnibus (including or providing for many items)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "exhaustive: so thorough it covers everything until exhausted.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "explicit", "pos": "adj", "pron": "eck-SPLISS-it", "also": "", "d1": "Direct, clear, fully revealed; clearly depicting sex or nudity", "sent": "The goal of my motivational talk is to make <b>explicit</b> the connection between staying in school and avoiding a life of crime.", "related": "Unequivocal (clear, having only one possible meaning), Explicate (make clear)", "moreinfo": "The antonym of explicit is implicit or tacit, meaning “hinted at, implied.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "explicit: spelled out plainly and directly, no guessing.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "exponent", "pos": "noun", "pron": "eck-SPOH-nent", "also": "", "d1": "Person who expounds or explains; champion, advocate, or representative", "sent": "An <b>exponent</b> of clean fuel, he petitioned the state government to commit to replacing conventional energy with solar and wind energy where possible.", "related": "Proponent (person who proposes or argues in favor of something)", "moreinfo": "Of course, an exponent is also the number to which another number is raised. Both senses of the word come from the verb expound, meaning “interpret, explain, state in detail.” In a sense, an exponent on a number expounds upon that number by telling us how many times the number is to be multiplied by itself.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "an exponent expounds and champions an idea, a promoter.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "extraneous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "eck-STRAY-nee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Irrelevant; foreign, coming from without, not belonging", "sent": "This essay would be stronger if you removed <b>extraneous</b> information; this paragraph about the author’s life doesn’t happen to be relevant to your thesis.\nMaize, which originated in the New World, is <b>extraneous</b> to Europe.", "related": "Superfluous (extra, unnecessary, excessive)", "moreinfo": "In Latin, “extra” means “outside of.” The “coming from without” meaning of extraneous can also be expressed with extrinsic, the antonym of which is intrinsic.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "extra: unneeded extra stuff that does not belong, irrelevant.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "extrapolate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "eck-STRAP-oh-layt", "also": "", "d1": "Conjecture about an unknown by projecting information about something known; predict by projecting past experience", "sent": "No, I’ve never been to Bryn Mawr, but I’ve visited several small, private women’s colleges in the Northeast, so I think I can <b>extrapolate</b>.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "In math and science, to extrapolate is to infer values in an unobserved interval from values in an observed interval. For instance, from the points (1, 4) and (3, 8), you could extrapolate the point (5, 12), since it would be on the same line. The antonym of extrapolate is interpolate, to estimate between two known values, such as by using (1, 4) and (3, 8) to assume (2, 6). Interpolate can also mean “insert words or foreign material into a text or conversation.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "extend the known line outward: predict beyond what is given.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "facetious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "fuh-SEE-shuss", "also": "", "d1": "Joking, humorous, esp. inappropriately; not serious, concerned with frivolous things", "sent": "When I said, “Sure, you can take anything in my house as a souvenir of this study session,” I was being <b>facetious</b>! I would like my nightgown back now.\nHe’s a <b>facetious</b> person—I doubt he will take your offer of a spiritual quest very seriously.", "related": "Waggish (merry, roguish), Risible (laughable, related to laughing), Jocular, Jocund, or Jocose (jesting, jolly), Droll (funny in an odd way)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse facetious with fatuous, meaning “foolish, silly, inane.” Facetious people can be smartly sarcastic; fatuous people are dull and dim-witted. Facetious comes from a Latin word for clever; fatuous comes from a word for gaping—as in, having one’s mouth hang open like a very stupid person.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "keeping a straight \"face\" while joking: humorous, not serious.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "facilitate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "fuh-SILL-it-tayt", "also": "", "d1": "Make easier, help the progress of", "sent": "A good meeting <b>facilitator</b> lets everyone be heard while still keeping the meeting focused.\nAs a midwife, my goal is simply to <b>facilitate</b> a natural process.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Speakers of Spanish and French will certainly recognize facilitate’s similarity with “fácil” or “facile,” meaning “easy.” Interestingly, though, the word facile in English can be negative, meaning “shallow or superficial”—that is, a little too easy.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 8", "setNum": 8, "hook": "facil (easy): make it easy, help the progress along.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fallacious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "fuh-LAY-shuss", "also": "fallacy (noun)", "d1": "Containing a fallacy, or mistake in logic; logically unsound; deceptive", "sent": "The formal study of logic can enable a student to more easily identify <b>fallacious</b> reasoning and, furthermore, to point out its fallacies.", "related": "That which is fallacious definitely embodies a logical mistake, while that which is fallible is capable of being wrong (but may not actually be).", "moreinfo": "Among the logical fallacies are the Ad Hominem Attack (attacking the person rather than the argument) and the Post Hoc fallacy (confusing correlation with causation).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "fallacious: built on a fallacy, logically false.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fanatical", "pos": "adj", "pron": "fuh-NAT-ick-ull", "also": "fanatic (noun)", "d1": "Excessively devoted, enthusiastic, or zealous in an uncritical way", "sent": "We avoid our neighbors—they’re fanatics who can’t go five minutes without trying to convert you to their beliefs.\nMrs. Becker was <b>fanatical</b> about grammar, once deducting fifteen points from a student’s paper for a misused semicolon—and it was a physics class!", "related": "Ardent (very passionate), Zealous (full of fervor or dedicated enthusiasm for a cause, person, etc.). Also Fervent, Fervid, and Perfervid all mean “passionate, fiery, deeply enthusiastic.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "a fan taken way too far: excessively, uncritically zealous.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fanciful", "pos": "adj", "pron": "FAN-sih-full", "also": "", "d1": "Whimsical, capricious; imaginary; freely imaginative rather than based on reason or reality", "sent": "The play was set in a <b>fanciful</b> version of New York City, one where all the cab drivers spoke perfect English and the Statue of Liberty seemed to be in the middle of the island.", "related": "Whimsical (fanciful, eccentric) is a near-synonym. Capricious (acting on impulse, erratic) is also close, but is sometimes used negatively.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "full of fancy: whimsical, imaginary, dreamed up.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fathom", "pos": "verb", "pron": "FA-thum", "also": "", "d1": "Measure the depth of (usually of water) as with a sounding line; penetrate and discover the meaning of, understand", "sent": "I cannot even remotely <b>fathom</b> how you interpreted an invitation to sleep on my couch as permission to take my car on a six-hour joyride!", "related": "Sound and Plumb (used as verbs) are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "A sounding line or plumb line is a length of rope with a weight at the bottom; dropping it into water will allow the weight to sink and water to be measured in fathoms (six-foot increments —fathom comes from a word for outstretched arms, which on an adult male tend to measure about six feet).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "you cannot fathom it: cannot sound the depths to understand it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "feasible", "pos": "adj", "pron": "FEE-zih-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Possible; logical or likely; suitable", "sent": "Your plan to promote our product launch with a parade is just not <b>feasible</b>—we don’t have the money or enough time to get the permits.", "related": "Plausible (credible, having the appearance of truth), Viable (able to live or develop; capable of success, practicable, workable)", "moreinfo": "Feasible shares a root (“do, make”) with factory, factitious, and laissez-faire.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "feasible: it can be done (root fais = do), workable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fidelity", "pos": "noun", "pron": "fid-DELL-it-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Faithfulness, loyalty; strict observance of duty; accuracy in reproducing a sound or image", "sent": "Wedding vows typically include a promise of <b>fidelity</b>—such as by “forsaking all others as long as I may live.”", "related": "Constancy (loyalty, the state of being unwavering)", "moreinfo": "Fidelity contains the root “fid,” for “trust,” also appearing in diffident, fiduciary, infidel, and Fidel Castro’s name.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "Fido the loyal dog: faithfulness and loyalty; also sound accuracy.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "figurative", "pos": "adj", "pron": "FIG-yer-uh-tiv", "also": "", "d1": "Metaphorical, based on figures of speech; containing many figures of speech (as fancy- sounding writing); related to portraying human or animal figures", "sent": "The painter was renowned for his <b>figurative</b> art, including many portraits—he had been known to say that abstract artists were just people who had never learned to draw.\nHighly <b>figurative</b> language can be difficult for English language learners—for instance, to “throw the baby out with the bath water” refers to being too hasty and unwisely getting rid of the good with the bad.", "related": "The opposite of figurative is literal, although in regular speech, people often use literal to mean figurative. People who say I was literally ready to kill someone rarely mean that, which is why we are not alarmed when they say that. We understand that they are really speaking figuratively.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "a figure of speech: metaphorical, not literal.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "finesse", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "fin-NESS", "also": "", "d1": "Extreme delicacy, subtlety, or diplomacy in handling a sensitive situation or in a performance or skill (noun); use tact or diplomacy; employ a deceptive strategy (verb)", "sent": "After the prince deeply insulted his hosts, the diplomat was able to <b>finesse</b> the situation, playing it off as a translation error and getting the negotiations back on track.", "related": "Tact (sensitivity to what is proper and inoffensive)", "moreinfo": "Finesse is also a brand of shampoo. The commercials typically feature a person getting into—and then out of—a tough situation. The slogan is “Sometimes you need a little finesse, sometimes you need a lot.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "handle it with finesse: smooth skill, delicacy, and tact.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "flag", "pos": "verb", "pron": "FLAG", "also": "", "d1": "Get tired, lose enthusiasm; hang limply or droop", "sent": "Our grandmother is so physically fit that she was ready to make the rounds of the entire amusement park again after lunch, while most of us were <b>flagging</b> and just wanted to sit.", "related": "Enervate (weaken, tire)", "moreinfo": "One way to think of the “get tired” meaning of flag is to think of how a flag waving in the breeze droops and hangs when the wind stops. Of course, flag as a verb can also mean “to hail or wave down,” as in “flag a taxi,” or “to mark,” as in “flag a page of a book with a sticky note.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "your energy flags: it droops like a flag with no wind.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fleeting", "pos": "adj", "pron": "FLEE-ting", "also": "", "d1": "Passing quickly, transitory", "sent": "I had assumed our summer romance would be <b>fleeting</b>, so I was very surprised when you proposed marriage!", "related": "Ephemeral, Evanescent and Fugacious are synonyms. Fugitive is best known as meaning “running from the law,” but can also mean fleeting.", "moreinfo": "A fleet is a group of ships controlled together. The connection is the root word, “to float”—a fleet of boats floats on the water, and a fleeting romance, for instance, is one that simply seems to float away.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "fleeting: fast as a fleet, gone in a flash.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "foment", "pos": "verb", "pron": "FOH-ment or FAH-ment", "also": "", "d1": "Incite, instigate, stir up, promote the growth of; apply medicated liquid to a body part", "sent": "The revolutionary group was quietly <b>fomenting</b> a rebellion, galvanizing student radicals, leading unions in revolutionary songs, and anonymously pasting incendiary posters in every quarter of the city.", "related": "Rouse (wake up; incite, stir up)", "moreinfo": "Abigail Adams famously told her husband John Adams that, if left out of the Constitution, ladies would “foment a rebellion.” Foment comes from a Latin word for a poultice or hot compress. The similiar-sound ferment (to undergo fermentation as in yogurt, kimchi, etc., such as by a yeast or bacterium) can also be used to mean “excite or agitate”—you can foment or ferment a rebellion.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "foment unrest: stir the pot and incite it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "foreshadow", "pos": "verb", "pron": "for-SHAD-oh", "also": "foreshadowing (noun)", "d1": "Indicate or suggest beforehand, presage", "sent": "You didn’t know this was a horror movie? I thought it was pretty clear that the children’s ghost story around the campfire was meant to <b>foreshadow</b> the horrible things that would happen to them years later as teenagers at a motel in the middle of the woods.", "related": "Prefigure is a synonym. Forerun means run before or foreshadow. Harbinger and Herald mean a person or thing that indicates what is to come (Herald can also mean “messenger,” including about something in the past). Portentous and Ominous can mean “giving a bad sign about the future” (portentous can also mean “very significant, exciting wonder and awe”).", "moreinfo": "“Fore” means “before”—foreshadow literally comes from the idea that an object’s shadow sometimes arrives before the object does.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "fore (before) + shadow: cast a shadow of what is coming.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "forfeit", "pos": "verb", "pron": "FOR-fit", "also": "forfeiture (noun)", "d1": "Surrender or lose as a result of an error, crime, or failure to fulfill an obligation", "sent": "“The rules are clear,” said the umpire. “This is a co-ed league, and if your team doesn’t have at least three women, you <b>forfeit</b>. Sorry, everybody, no game today!”\nIf you are found guilty of defrauding this casino, the <b>forfeiture</b> of your winnings will be only the first of the consequences coming your way.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Forfeit comes from the Middle English “forfet,” for “crime” (people often have to give up, or forfeit, rights or property as punishment for a crime). A forfeit, as a noun, simply means the property, right, etc. that was forfeited.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "forfeit the game: surrender or lose it as a penalty.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fortify", "pos": "verb", "pron": "FOR-tih-fy", "also": "", "d1": "Strengthen, invigorate, encourage", "sent": "The white bread found in American grocery stores has been stripped of all the nutrients naturally found in wheat, and then artificially fortified with vitamins and minerals.\nThe general called for reinforcements to <b>fortify</b> the defenses around the capital.", "related": "Bolster (strengthen or support), Buttress (a support against a building; to strengthen or support), Galvanize (stimulate with electric current; excite, stimulate to action)", "moreinfo": "Fortify shares a root (meaning “strong”) with fortress and fortitude (strength in facing adversity).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "fort + ify: build a fort around it, strengthen it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "fringe", "pos": "noun, adj, noun", "pron": "FRIN’j", "also": "", "d1": "On the margin, periphery (adj); the people in a group who hold the most extreme views (noun)", "sent": "In America, reincarnation is a <b>fringe</b> belief, but in primarily Hindu countries, the belief is quite mainstream.\nStacey and Mark liked to say they lived on the <b>fringe</b> of the big city, but really they had just moved to the suburbs.", "related": "Penumbra (partial shadow in an eclipse; outer area, periphery)", "moreinfo": "Fringe is also, of course, a clothing decoration of hanging cords, strings, etc. (as worn by cowboys or hippies). The connection is that fringe is on the border or edge of clothes, just as fringe ideas are on the border or edge of society.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "the fringe is the outer edge; fringe views are far out on the margin.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "frugal", "pos": "adj", "pron": "FROO-gull", "also": "frugality (noun)", "d1": "Economical, thrifty, not wasteful with money; inexpensive", "sent": "It wasn’t terribly surprising when Lea—who was so <b>frugal</b> in restaurants that she always drank water, ate salad, and requested a separate check—said she had never tried lobster.", "related": "Provident (showing foresight, providing for the future, frugal), Stinting (frugal, acting sparingly or with restraint)", "moreinfo": "Frugal comes from the Latin word for “fruit.” By the way, don’t confuse these two adjectives: economical means not spending too much money; economic means pertaining to the science of economics.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "frugal \"few-gal\": spends few dollars, thrifty.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "futile", "pos": "adj", "pron": "FYOO-tull or FYOO-tile", "also": "", "d1": "Producing no useful result, ineffective; trivial or unimportant", "sent": "She spent months trying to coax Fluffy to fetch and sit, but it was <b>futile</b> —cats just can’t be trained to perform tricks.", "related": "Bootless (useless), Otiose (lazy, idle; ineffective or useless)", "moreinfo": "Futile comes from a Latin word for “pouring out easily,” as in a water jug that is full of unfortunate cracks or holes.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "futile: pointless, producing no useful result.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "gainsay", "pos": "verb", "pron": "gayn-SAY", "also": "", "d1": "Declare false, deny; oppose", "sent": "The professor is quite doctrinaire—she’s been known to lower the grade of any student who dares <b>gainsay</b> her.", "related": "Dissent (disagree or take an opposing view, esp. in relation to a formal body such as a government)", "moreinfo": "In Old English, “gain” actually meant “against,” although most other words formed from this word part are now obsolete (as in “gainclap,” a counterstroke, such as in battle).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "gain + say: to say against, to deny or contradict.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "garrulous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "GAIR-ull-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Talkative, wordy, rambling", "sent": "Uncle Bill is so <b>garrulous</b> that our dinner conversation lasted three hours—and the only person who said more than ten words was Uncle Bill.", "related": "Prolix, Verbose, and Loquacious all mean talkative or wordy. Garrulous adds the sense of going off-topic or being tiresome. For instance, a very long-winded but informative academic paper would be prolix or verbose, but not garrulous.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "garrulous: gargles words endlessly, will not stop talking.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "gauche", "pos": "adj", "pron": "GOH’sh", "also": "", "d1": "Tactless, lacking social grace, awkward, crude", "sent": "It is terribly <b>gauche</b> to put ketchup on your steak and then talk with your mouth full as you eat it. That’s the last time I ever bring you to a nice place.", "related": "Boorish (rude, ill-mannered, insensitive), Meretricious (attractive in a vulgar way, specious), Uncouth (having bad manners, awkward)", "moreinfo": "Sadly, nearly all cultures are biased against left-handed people. Gauche is simply the French word for left (similarly, the word sinister comes from the Latin word for left). The French word for right gives us the English word adroit, which means “skilled.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "gauche is French for \"left\": socially clumsy and off, tactless.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "gawky", "pos": "adj", "pron": "GAW-kee", "also": "", "d1": "Physically awkward (esp. of a tall, skinny person, often used to describe teenagers)", "sent": "As a teenager, she thought of herself as <b>gawky</b> and often slouched so as not to seem so much taller than her peers; of course, now that she’s a supermodel, no one thinks of her as <b>gawky</b> at all.", "related": "Coltish (playful, wild; resembling a young horse, esp. having disproportionately long legs), Ungainly (awkward, ungraceful)", "moreinfo": "To gawk is to stare at something in a blatant, often surprised way. The origin of gawk is related to an old word for left, or left-handed.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "gawky: a gangly, staring, awkward teen.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "germane", "pos": "adj", "pron": "jer-MANE", "also": "", "d1": "Relevant and appropriate, on-topic", "sent": "This is a business meeting, not a social club—let’s keep our comments <b>germane</b> to the issue of the new campaign.", "related": "Pertinent (relevant), Akin (related by blood; analogous or similar) Memory Trick: When four of the Jackson 5 would get off track, it was always Jermaine who would direct the conversation back to the topic at hand.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "germane, akin and related: relevant and on-topic.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "gist", "pos": "noun", "pron": "JIST", "also": "", "d1": "Main idea, essence", "sent": "I didn’t read the whole book, but I read enough to get the <b>gist</b>.", "related": "Pith (central part, essence), Precis (summary of the essentials of a text)", "moreinfo": "If you read enough to get the pith of something, you really deeply understand it to the core; if you read enough to get the gist, you probably just skimmed, and might need to go back later to get more detail.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "just give me the gist: the core point, the essence.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "glib", "pos": "adj", "pron": "GLIB", "also": "", "d1": "Fluent and easy in a way that suggests superficiality or insincerity", "sent": "She was the worst teacher he had ever encountered, giving <b>glib</b> responses to every question. “Can you help me with this algebra problem?” he asked. “Oh, just solve for x,” she said, and walked away.", "related": "Flippant (disrespectfully casual or light in manner), Impertinent (inappropriately bold), Saucy (disrespectful or irrepressible, esp. in an entertaining way)", "moreinfo": "Glib comes from a Germanic root for “slippery.” A glib comment “slips” right out of your mouth—when you should have spent more time thinking up something more meaningful.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "glib: slick words that glide out too easily, superficial.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "goosebumps", "pos": "noun", "pron": "GOOSE-bumps", "also": "", "d1": "The “bumps” created by hairs standing up on the skin in response to cold, fear, etc.", "sent": "That detective novel is hard to put down! I’ve got <b>goosebumps</b> just waiting to find out what happens next!\nYou’ve got <b>goosebumps</b>—why don’t you borrow my jacket?", "related": "", "moreinfo": "When a goose’s feathers are plucked, bumps are left behind on the skin. “Goose flesh” or “goose pimples” are expressions that mean the same thing (goosebumps, however, is more often used metaphorically and has appeared in official GRE materials).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 9", "setNum": 9, "hook": "literal goosebumps, the tiny bumps on your skin from cold or fear.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "gradation", "pos": "noun", "pron": "gray-DAY-shun", "also": "grade (verb)", "d1": "A progression, a process taking place gradually, in stages; one of these stages", "sent": "The hill’s <b>gradation</b> was so gradual that even those on crutches were able to enjoy the nature trail.\nThe short story’s language graded from the vernacular to the erudite so gradually that you practically didn’t realize until the end that the speaker had become educated almost before your eyes.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "A grade is a step, stage, or rank, as in grades on papers, school years (“sixth grade”), or “Grade A” milk.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "grades and steps in a gradual scale: a step-by-step progression.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "gregarious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "greh-GAIR-ee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Sociable, pertaining to a flock or crowd", "sent": "“We need to be a little more productive and a little less <b>gregarious</b>,” said the chemistry teacher when he saw that the two-person lab groups had devolved into clusters of five and six students standing around talking and laughing.", "related": "Genial, Congenial, Convivial are all words for sociable, friendly.", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse with garrulous, which means “talkative.” If it helps, think of a guy named Greg who’s the life of the party and a guy named Gary who never shuts up.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "greg (flock): loves the flock and the crowd, very sociable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "guile", "pos": "noun", "pron": "GUY-ull", "also": "guileless (adj)", "d1": "Clever deceit, cunning, craftiness", "sent": "The game of poker is all about <b>guile</b>, manipulating your own body language and patter to lead other players to erroneous conclusions about the cards you’re holding.", "related": "Duplicity (deceit, double-dealing, acting in two different ways for the purpose of deception), Wily (crafty, cunning)", "moreinfo": "Beguile can mean to trick or mislead, but can also mean to charm or bewitch—that is, to trick in a pleasant, sometimes flirtatious way.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "guile: sly cunning; a beguiling trickster's craft.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "hackneyed", "pos": "adj", "pron": "HACK-need", "also": "", "d1": "So commonplace as to be stale; not fresh or original", "sent": "This screenplay is so <b>hackneyed</b>—the leading lady has a quirky, artsy job in the city and has a minor problem early in the movie from which the male lead rescues her, and they get together but then break up due to a misunderstanding, and then they end up together anyway, all while the female lead’s “sassy” friend gives advice. Ugh. I’ll bet they’re auditioning Jennifer Aniston right now.", "related": "Banal, Hackneyed, Inane, Insipid and Trite all mean “lacking freshness and originality, shallow”", "moreinfo": "Hackney in London was once out in the countryside and was a place from which you could rent a horse, leading to the sense of an overworked, broken-down horse, and consequently to someone, such as a writer, who works for hire and has few fresh ideas left.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "hackneyed: a tired old \"hack\" cliche, worn stale.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "hardy", "pos": "adj", "pron": "HAR-dee", "also": "", "d1": "Bold, brave, capable of withstanding hardship, fatigue, cold, etc.", "sent": "While the entire family enjoyed the trip to South America, only the hardier members even attempted to hike to the top of Ecuador’s tallest volcano.", "related": "Robust (strong, vigorous, full-bodied), Hale (vigorous, healthy)", "moreinfo": "Hardy, unsurprisingly, comes from “harden,” in the sense of “make brave.” Hardy is also an English last name; the adventurous Hardy Boys are the heroes of a series of young adult novels popular since their debut in 1927.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "hardy: hard and hearty, withstands cold and hardship.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "haven", "pos": "noun", "pron": "HAY-ven", "also": "", "d1": "Harbor or port; refuge, safe place", "sent": "The relief workers set up the camp as a <b>haven</b> from persecution.", "related": "Succor (relief, aid), Asylum (refuge or sanctuary; refuge granted by a country to a foreigner persecuted in her or her own country). Asylum was once used to mean a care facility for the mentally ill, orphans, etc., but this usage is generally considered insensitive today.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "a safe haven: a harbor, a place of refuge.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "hearken", "pos": "verb", "pron": "HAR-ken", "also": "hark (verb)", "d1": "Listen, pay attention to", "sent": "“<b>Hearken</b>, students!” said the old-fashioned music teacher. “We are going to practice ‘Hark, the Herald Angels Sing.”\nThe simple lifestyle and anachronistic dress of the Amish <b>hearken</b> back to an earlier era.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Hark is a variant of hearken and has the same meaning. The expression hearken back or hark back is much more common in modern usage than hearken alone, and means to turn back to something earlier or return to a source.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "hearken = hear + ken: hear and heed, pay attention.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "hedonist", "pos": "noun", "pron": "HEE-dun-ist", "also": "hedonism (noun), hedonistic (adj)", "d1": "Person devoted to pleasure", "sent": "A vacation is a fine time to practice hedonism, letting your troubles go and pursuing massages and cocktails as though it’s your life mission.", "related": "Sybarite (synonym). Voluptuary is also very similar, but adds the idea of seeking luxury. Licentious (sexually unrestrained; immoral; ignoring the rules), Libertine (morally or sexually unrestrained person; freethinker)", "moreinfo": "The original Hedonists were Greek philosophers who held that pleasure is the highest moral good, so whatever policy causes the most pleasure (and least pain) to the most people is the most moral policy.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "a hedonist chases pleasure head-on, all fun and indulgence.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "heterogeneous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "het-er-oh-JEE-nee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Different in type, incongruous; composed of different types of elements", "sent": "Rather than build the wall with plain brick, we used a <b>heterogeneous</b> mixture of stones—they are not only different colors, but a variety of sizes as well.", "related": "Homogeneous (of the same kind) is the opposite of heterogeneous.", "moreinfo": "The Latin root “gen” means “birth, produce, race” and appears in generate, genus, gender, genocide. “Hetero” means “different” and appears in heterodox (unorthodox).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "hetero (different) + genos (kind): mixed, of different kinds.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "hierarchy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "HIGH-er-ar-kee", "also": "", "d1": "A ranked series; a classification of people according to rank, ability, etc.; a ruling body", "sent": "The activist, accustomed to groups ruled by consensus, was quite surprised to find that the Eco-Action Coalition was led by a strict <b>hierarchy</b>—members followed orders from district leaders, district leaders from regional leaders, and regional leaders from the national head.", "related": "Echelon (a level, rank or grade; the people at that level), Stratum (a layer, esp. one of a number of parallel layers, such as in sedimentary rock or the Earth’s atmosphere; plural is strata, as in “Of all the strata of society, the middle class is the stratum hit hardest by the recession.”)", "moreinfo": "A hierarchy was originally a ranked division of angels.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "a hierarchy is the ranked ladder, from top to bottom.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "hodgepodge", "pos": "noun", "pron": "HAHJ-pahj", "also": "", "d1": "Mixture of different kinds of things, jumble", "sent": "The comedian’s book wasn’t a proper memoir, but more a <b>hodgepodge</b> of old bits, personal stories that went nowhere, random political opinions, and childhood photos.", "related": "Heterogeneous means “made up of diverse elements.” Medley, Farrago, Potpourri, Pastiche and Olio are all words for mixtures of diverse things.", "moreinfo": "Hodgepodge comes from hotchpotch, a stew of meat and vegetables.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "hodgepodge: a random mishmash, a jumbled stew.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "homogeneous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "hoh-moh-JEE-nee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Of the same kind; uniform throughout", "sent": "While Sweden seems to have solved many of its social ills, critics point out that Sweden’s largely <b>homogeneous</b> population doesn’t present the challenges extant in a more diverse nation with many cultures and languages.", "related": "Heterogeneous (different in type, incongruous; composed of different types of elements) is the antonym of homogeneous.", "moreinfo": "The Latin root “gen” means “birth, produce, race” and appears in generate, genus, gender, genocide. “Homo” means “same” and appears in the biology term homologous (having the same relation or structure, as in human arms and bat wings).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "homo (same) + genos (kind): all the same kind, uniform.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "hyberbole", "pos": "noun", "pron": "high-PER-boh-lee", "also": "", "d1": "Deliberate exaggeration for effect", "sent": "Oh, come on. Saying “That movie was so bad it made me puke” was surely hyperbole. I strongly doubt that you actually vomited during or following The Back-Up Plan.", "related": "Overstatement is similar in meaning.", "moreinfo": "Hyperbole shares a root (meaning “throw”) with ballistics—think of it as throwing your statement quite a bit past the mark.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "hyperbole: hyper, over-the-top exaggeration for effect.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "idiosyncrasy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "id-dee-oh-SINK-rah-see", "also": "idiosyncratic (adj)", "d1": "Characteristic or habit peculiar to an individual; peculiar quality, quirk", "sent": "Sometimes, the richer people get, the more idiosyncratic they become. After he made his first billion, he began traveling with a pet iguana, sleeping in an oxygen chamber, and, oddly, speaking with a slight Dutch accent.", "related": "Eccentric (peculiar, odd, deviating from the norm esp. in a whimsical way)", "moreinfo": "The root “idio” means “referring to one person, peculiar” (an idiolect is a speech pattern spoken by only one person).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "your own quirky \"idiot-crasy\": a habit peculiar to you.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "illiberality", "pos": "noun", "pron": "il-lib-er-AL-ih-tee", "also": "illiberal (adj)", "d1": "Narrow-mindedness, bigotry; strictness or lack of generosity", "sent": "Students protested the <b>illiberality</b> of an admissions policy that made no allowances for those from disadvantaged areas or backgrounds who may not have had access to advanced classes and tutors.", "related": "Chauvinism (fanatical patriotism or blind enthusiasm for military glory; undue or biased devotion to any group, cause, etc.), Bigot (obstinately prejudiced person), Xenophobia (fear of foreigners), Jingoism (extreme chauvinism plus warlike foreign policy), Insular (pertaining to an island; isolated; illiberal)", "moreinfo": "Illiberal can also mean lacking a liberal arts education (as a person), or not requiring such an education (as a profession).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "il (not) + liberal: not open or generous, narrow-minded.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "imminent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "IMM-in-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Ready to occur, impending", "sent": "In the face of <b>imminent</b> war, the nation looked to FDR for reassurance.\nEveryone was excited and nervous; Madonna’s arrival was <b>imminent</b>!", "related": "Imminent can carry the sense of something bad on the horizon, but can also just mean “about to happen.” Ominous means “giving a bad sign about the future.” Portentous can mean “ominous” or simply “very significant, exciting wonder and awe.”", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse imminent with eminent, which means “highly respected.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "imminent \"in a minute\": about to happen, impending.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "impair", "pos": "verb", "pron": "im-PAIR", "also": "", "d1": "Make worse, weaken", "sent": "Playing in a rock band without earplugs will almost certainly <b>impair</b> your hearing over time.", "related": "Exacerbate and Aggravate (make worse or irritate), Mar (damage, spoil, deface), Vitiate (ruin, corrupt)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "impaired driving: damaged, weakened, made worse.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "impartial", "pos": "adj", "pron": "im-PAR-shull", "also": "", "d1": "Unbiased, fair", "sent": "Judge Gonzales removed himself from the case because, having a personal connection to the school where the shooting took place, he did not think he could be appropriately <b>impartial</b>.", "related": "Disinterested, Dispassionate, and Nonpartisan are all related to being fair and not having a bias or personal stake.", "moreinfo": "The root for “part” appears in partisan, partial, and party—just as a member of a political party is rooting for his own side, someone who is partial is on the side of only part of the group, not everyone equally.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "im (not) + partial: taking no side or part, fair.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "impede", "pos": "verb", "pron": "im-PEED", "also": "", "d1": "Hold back, obstruct the progress of", "sent": "I didn’t realize business school would be entirely group work—sadly, there’s always at least one person in every group who <b>impedes</b> the group’s progress more than helps it.", "related": "Hinder and Hamper are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "Impede contains the root “ped” (feet), also occurring in pedestrian, pedal. Impede thus has the sense of shackling the feet, preventing movement.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "im + pede (foot): shackle the feet, obstruct (think impediment).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "implication", "pos": "noun", "pron": "im-plick-CAY-shun", "also": "", "d1": "Act of implying or that which is implied; close connection, esp. in an incriminating way", "sent": "When the boss said, “Times are tight around here, I just think you should know,” the <b>implication</b> was that maybe we should start looking for new jobs.\nShe implicated her boyfriend in the robbery after less than 20 minutes of interrogation.", "related": "Implicit and Tacit (implied, unspoken)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "what is implied; or being tied into a crime.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "implicit", "pos": "adj", "pron": "im-PLISS-it", "also": "", "d1": "Implied, not stated directly; involved in the very essence of something, unquestionable", "sent": "He didn’t have to be told to resign; it was <b>implicit</b> in his not getting the promotion that he had no future at the company.\nI enjoy ice climbing with my father because, in such a dangerous situation, it’s important to have a partner you trust <b>implicitly</b>.", "related": "Tacit also means implied, unspoken.", "moreinfo": "The antonym of implicit is explicit (direct, clear, fully revealed, or clearly depicting sex or nudity).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "implicit: folded in and understood without being said.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "implode", "pos": "verb", "pron": "im-PLOHD", "also": "", "d1": "Burst inward", "sent": "The startup struggled for years before it simply <b>imploded</b>—the management team broke into factions, all the clients were scared off, and employees who hadn’t been paid in weeks began taking the office computers home with them in retribution.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Implode is, of course, the opposite of explode.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "im (in) + plode: it bursts inward (opposite of explode).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "inadvertent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-ad-VER-tent", "also": "inadvertently (adv)", "d1": "Unintentional; characterized by a lack of attention, careless", "sent": "In attempting to perfect his science project, he <b>inadvertently</b> blew a fuse and plunged his family’s home into darkness.", "related": "Fortuitous (happening by chance; lucky), Fluke (stroke of luck, something accidentally successful)", "moreinfo": "To advert is to turn attention to (as in an advertisement). Thus, inadvertent indicates a lack of attention paid.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "in (not) + advert (attend): not paying attention, accidental.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "inasmuch", "pos": "adv", "pron": "in-az-MUCH", "also": "", "d1": "In like manner, considering that (contraction of “in as much,” generally followed by “as”)", "sent": "<b>Inasmuch</b> as you missed my birthday party to do the Walk for the Cure, I am not angry at all. Good for you for doing that!\nNormally, a student would graduate in four years, but <b>inasmuch</b> as you failed several courses in your first two semesters, that will now be impossible for you.", "related": "Whereas (while on the contrary, considering that)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "inasmuch = \"in as much as\": seeing that, considering that.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "incendiary", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-SEND-ee-air-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Setting on fire, pertaining to arson; arousing strife, rebellion, etc.; “inflaming” the senses", "sent": "The college suspended him for creating an <b>incendiary</b> website, encouraging sit-ins and protests, but the ACLU defended his right to freedom of speech.\nIt is illegal to bring incendiaries on a plane—there are many good reasons why you may not have a stick of dynamite in your carry-on.", "related": "Kindle (ignite, cause to begin burning; incite, arouse, inflame), Rouse or Foment (incite, stir up)", "moreinfo": "The root “candere” (“to shine”) also appears in candle, incense, and incensed (furious, “on fire” with anger).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 10", "setNum": 10, "hook": "an incendiary bomb starts fires; it also inflames tensions.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "incentive", "pos": "noun", "pron": "in-SENT-iv", "also": "incentivize (verb)", "d1": "Something that encourages greater action or effort, such as a reward", "sent": "A controversial program in a failing school system uses cash payments as an <b>incentive</b> for students to stay in school.", "related": "Inducement (motive for action, incentive)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "an incentive incites you to act, a carrot dangled ahead.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "inchoate", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-COH-it", "also": "", "d1": "Just begun, undeveloped, unorganized", "sent": "The first few weeks of language class went well, but her <b>inchoate</b> French was all but useless when she found herself at an academic conference in Quebec.", "related": "Nascent and Incipient mean “just beginning to exist, or in a very early stage of development.” Inchoate has more of a sense of vagueness. An inchoate idea for a novel probably means you don’t know where to start writing; a nascent project, although just beginning, might be right on track.", "moreinfo": "Inchoate comes from the Latin “cohum,” a strap attached to an ox’s yoke; the sense here is “to begin work.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "inchoate: at \"inch one,\" barely begun and unformed.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "incipient", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-SIP-ee-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Just beginning; in a very early stage", "sent": "The movie producer was devastated when, due to legal trouble over the screenplay, the <b>incipient</b> project was crushed before it had even begun shooting.", "related": "Nascent (synonym), Inchoate (just begun, undeveloped, unorganized)", "moreinfo": "The “in” here means “on,” and the remainder of the word shares a root with capable.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "incipient: at the inception, in the earliest stage.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "incongruous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-CON-groo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Out of place, inappropriate, not harmonious", "sent": "Among the student artwork posted in the halls, Angelina’s submission was <b>incongruous</b>, a dark, gruesome, and even worldly work amidst the happy family portraits and other childish drawings.", "related": "Heterogeneous (different in type, incongruous), Conspicuous (standing out)", "moreinfo": "Incongruous is, of course, related to congruent, as in “congruent triangles” (those that are identical).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "in (not) + congruous: it does not match, out of place.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "inconsequential", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-con-seck-WENCH-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Insignificant, unimportant; illogical", "sent": "You wrote a bestselling book and got a stellar review in the New York Times—whatever your cousin has to say about it is simply <b>inconsequential</b>.\nGiven that your entire essay is about Hamlet’s relationship with his mother, your thesis that Hamlet’s relationship with Laertes drives the plot is <b>inconsequential</b> —that is, it does not follow as a consequence of the evidence you’ve provided.", "related": "Negligible, Null, and Nil mean “nothing, or too insignificant to matter.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "in (not) + consequential: of no consequence, unimportant.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "incorporate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "in-CORE-per-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Combine, unite; form a legal corporation; embody, give physical form to", "sent": "When a business <b>incorporates</b>, it becomes a separate legal entity—for instance, the business can declare bankruptcy without the owners doing so.\nLocal legend has it that ghosts can <b>incorporate</b> on one night of the year and walk among the living.", "related": "Incarnate (embodied, personified; put into concrete form, embody, take on a human body)", "moreinfo": "Incorporate contains the Latin “corpus” (body), also found in corpse (dead body), corpulent (fat), and even “Marine Corps.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "in + corpor (body): bring it into the body, combine it in.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "indeterminate", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-dee-TER-min-it", "also": "", "d1": "Not fixed or determined, indefinite; vague", "sent": "The results of the drug trial were <b>indeterminate</b>; further trials will be needed to ascertain whether the drug can be released.\nThe lottery can have an <b>indeterminate</b> number of winners—the prize is simply divided among them.", "related": "Ambiguous (not clear, hard to understand, open to having several meanings or interpretations)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "in (not) + determinate: not fixed or settled, vague.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "indifferent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-DIFF-er-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Not caring, having no interest; unbiased, impartial", "sent": "Do whatever you want—I’m <b>indifferent</b>. I won’t even notice.", "related": "Apathy (not caring; absence of feeling; lack of interest or concern)", "moreinfo": "The presence of “different” in indifferent comes from the sense of “not differing, neither good nor bad.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "it makes no difference to me: not caring, unbiased.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "inform", "pos": "verb", "pron": "in-FORM", "also": "", "d1": "Inspire, animate; give substance, essence, or context to; be the characteristic quality of", "sent": "Her work as an art historian is <b>informed</b> by a background in drama; where others see a static tableau, she sees a protagonist, a conflict, a denouement.\nMarjorie’s desire to work in forensics is <b>informed</b> by a family history in the police department.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Of course inform most commonly means “impart knowledge to”; thus, many students are confused when they see the word used in other ways on the GRE.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "inform = give inner form or essence to; shape and pervade it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ingenuous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-JEN-yoo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Genuine, sincere, not holding back; naive", "sent": "Multi-level marketing scams prey on the <b>ingenuous</b>, those who really think there’s someone out there who just wants to help them get rich.", "related": "Guileless and Artless are near-synonyns. An Ingenue is a young—presumably innocent—actress or other female performer.", "moreinfo": "The “gen” in ingenuous is the same root as in genuine, and the two words are very similar in meaning. Don’t misread ingenuous as ingenious, which means “brilliant” (an ingenious idea).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "ingenuous: genuine and innocent, no guile (not \"ingenious\").", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ingrained", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-GRAY-nd", "also": "", "d1": "Deep-rooted, forming part of the very essence; worked into the fiber", "sent": "Religious observance had been <b>ingrained</b> in him since birth; he could not remember a time when he didn’t pray five times a day.", "related": "Inculcate (teach persistently, implant [an idea] in a person)", "moreinfo": "The “grain” root is related to using plant parts for dye; something ingrained is suffused into the very fiber, like dye.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "worked into the grain of the wood: deep-rooted.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "inherent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-HAIR-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Existing as a permanent, essential quality; intrinsic", "sent": "New research seems to support the idea that humans have an <b>inherent</b> sense of justice—even babies become upset at puppet shows depicting unfairness, and are gratified at seeing the “bad” puppets punished.", "related": "Innate (inborn)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "inherent: inherited within, an essential built-in quality.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "innocuous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-NOCK-yoo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Harmless, inoffensive", "sent": "While it’s quite acrid in here, fortunately the fumes that come from our factory are completely <b>innocuous</b>—you don’t need a face mask unless you’d like one.", "related": "Benign (harmless, favorable)", "moreinfo": "The rare word nocuous means “harmful.” Innocuous can also have the sense of “boring, insignificant,” as in something so harmless as to lack interest.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "in (not) + nocuous (harmful): it cannot hurt you, harmless.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "intelligible", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-TELL-ih-juh-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Able to be understood, clear", "sent": "You are doing a disservice to all music by listening through those horrible speakers! None of the lyrics are even <b>intelligible</b>! I’ll bet you have no idea what this song is even about!", "related": "Legible applies to handwriting and means “able to be read.” Intelligible can be used for speech, writing, or ideas. Lucid means clear and applies to people as well as ideas. As in, “After brain surgery, it will take awhile for the anesthesia to wear off and for her to become lucid. Then we can test whether her handwriting is intelligible, which will help pinpoint any loss of functioning.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "your intellect can grasp it: understandable, clear.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "intractable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-TRACT-uh-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Difficult to control, manage, or manipulate; hard to cure; stubborn", "sent": "That student is positively <b>intractable</b>! Last week, we talked about the importance of staying in your seat during the lesson—this week, she not only got up mid-class, but she actually scrambled on top of a bookcase and refused to come down!\nBack injuries often result in <b>intractable</b> pain; despite treatment, patients never feel fully cured.", "related": "Intransigent, Obdurate, and Obstreperous are also used to describe people who are stubborn and hard to control.", "moreinfo": "The antonym of intractable is tractable, meaning “compliant.” The root “tract” means “manage or handle” (and originally “drag about”) and also appears in tractor, distract, retract, and, of course, tract (a stretch of land).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "in (not) + tractable: cannot be led or managed, stubborn.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "intrepid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-TREPP-id", "also": "", "d1": "Fearless, brave, enduring in the face of adversity", "sent": "<b>Intrepid</b> explorers Lewis and Clark led the first U.S. expedition to the West Coast, facing bitter winters and rough terrain.", "related": "Hardy (bold, brave, capable of withstanding hardship, fatigue, cold, etc.), Fortitude (strength in facing adversity), Grit (firmness of mind, esp. during hardship; courage)", "moreinfo": "The antonym trepid means frightened and trepidation means fear or alarm. The USS Intrepid is a decommissioned Naval aircraft carrier docked in NYC as a museum. As a rule, if it’s the name of a ship, it’s probably something good (even the Titanic was meant to be positive—“titanic” means very large).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "in (not) + trepid (trembling): not trembling, fearless and bold.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "intrinsic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "in-TRIN-zick", "also": "", "d1": "Belonging to the essential nature of a thing", "sent": "Despite all this high-tech safety equipment, skydiving is an <b>intrinsically</b> dangerous proposition.\nCommunication is <b>intrinsic</b> to a healthy relationship.", "related": "Innate (inborn), Inherent (existing as a permanent, essential quality; intrinsic)", "moreinfo": "The antonym extrinsic means “not forming part of a thing, extraneous, coming from the outside.” Intrinsic motivation is wanting to do something due to interest in the task itself; extrinsic motivation is wanting to do something for a reward, to earn a grade, to avoid punishment, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "intrinsic: the built-in essence, part of the thing itself.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "jargon", "pos": "noun", "pron": "JAR-gun", "also": "", "d1": "Vocabulary specific to a group or occupation; convoluted or unintelligible language", "sent": "The information my doctor gave me was so full of medical <b>jargon</b> I couldn’t understand it at all! I’m going to look on the Internet for something written for regular people.", "related": "Argot and Cant also refer to specialized vocabularies (more often used to describe the underworld—lawyers have jargon, mobsters have argot or cant.) Cant can also mean lofty but insincere talk (e.g., pageant queens talking about world peace). A lexicon is a dictionary or specialized vocabulary.", "moreinfo": "Jargon comes from a Latin root for “chatter” that also gives us gargle and gargoyle. Vague business-talk (“It is a best practice to synergize our efficiencies”) is often called jargon.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "jargon: insider lingo that sounds like an unintelligible jumble.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "jocular", "pos": "adj", "pron": "JOCK-yoo-ler", "also": "", "d1": "Joking or given to joking all the time; jolly, playful", "sent": "He’s certainly a <b>jocular</b> fellow—if we were friends, I’m sure I’d find his antics amusing, but as his professor, I do wish he’d contribute some serious comments to the class discussion instead of his constant stream of <b>jocular</b> comments.", "related": "Jocund and jocose come from the same root and mean the same thing. Waggish (merry, roguish), Risible (laughable, related to laughing), Droll (funny in an odd way)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "jocular: full of jokes, jolly (think \"joker\").", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "judicious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "joo-DISH-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Using good judgment; wise, sensible", "sent": "In his will, the old titan of industry left little to his hard-partying younger son, and left the bulk of his estate to the more <b>judicious</b> older son, with instructions that the older son see that the rest of the family was taken care of.", "related": "Prudent (wise, careful in providing for the future), Circumspect (cautious, careful to consider the circumstances and consequences)", "moreinfo": "The related judicial means “relating to judges, courtrooms, justice, etc.” While a judicious person is wise, a judicial attitude would probably be cold, impartial, detached.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "judicious: like a good judge, wise and sensible.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "juncture", "pos": "noun", "pron": "JUNK-tcher", "also": "", "d1": "Critical point in time, such as a crisis or a time when a decision is necessary; a place where two things are joined together", "sent": "We are at a critical <b>juncture</b> in the history of this organization: either we can remain a nonprofit, or we can register as a political action committee and try to expand our influence.\nThe little canoe started to sink when it split at the <b>juncture</b> between the old wood and the new material used to repair it.", "related": "Dovetail (join or fit together), Diverge (differ, deviate; branch off or turn aside, as from a path)", "moreinfo": "Junction is the act of joining, or a place where two things meet, esp. railroad lines or roads. A juncture is more the actual line where two things join, and a junction is the place where they come together. Junction often occurs in place names.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "at this juncture: a critical junction or turning point in time.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "keen", "pos": "adj", "pron": "KEEN", "also": "", "d1": "Sharp, piercing; very perceptive or mentally sharp; intense (of a feeling)", "sent": "Dogs have a <b>keen</b> sense of smell.\nAs homecoming queen, she had experienced the envy of others, but their jealousy only grew more <b>keen</b> when she was selected for a small role in a movie.", "related": "Acumen (keen, quick, accurate insight or judgment), Astute (shrewd, very perceptive), Perspicacity (acuteness of perception)", "moreinfo": "Keen comes from the same root as “can” (to be able)—if there’s a job to do, a keen person can probably get it done!", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "a keen blade or a keen mind: sharp and perceptive.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "kudos", "pos": "noun", "pron": "KOO-dohss", "also": "", "d1": "Praise, honor, congratulations", "sent": "“<b>Kudos</b> on your amazing GRE score!” said the teacher.\nWhile the critics weren’t impressed, the play received plentiful <b>kudos</b> from the audience.", "related": "Plaudits (applause, approval), Laudation (praise), Panegyric (formal, lofty, or elaborate praise), Paean (song of praise, triumph, or thanks)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "kudos to you: praise, honor, congratulations.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "lackluster", "pos": "adj", "pron": "LACK-luss-ter", "also": "", "d1": "Not shiny; dull, mediocre; lacking brilliance or vitality", "sent": "Many young people today are so accustomed to being praised by parents and adults that they are shocked when a <b>lackluster</b> effort in the workplace receives the indifference or mild disapproval it deserves.", "related": "Pedestrian and prosaic mean commonplace, dull, or lacking imagination. Quotidian means daily or commonplace, as daily things tend to be! Middling comes from the idea of being in the “middle” and means medium, average, or mediocre.", "moreinfo": "This is an easy word—something lackluster literally lacks luster. Lustrous means shining (This shampoo will add luster to your hair!).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "lack + luster: no shine, dull and mediocre.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "laconic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "luh-CON-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Using few words, concise", "sent": "The boss was famously <b>laconic</b>; after allowing his employees to present their new plan for an entire hour, he finally responded, “Confirmed.”", "related": "Reticent and Taciturn (not talking much) are often used to describe shy people and do not have the sense of “getting the point across efficiently” that laconic does. Pithy, however, takes this idea even further—it means getting the point across in just a few, cleverly-chosen words.", "moreinfo": "Laconic comes from the Greek place named Laconia, the region in which Sparta (which of course gives us spartan) was located. A famous story has an invading general threatening, “If I enter Laconia, I will raze Sparta to the ground.” The Spartans laconically replied, “If.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 11", "setNum": 11, "hook": "laconic \"lack-onic\": lacking words, terse and concise.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "lament", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "luh-MENT", "also": "lamentable (adj)", "d1": "Mourn; express grief, sorrow, or regret (verb); an expression of grief, esp. as a song or poem (noun)", "sent": "Silda said she couldn’t make it to the party—she’s still <b>lamenting</b> the death of her cat. In fact, she wrote a poem: “A <b>Lament</b> On the Topic of Buttons McFlufferton.” <b>Lamentably</b>, Silda is a very bad poet.", "related": "To bewail is to lament, while to bemoan can be to express grief or simply disapproval (to bemoan one’s fate). The two words, of course, come from wail and moan. Lachrymose means tearful or mournful. A person might get lachrymose over a lamentable situation.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "a lament: a wail of grief, to mourn.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "lampoon", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "lam-POON", "also": "", "d1": "A harsh satire (noun); ridicule or satirize (verb)", "sent": "“As a Democrat,” said Bob, “I can’t say I appreciated watching that comedian in the Obama mask <b>lampoon</b> the State of the Union address.”", "related": "To satirize is to use ridicule, sarcasm, etc. as a form of criticism. Parody is similar but often not as harsh—the key difference is that a parody is actually a copy (the Scary Movie series parodies the horror genre).", "moreinfo": "Lampoon comes from a French word for to lap up—as in, to guzzle beer, which is what you’d probably be doing while watching a lampoon.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "lampoon: harpoon them with mockery, a biting satire.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "landmark", "pos": "noun, adj", "pron": "LAND-mark", "also": "", "d1": "Object (such as a building) that stands out and can be used to navigate by; a very important place, event, etc.", "sent": "The Civil Rights Act of 1964 was a <b>landmark</b> in the battle for equality.\nIn Lebanon, many roads are unmarked, and people navigate by <b>landmarks</b>—for instance, “third house down from the water tower.”", "related": "A watershed is literally a division between two water drainage areas; a watershed or watershed moment is the point of a very important decision or shift.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "a landmark you navigate by; also a milestone event.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "languid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "LANG-wid", "also": "languor (noun)", "d1": "Drooping from exhaustion, sluggish, slow; lacking in spirit", "sent": "We signed up for a fitness boot camp, but after a single hour of exercise in the heat, we all felt so overcome with languor that we refused to go on. Turns out the reason we need a fitness boot camp in the first place is that we’re pretty <b>languid</b> people.", "related": "Torpid and slothful mean slow and lazy. Indolent means habitually lazy, such as a person who chooses never to work. Lassitude is weariness or lack of caring.", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse languor with langur, a type of monkey. Languorous is a more pleasant version of languid, suggesting dreamy relaxation.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "languid, languishing: droopy, sluggish, slow.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "lassitude", "pos": "noun", "pron": "LASS-it-tood", "also": "", "d1": "Tiredness, weariness; lazy indifference", "sent": "It’s so difficult to get anything done in the dead heat of August! I can’t seem to shake my <b>lassitude</b> enough to get out of this hammock, much less study for the GRE.", "related": "Languid (drooping from exhaustion, sluggish, slow; lacking in spirit). Torpid and slothful mean slow and lazy. Indolent means habitually lazy, such as a person who chooses never to work.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "lassitude: a \"lazy attitude,\" weariness and listlessness.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "laudable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "LAW-dib-bull", "also": "laud (verb)", "d1": "worthy of praise", "sent": "When a major discount mart fired several employees for subduing a gunman, most people considered the action a <b>laudable</b> act of heroism, but the discount chain fired the employees for “violating company policies.” Nevertheless, the mayor lauded the former employees in a medal- granting ceremony.", "related": "Encomium (warm, glowing praise, esp. a formal expression of praise), Panegyric (formal, lofty, or elaborate praise), Paean (song of praise, triumph, or thanks)", "moreinfo": "Laud shares a root with applaud and plaudits (applause, approval).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "laudable: worthy of applause and laud (praise).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "lavish", "pos": "adj, verb", "pron": "LAVV-ish", "also": "", "d1": "Abundant or giving in abundance; marked by excess (adj); give very generously (verb)", "sent": "Anita wanted to live as she imagined Beyonce lived, and ran up huge credit card bills pursuing a <b>lavish</b> lifestyle she could scarcely afford.\nAlthough her rich banker boyfriend <b>lavished</b> gifts on her, she didn’t want to be with someone she didn’t really love.", "related": "Posh (luxurious, elegant), Tony (aristocratic)", "moreinfo": "From Middle French lavasse, a downpour of rain. To lavish someone with gifts is to “rain down” upon that person with gifts.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "lavish: pour it on extravagantly, or give very generously.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "layperson", "pos": "noun", "pron": "LAY-per-son", "also": "laity (noun), lay (adj)", "d1": "a person who is not a member of the clergy or not a member of a particular profession (such as medicine, law, etc.)", "sent": "The actress Jenny McCarthy has written a book about autism. While her experience as a parent is interesting to anyone in a similar situation, it’s still important to remember that McCarthy is a <b>layperson</b>, not a doctor.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "A layperson is literally a member of a church who is not a minister or priest. The laity is the church’s membership (not including the clergy), or a group of people outside a particular profession. Lay can be an adjective meaning “pertaining to regular people,” as in a lay version of a medical text.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "a layperson is a \"lay,\" non-clergy, non-expert person.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "levity", "pos": "noun", "pron": "LEVV-it-tee", "also": "", "d1": "Lightness (of mind, spirit, or mood) or lack of seriousness, sometimes in an inappropriate way", "sent": "My late uncle Bill loved practical jokes and absolutely would have approved of the iPod mix my aunt played at the wake, which added a little <b>levity</b> by segueing from “Amazing Grace” to the party anthem “Let’s Get It Started.”", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Levity comes from a Latin word for “lightness,” which also gives us elevator, lever, leaven (to cause bread to rise) and levitate (to cause to float, such as in a magic show).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "levity, from levitate: lightness of mood, lack of seriousness.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "levy", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "LEVV-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Collect tax from, wage war on, or enlist for military service; (verb); act of colleting tax or amount owed, or the drafting of troops into military service (noun)", "sent": "When England levied yet another tax on the colonists, the colonists were pushed one further step towards <b>levying</b> war. Soon, the worried British began to <b>levy</b> troops.", "related": "Do not confuse levy with levee (a slope or wall along a waterway, designed to stop flooding).", "moreinfo": "Levy has many definitions that seem somewhat unrelated; however, the root means “raise” or “lightness” (as in lever, elevate), so you can think of raising money through taxes, raising an army by drafting soldiers, or using that army to wage war.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "levy a tax: raise and collect money from people (root lev = raise).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "liberal", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "LIBB-er-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Favorable to progress or reform; believing in maximum possible individual freedom; tolerant, open-minded; generous (adj); a person with such beliefs or practices (noun)", "sent": "Split pea soup benefits from a <b>liberal</b> application of pepper.\n<b>Liberal</b> reformers in Egypt pushed for freedom of speech, freedom of the press, and freedom of assembly.", "related": "“Liberal” in modern American politics isn’t quite the same as the dictionary definition. For instance, liberal Democrats tend to favor social programs that require a larger government to administer, while some conservatives say that liberalism means having the smallest government possible in order to maximize freedom. For the GRE, liberal means generous, freely- flowing, open-minded, or pertaining to values like freedom of speech.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "liberal, for liberty: open-minded, tolerant, and generous.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "libertine", "pos": "noun", "pron": "LIBB-er-teen", "also": "", "d1": "Morally or sexually unrestrained person; freethinker (regarding religion)", "sent": "A famed <b>libertine</b>, the sitcom star was constantly in the news for cavorting with women of dubious occupations and overdosing on drugs often enough to regularly hold up production of his popular television show.", "related": "Hedonists, sybarites, and voluptuaries are people devoted to pleasure. Debauchery is excessive indulgence in sensual pleasures.", "moreinfo": "Libertine certainly is related to “liberty”—think of a libertine as someone who has a little too much liberty. You can also use libertine as an adjective (as in libertine attitudes).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "a libertine takes \"liberty\" with all morals, unrestrained.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "likewise", "pos": "adverb", "pron": "LIKE-wize", "also": "", "d1": "Also, in addition to; similarly, in the same way", "sent": "Chip was baffled by all the silverware set before him, so when his host began eating salad with the smallest, leftmost fork, Chip did <b>likewise</b>.", "related": "Moreover (in addition to what has been said, for instance “His actions cost us the job; moreover, he seriously offended our client).”", "moreinfo": "In conversation, likewise can mean “Me, too.” When someone says “Nice to meet you,” you could say, “Likewise.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "likewise: in like manner, similarly, same to you.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "log", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "LAHG", "also": "", "d1": "Keep a record of, write down; travel for or at a certain distance or speed (verb); a written record (noun)", "sent": "Lawyers who bill by the hour have to be sure to <b>log</b> all the time they spend on every client’s case.\nYou cannot get your pilot’s license until you have <b>logged</b> 40 hours of flight time.", "related": "Chronicle (a historical account in time order)", "moreinfo": "Of course, a log is a piece of a tree, and to log can also mean to cut down trees (what loggers do).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "log it: write it down in the logbook, keep a record.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "loquacious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "loh-KWAY-shuss", "also": "", "d1": "Talkative, wordy", "sent": "The <b>loquacious</b> professor spoke at a million miles an hour and still regularly talked past the scheduled end time of the class.", "related": "Prolix and Verbose are synonyms. Magniloquent and Grandiloquent mean speaking in a lofty, grandiose style.", "moreinfo": "The root “loq” means “speech” and also appears in eloquent and interlocutor (participant in a dialogue; interrogator).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "loqu (speak) + acious: talks and talks, very wordy.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "lucid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "LOO-sid", "also": "", "d1": "Clear, easy to understand; rational, sane", "sent": "After surgery, it’ll take at least an hour until she’s <b>lucid</b>—it’s nothing to worry about, but patients sometimes talk complete nonsense until the anesthesia wears off and their speech becomes <b>lucid</b>.", "related": "Lucid can mean literally clear, but often means metaphorically clear or clearheaded —a lucid argument, a lucid person. The similar pellucid usually means literally transparent, as in pellucid waters.", "moreinfo": "The root “luc” for “light” also occurs in translucent, luminous, luminary, lucubrate (work or study, originally by candlelight), and elucidate (make clear, explain).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "lucid, luminous: clear and easy to follow (a lucid dream).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "lull", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "LULL", "also": "", "d1": "Soothe or cause to fall asleep (as in a lullaby); quiet down; make to feel secure, sometimes falsely (verb); a period of calm or quiet (noun)", "sent": "Tisha always tried to be polite—if she had to slip out of class to use the bathroom, she waited for a <b>lull</b> in the action so as not to attract too much attention.\nGrandma’s singing <b>lulled</b> the baby to sleep—much as, in her work as a spy during World War II, Grandma used her beauty and charm to <b>lull</b> foreign dignitaries into giving up their secrets.", "related": "Slack usually means loose, negligent, or lazy, but a slack can also be a calm spot in the water or a period of quiet or suspended activity. The holidays represent a lull or slack in work at many companies.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "a lullaby lulls you calm; a lull is a quiet stretch.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "makeshift", "pos": "noun, adj", "pron": "MAKE-shift", "also": "", "d1": "A temporary, often improvised, substitute (noun); improvised for temporary use (adj)", "sent": "Lost in the woods for over 24 hours, the children were eventually found sleeping under a <b>makeshift</b> tent made from branches and old plastic bags.\nI don’t have a ladder, but I can stack up some boxes as a <b>makeshift</b>.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The expressions “thrown together” or “slapped together” express a similar idea of “making do” with the resources on hand. To “jury rig” something is to assemble it quickly with whatever materials you have available.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "make + shift: thrown together to make do for now, temporary.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "malleable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "MAL-ee-uh-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Able to be bent, shaped, or adapted", "sent": "The more <b>malleable</b> the material, the easier it is to bend into jewelry—and the easier it is to damage that jewelry.\nMy mother is a little too <b>malleable</b>—she said she liked all the things her first husband liked, and now she says she likes all the things her second husband likes.", "related": "Tractable, pliable, and plastic can also mean physically bendable, or metaphorically bendable, as in “easily influenced or shaped by others.” Mutable means changeable.", "moreinfo": "Malleable comes from a Latin word for “hammer” and thus shares a root with mallet.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "malleable metal takes a mallet's shape: bendable, adaptable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "maverick", "pos": "noun", "pron": "MAV-er-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Rebel, individualist, dissenter", "sent": "Most cop movies feature heroes who are <b>maverick</b> police officers, breaking all the rules, blowing things up, and getting their guns confiscated by the chief—but ultimately saving the day.", "related": "“Lone wolf” and “loose cannon” are expressions with similar meanings (although “loose cannon” implies that the person might do something crazy or dangerous).", "moreinfo": "Samuel Maverick was an American pioneer who didn’t brand (that is, show ownership by marking with a hot iron) his calves. In ranching lingo, a maverick is still an unbranded animal. Just as a maverick calf doesn’t bear a mark of ownership, a political maverick doesn’t belong to any party.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "a maverick brands no cattle: an independent rebel, a lone wolf.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "mendacious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "men-DAY-shuss", "also": "mendacity (noun)", "d1": "Lying, habitually dishonest", "sent": "She was so <b>mendacious</b> that, when she broke the television, she blamed it on her little brother, even though he was in a wheelchair and could hardly have tipped over a piece of furniture. Her mendacity knows no bounds!", "related": "Disingenuous (insincere, not genuine), Dissembling (misleading, concealing the truth, acting hypocritically), Prevaricating (misleading or lying)", "moreinfo": "Mendacious comes from a root for “defect, fault” that also gives us mend and amendment, which relate to fixing faults.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "mendacious \"amends the truth\" with lies: habitually dishonest.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "mercurial", "pos": "adj", "pron": "mer-CURE-ee-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Quickly and unpredictably changing moods; fickle, flighty", "sent": "It’s tough being married to someone so <b>mercurial</b>. I do pretty much the same thing every day— some days, she thinks I’m great, and other days, the exact same behaviors make her inexplicably angry.", "related": "Erratic (inconsistent, wandering, having no fixed course)", "moreinfo": "Mercurial comes from the idea that people born under the planet Mercury are quick but volatile.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "mercurial like quicksilver Mercury: darting, moody, fickle.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "metamorphosis", "pos": "noun", "pron": "met-uh-MORE-fuh-siss", "also": "metamorphose (verb)", "d1": "A complete change or transformation (in biology, a change such as a caterpillar becoming a pupa and then a butterfly)", "sent": "Many reality show competitions are based on the idea of a contestant undergoing a <b>metamorphosis</b>, such as through dieting, exercise, life coaching, rehab, or plastic surgery—or all of the above.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "“Meta” means “change” and “morph” means “shape” (morphology is the branch of biology dealing with form and structure of organisms). When people use “morph” as a short form of metamorphose (“Mighty Morphin Power Rangers”), they have actually used the wrong part of the word; the television heroes should be the Mighty Meta Power Rangers.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "meta (change) + morph (form): caterpillar into butterfly.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "meticulous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "meh-TICK-yoo-luss", "also": "", "d1": "Taking extreme care in regards to details; precise, fussy", "sent": "As a surgeon, of course Mom needs to be highly <b>meticulous</b> in her work—she gets things right down to a fraction of a millimeter. But this also means that when it’s her night to cook, she’s sometimes a little too <b>meticulous</b>—no one really needs a meatloaf to be sculpted into an absolutely perfect rectangular prism.", "related": "Finicky or Fastidious (excessively particular, hard to please), Exacting (very severe in making demands; requiring precise attention)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "meticulous: fussing carefully over every tiny detail.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "mitigate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "MITT-igg-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Make less severe; lessen or moderate (damage, grief, pain, etc.)", "sent": "Sadly, his illness could not be cured, but the nurses made every effort to <b>mitigate</b> the symptoms.", "related": "Ameliorate, Palliate, and Alleviate are near-synonyms, all of which could take the place of mitigate in the sentence above.", "moreinfo": "Exacerbate and aggravate are antonyms, referring to making something worse.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 12", "setNum": 12, "hook": "\"might-gate\": shut the gate on the harm, make it less severe.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "modest", "pos": "adj", "pron": "MAH-dest", "also": "", "d1": "Humble; simple rather than showy; decent (esp. “covering up” in terms of dress); small, limited", "sent": "The reporter was surprised that the celebrity lived in such a <b>modest</b> house, one that looked just like every other plain, two-story house on the block.\nHer first job out of college was a rude awakening—her <b>modest</b> salary was barely enough for rent, much less going out and having fun.", "related": "Paltry (extremely small or worthless, insultingly small—in terms of money, often “a paltry sum”)", "moreinfo": "Many students know modest as in “a modest outfit” (one that doesn’t show too much) and are confused by references to “modest beginnings”—a person from a modest or humble background grew up poor (or relatively poor).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "modest: humble, not showy, decent and in moderation.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "mollify", "pos": "verb", "pron": "MAH-lih-fy", "also": "", "d1": "Calm or soothe (an angry person); lessen or soften", "sent": "The cellular company’s billing practices were so infuriating to customers that the customer service representatives spent every workday <b>mollifying</b> angry customers.", "related": "Appease, Placate, and Assuage are near-synonyms.", "moreinfo": "Mollify shares a root (“soften”) with emollient, an ointment or lotion.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "mollify: make them \"mellow,\" soften the anger and soothe.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "monotony", "pos": "noun", "pron": "muh-NOT-uh-nee", "also": "", "d1": "Sameness or repetitiousness to the point of being boring; lack of variation, uniformity, esp. repetition in sound", "sent": "The <b>monotony</b> of working on a factory assembly line made her feel as though she would go insane from boredom.", "related": "Tedium (that which is tiresome due to being too long, dull, or slow, as in The plot moved at a tedious pace).", "moreinfo": "Monotone comes from Latin parts for “one tone,” like a dial tone on an old-fashioned phone, or a very boring public speaker. Now, we can use monotony for things other than sound—for instance, studying vocabulary can get a little monotonous if you don’t make an effort to keep it interesting by writing your own fun sentences, practicing with friends, and thinking about the gloriousness of a high GRE score.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "mono (one) + tone: one flat tone forever, boring sameness.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "moreover", "pos": "adverb", "pron": "more-OH-ver", "also": "", "d1": "Besides; in addition to what was just stated", "sent": "You are fired. <b>Moreover</b>, the police are coming to arrest you for theft.", "related": "Furthermore is a synonym. Both words can be used to begin a new independent clause after a semicolon, as in, We need additional studies to confirm our results; furthermore, we need funding for those studies.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "moreover: what is more, on top of that.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "mores", "pos": "noun", "pron": "MORE-ayz", "also": "", "d1": "Customs, manners, or morals of a particular group", "sent": "An American in Saudi Arabia should study the culture beforehand so as to avoid violating deeply conservative cultural <b>mores</b>.", "related": "Ethos (the character, personality, or moral values specific to a person, group, time period, etc.)", "moreinfo": "Pronounce this word as two syllables (rhymes with “more ways”).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "mores: a group's customs, manners, and morals.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "mundane", "pos": "adj", "pron": "mun-DAYN", "also": "", "d1": "Common, ordinary, everyday", "sent": "Dominique had been excited to visit France for the first time, but she was soon disappointed at how <b>mundane</b> the stay with her relatives was: they shopped at the market, cooked dinner, chatted with one another, and occasionally went shopping.", "related": "Quotidian can simply mean daily, or can have the more negative meaning of mundane.", "moreinfo": "Mundane comes from a root for “world” (the same root that gives us the Spanish el mundo and French le monde), the idea being that “earthly” things are below spiritual things.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "mundane, of the world (mundus): everyday, humdrum, ordinary.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "naïve", "pos": "adj", "pron": "nye-EEV", "also": "naivety or naivete (noun)", "d1": "Simple and unsophisticated, unsuspecting, lacking worldly experience and critical judgment", "sent": "I was a little <b>naïve</b> during the hiring process—the HR rep asked for my acceptable salary range, and I answered honestly. Of course I got an offer for the very lowest number in the range! Now I know I should have tried to get a number from her first, or named a higher number so I could negotiate down.", "related": "Dupe (person who is easily fooled or used, or to fool or exploit), Sap (person easily taken advantage of), Credulous (gullible)", "moreinfo": "Naïvete (or naïveté) looks strange because it’s French; it just means naïveness. Other strange spellings also pop up in regards to this word: naïf, naïfness, naïfly—all the same word. As a noun, a naïf or naïve is simply a naïve person.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "naive: newborn-innocent, lacking worldly experience.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "nascent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "NA-sent or NAY-sent", "also": "", "d1": "Coming into existence, still developing", "sent": "The violin teacher was always very encouraging with children. All children sound terrible the first couple of years, so she offered plenty of praise to encourage <b>nascent</b> talents.", "related": "Inchoate and incipient also mean just beginning, not yet completed, although inchoate can also have the sense of chaotic or disordered.", "moreinfo": "Nascent contains a Latin root (“born”) that also occurs in prenatal and neonate (a newborn baby).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "nascent, being born (nasc = birth): just coming into existence.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "negate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "neh-GAYT", "also": "", "d1": "Deny or refute; make void or cause to be ineffective", "sent": "Darling, if you add “I promise to try to work things out for at least a couple of weeks before giving up” to our wedding vows, it would kind of <b>negate</b> the part where you promise to love, honor, and cherish me “until death do us part.”\nThe debate coach reminded the students that they had to <b>negate</b> each one of their opponents’ major points in order to win.", "related": "Nullify is to make void or invalid. Sap, Enfeeble, and Undermine all mean “weaken” and thus are not as strong as negate or nullify.", "moreinfo": "Of course, negate is the same word we use in math, as in “To negate both sides of the equation, multiply through by -1.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "negate: make it negative, cancel or deny it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "net", "pos": "adj, verb", "pron": "NET", "also": "", "d1": "Remaining after expenses or other factors have been deducted; ultimate (adj); to bring in as profit, or to catch as in a net (verb)", "sent": "In one day of trading, my portfolio went up $10,000 and down $8,000, for a <b>net</b> gain of $2,000.\nAll those weeks of working weekends and playing golf with the boss ought to <b>net</b> her a promotion.", "related": "The opposite of net is gross. Your gross income is what your company pays you, and your net income is what you actually get to take home. If you sell merchandise, all the money you collect is the gross, but very little of that (or even none of it) may be profit—you have to subtract expenses to calculate the net.", "moreinfo": "A related idea is tare. If you go to a pay-by-the-pound salad bar and get your salad in a heavy dish, the person weighing the salad will often deduct the tare—the weight of the dish—prior to calculating what you pay.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "net: what is left in the net after everything is deducted.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "nevertheless or nonetheless", "pos": "adverb", "pron": "NEV-er-the-less or NUN-the-less", "also": "", "d1": "However, even so, despite that", "sent": "While losing the P&G account was a serious blow, we <b>nevertheless</b> were able to achieve a new sales goal this month due to the tireless efforts of the sales team in bringing in three new clients.\nI really can’t stand working with you. <b>Nonetheless</b>, we’re stuck on this project together and we’re going to have to get along.", "related": "Notwithstanding means “in spite of” or “all the same,” as in “The ballerina kept dancing, notwithstanding her injuries.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "none the less: even so, in spite of that.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "notoriety", "pos": "noun", "pron": "noh-ter-RY-et-ee", "also": "notorious (adj)", "d1": "Ill fame; the state of being well-known for a disgraceful reason", "sent": "Bill was notorious for being late to everything—after awhile, his friends just stopped inviting him to the movies.\nSome countries ban convicted criminals from capitalizing on their <b>notoriety</b> by writing books from prison or selling rights to movies about their lives.", "related": "Infamous is a synonym for notorious.", "moreinfo": "Not only did rapper The Notorious B.I.G. adopt the word notorious for his name, actress Tori Spelling has starred in a reality show entitled “So NoTORIous.” We guess it’s cool to be famous in a bad way?", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "notoriety: fame for being notorious, a bad reputation.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "novel", "pos": "adj", "pron": "NAH-vull", "also": "", "d1": "New, fresh, original", "sent": "You can make your writing better by eliminating clichés and replacing those clichés with more <b>novel</b> turns of speech.\nSmoked salmon on a pizza? That’s certainly a <b>novel</b> idea.", "related": "There are many more words for old, stale, overused ideas or writing lacking in novelty; trite, insipid, banal, and hackneyed are all antonyms of novel.", "moreinfo": "Novel is related to the French nouvelle, “new.” The meaning of novel as a book-length work of fiction comes from the idea of a “new story.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "a novel idea: brand-new, fresh, original.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "nuance", "pos": "noun", "pron": "NOO-ahnss", "also": "", "d1": "A subtle difference in tone, meaning, expression, etc.", "sent": "People with certain cognitive disabilities cannot understand the <b>nuances</b> of non-literal speech. For instance, “You can come if you want to, but it’s really going to be mostly family” means that you shouldn’t try to come.", "related": "Usually occurring in the plural, nuances has the synonym subtleties.", "moreinfo": "Nuance can be important in negotiating, social situations, and flirting. Nuance comes from a root for “cloud” and describes that which is “cloudy” in the sense of being hard to discern. Incidentally, nubilous means cloudy.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "a nuance: a subtle shade of difference in meaning or tone.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "objective", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ahb-JECT-iv", "also": "", "d1": "Factual, related to reality or physical objects; not influenced by emotions, unbiased", "sent": "You cannot be forced to testify against your spouse in a court of law—it’s pretty impossible for anyone to be <b>objective</b> about the guilt or innocence of a spouse.\nSome philosophers argue that things like “love” and “guilt” don’t exist, and that only <b>objective</b> reality—that is, physical matter—is of consequence.", "related": "Impartial, Disinterested, Dispassionate, and Nonpartisan are all related to being fair and not having a bias or personal stake.", "moreinfo": "The opposite of objective is subjective (taking place only in the mind, personal).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "objective: about the object and facts, not personal feelings.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "obsequious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ahb-SEEK-wee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Servile, very compliant, fawning", "sent": "Sammy thought he could get ahead by being <b>obsequious</b>, but instead, his boss gave a promotion to someone he viewed as more of a peer; truthfully, he thought Sammy’s sucking up was pretty pathetic.", "related": "Sycophant, Lackey, Toady, and Myrmidon are all words for an obsequious person (colloquially, a “bootlicker”). Fawn means to try to please in a submissive way, Truckle means to act subserviently and Kowtow means to fawn— or, literally, to bow until one’s forehead touches the floor.", "moreinfo": "The root “sequi” means “follow” and also appears in sequel and sequence.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "\"obey-sequious\": follows and fawns servilely to please.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "obsolete", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ahb-suh-LEET", "also": "obsolescence (noun)", "d1": "Out of date, no longer in use", "sent": "She kept her old laptop so long that it was <b>obsolete</b>—she couldn’t sell it on Craigslist, and the local elementary school didn’t even want it as a donation.\nWhen you look up a word in the dictionary and see “Archaic” next to a definition, that means that definition is <b>obsolete</b>—people don’t use the word that way anymore, although you might want to know that meaning if you’re reading old texts.", "related": "Antediluvian (extremely old, before the Biblical flood)", "moreinfo": "“Planned obsolescence” is a business strategy of making products with a deliberately limited life so you’ll have to buy new ones.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "obsolete: so old it is out of use, like a floppy disk.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "obstinate", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AHB-stinn-it", "also": "", "d1": "Stubborn or hard to control", "sent": "It’s difficult to get an <b>obstinate</b> child to eat food he doesn’t want to eat. When Toby realized that his son would rather sit and starve than eat mahi-mahi, he gave in and made him a peanut butter and jelly sandwich.", "related": "Intractable is a synonym. Obdurate (stubborn, hardhearted, hardened in wrongdoing) can mean obstinate but is often more negative.", "moreinfo": "From the Latin “stinare,” meaning “to stand.” Obstinate people certainly do stand their ground.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "obstinate: digs in like a mule, stubborn.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "obviate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "AHB-vee-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Prevent, eliminate, or make unnecessary", "sent": "Adding protective heel taps to your dress shoes can <b>obviate</b> the need to take them to the shoe repair store later, once the heels have worn down.", "related": "Circumvent (go around, avoid, bypass, such as circumventing the rules)", "moreinfo": "It can be confusing that obviate looks so much like obvious but doesn’t seem related. The words do share a root, and the common meaning is that something that is “in the way” is obvious —and also takes some effort to avoid or obviate.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "obviate: make it \"obviously unneeded,\" prevent the need for it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "occult", "pos": "noun, adj, verb", "pron": "uh-CULT", "also": "", "d1": "The supernatural (noun); pertaining to magic, astrology, etc.; mysterious, secret or hidden (adj); to hide, to shut off from view (verb)", "sent": "A group of religious parents demanded that a popular series of young adult vampire novels be banned in schools because it promoted the <b>occult</b>.\nDuring a solar eclipse, the moon <b>occults</b> the sun and it is momentarily dark in the middle of the day.", "related": "Eclipse (the obscuring of one thing by another, such as the sun by the moon or a person by a more famous or talented person, or to obscure, darken, make less important)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "occult: hidden, covered mysteries and the supernatural (think occlude).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "offhand", "pos": "adj", "pron": "OFF-hand", "also": "", "d1": "Casual, informal; done without preparation or forethought; rude in a short way, brusque", "sent": "I was pretty happy with my salary until my coworker Deena mentioned <b>offhandedly</b> that she was thinking about buying a house now that she made six figures.", "related": "Extemporaneous and Impromptu (done with no or little preparation), Ad-lib (to improvise; something improvised)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "offhand: off the top of your head, casual and unprepared.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "officious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "uh-FISH-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Excessively eager in giving unwanted advice or intruding where one is not wanted; meddlesome, pushy", "sent": "Lisa’s dinner parties are exhausting. She’s an <b>officious</b> host who butts in and runs everyone’s conversations, keeps an eye on what everyone is eating and makes sure you finish your vegetables, and even knocks on the bathroom door to make sure you’re “okay in there.”", "related": "In common speech, most of us would call an officious person “nosy” or a “busybody.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "officious: a busybody acting too \"official,\" meddlesome and pushy.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "offset", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "off-SET (v), OFF-set (n)", "also": "", "d1": "Counteract, compensate for (verb); a counterbalance (noun)", "sent": "Property taxes did go up this year, but the hit to our finances was <b>offset</b> by a reduction in fees paid to our homeowners association.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Something offset is “set off” against something else, such as by acting as a counterweight. A carbon offset is a reduction in emissions of greenhouse gases that can be applied to compensate for emissions made someplace else. That is, a company that sends out a lot of carbon dioxide can compensate for polluting by financially supporting energy efficiency or wind turbine projects.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "offset: set one thing off against another to balance it out.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "onerous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ONN-er-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Burdensome, oppressive, hard to endure", "sent": "Doctors are often faced with the <b>onerous</b> task of telling waiting families that their loved one has died.", "related": "Cumbersome also means burdensome (or clumsy or unwieldy), but usually in a physical way, whereas onerous is generally metaphorical. Arduous means difficult.", "moreinfo": "Onerous comes from onus, a modern-day word meaning “burden,” and is often used in the expression “put the onus on” (The committee put the onus on Joe to get everything turned in on time).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "onerous, an \"onus\" load: a heavy, oppressive burden.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "opaque", "pos": "adj", "pron": "oh-PAYK", "also": "", "d1": "Not translucent; not allowing light, heat, etc. to pass through; dark, dull, unclear or stupid", "sent": "The school dress code required <b>opaque</b> tights under skirts—the rules specified that sheer stockings were unacceptable.\nRena was tragically <b>opaque</b>—when her boyfriend said “I want to see other people,” she thought he meant he needed glasses.", "related": "Turbid refers literally to water that is muddy and not clear because of stirred-up dirt or mud; metaphorically, it can mean obscured or confused, as in turbid feelings.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 13", "setNum": 13, "hook": "opaque: solid so no light passes through; also dull or unclear.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "optimal or optimum", "pos": "adj", "pron": "AHP-tim-ull or AHP-tim-um", "also": "optimize (verb)", "d1": "Best, most desirable or favorable", "sent": "Many believe that the U.S. Constitution’s genius lies in its striking and <b>optimal</b> balance between freedom and order.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Since prime means “of first importance,” Transformers hero Optimus Prime’s name seems a bit redundant, but gets the message across—he’s best and first!", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "optimal: the optimum, the best possible choice.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "orthodox", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ORE-thoh-docks", "also": "", "d1": "Adhering to a traditional, established faith, or to anything customary or commonly accepted", "sent": "I appreciate that the new dentist thinks that my oral health can be improved through yoga, but I really prefer a more <b>orthodox</b> approach to dental care.", "related": "Conventional means traditional or customary. The opposite of orthodox is, of course, unorthodox—or its synonym, heterodox.", "moreinfo": "“Ortho” means “correct, true, straight” and also appears in orthodontist, orthopedist, etc. The root “doct/dox” means “opinion” or “teach, know” and occurs in doctrine, doctor, paradox, and doctrinaire (person who applies doctrine in an impractical or rigid and close-minded way).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "ortho (correct) + dox (belief): the correct, established standard belief.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "oscillate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "AH-sill-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Swing back and forth; waver, change one’s mind", "sent": "I wish we had air conditioning, but at least I replaced our old fan with this <b>oscillating</b> one that swings side to side and blows air on the whole room.\nStop <b>oscillating</b> and pick a restaurant! Seriously—Indian or Thai?", "related": "Equivocate (use unclear language to deceive or avoid committing to a position), Ambivalent (uncertain; unable to decide, or wanting to do two contradictory things at once), Vacillate and Waffle (waver, be indecisive), Dither (act irresolutely), Tergiversate (repeatedly change one’s opinions, equivocate)", "moreinfo": "This word has a weird story—the Latin “oscillum” means “little face.” Masks of Bacchus, the god of wine, used to be hung in vineyards to oscillate in the breeze.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "an oscillating fan swings side to side: to waver back and forth.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "outstrip", "pos": "verb", "pron": "out-STRIP", "also": "", "d1": "Surpass, exceed; be larger or better than; leave behind", "sent": "Our sales figures this quarter have <b>outstripped</b> those of any other quarter in the company’s history.", "related": "The expression “leave in the dust” has a similar meaning. Supersede means to replace or cause to be set aside (iPods have superseded portable CD players).", "moreinfo": "Other out- words can have the sense of “do better than”—for instance, outweigh, outsell, outshine. To outpace is to be faster than and to outwit or outfox is to be more clever than. Here’s a weird one: to outbrave is to be more brave than.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "outstrip: strip right past the others, surpass them.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "overshadow", "pos": "verb", "pron": "oh-ver-SHAD-oh", "also": "", "d1": "Cast a shadow over, darken; dominate, make to seem less important", "sent": "She was a straight-A student who excelled at field hockey, but she still felt <b>overshadowed</b> by her older sister, who won a national science competition for her work on cancer in mice, and also had time to become a pole vaulting champion and model who walked in Fashion Week.", "related": "Eclipse (the obscuring of one thing by another, such as the sun by the moon or a person by a more famous or talented person, or to obscure, darken, make less important)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "overshadow: cast a shadow over, dominate and outshine.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "paradigm", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PAIR-uh-dime", "also": "", "d1": "Model or pattern; worldview, set of shared assumptions, values, etc.", "sent": "Far from being atypically bawdy, this limerick is a <b>paradigm</b> of the form—nearly all of them rely on off-color jokes.", "related": "Prototype (original model serving as the basis for future copies or versions), Exemplar (example, item that is representative or typical, something worthy of imitation)", "moreinfo": "“Para” means “beside.” Paradigm comes from two Greek word parts meaning “beside” and “show” and combined into the Greek “paradiegma” for “pattern or example.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "a paradigm is the model or pattern everyone follows.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "paradox", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PAIR-uh-docks", "also": "", "d1": "Contradiction, or seeming contradiction that is actually true", "sent": "Kayla was always bothering the youth minister with her <b>paradoxes</b>, like “If God is all- powerful, can He make a burrito so big He can’t eat it?”", "related": "Conundrum (a riddle, the answer to which involves a play on words; any mystery), Enigma (puzzle, mystery, riddle; mysterious or contradictory person)", "moreinfo": "The root “doct/dox” means “opinion” or “teach, know” and occurs in doctrine, doctor, and doctrinaire (person who applies doctrine in an impractical or rigid and close-minded way).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "a paradox: \"this statement is false,\" a contradiction that is still true.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pariah", "pos": "noun", "pron": "puh-RYE-uh", "also": "", "d1": "Social outcast, untouchable", "sent": "After the schoolteacher was fired for participating in what some called a “hate rally,” he became a <b>pariah</b> in his own town, glared at in the grocery store—even his longtime barber refused him a haircut.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "This word comes from the Tamil language, spoken in India, and is related to the caste system and to the lowest group of people in it, the “untouchables” or pariahs.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "a pariah is shunned, an untouchable outcast.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "partial", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PAR-shull", "also": "", "d1": "Biased, prejudiced, favoring one over others; having a special liking for something or someone (usually partial to)", "sent": "Although I grew up in New York, I’ve always been <b>partial</b> to country music.\nHis lawyers are appealing on the grounds that the judge was <b>partial</b> to the plaintiff, even playing golf with the plaintiff during the trial.", "related": "Partisan (partial to a particular party, group, etc., esp. in a biased, emotional way). Predilection, Propensity, Proclivity, and Bent are all words for a preference or inclination (He has an arrogant bent about him, and a propensity to offend others).", "moreinfo": "Of course, partial can also mean “not complete.” The connection between the two meanings is that, if you are partial to pumpkin pie, for instance, you are “one-sided” about it.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "partial: takes a part or side, biased; or partial to sweets, fond.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "partisan", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "PAR-tiss-un", "also": "", "d1": "Devoted to a particular group, cause, etc. (adj.); fervent supporter of a group, party, idea, etc.; guerilla fighter (noun)", "sent": "It is unconscionable to engage in <b>partisan</b> politics in a time of crisis. People are trapped in the rubble of an earthquake, and you suggest that we vote for your tax bill in exchange for your voting for our relief bill?", "related": "Partial (biased, prejudiced; having a special liking for something)", "moreinfo": "Partisan is related to the idea of a party (as in a political party). A party or other self- interested group can also be called a faction. Partisanship gone too far could be called factionalism. Bipartisan means pertaining to both parties (typically in a two-party system), and nonpartisan means unbiased or not affiliated with a party.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "partisan: fiercely for one party, a biased supporter.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "patent", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "PAT-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Obvious, apparent, plain to see (adj); a letter from a government guaranteeing an inventor the rights to his or her invention (noun)", "sent": "Her resume was full of <b>patent</b> lies: anyone could check to see that she had never been president of UNICEF.", "related": "Blatant means obvious or flagrant and is used to describe something bad (blatantly disregarding the rules).", "moreinfo": "Patent comes from a Latin root for “openness,” also appearing in petal. A “letter patent” was once an open letter, and this is the origin of “patent” as a record of idea ownership—if you have a patent, there is an “open letter” (patent for all to see) attesting that your invention belongs to you.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "a patent lie is plainly obvious; also a patent protects an invention.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pathological", "pos": "adj", "pron": "path-oh-LODGE-ick-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Relating to or caused by disease; relating to compulsive bad behavior", "sent": "She thought her skin darkening was simply a result of the sun, but it was actually <b>pathological</b>, the result of a serious disease.\nI can’t believe you stole from the Make a Wish Foundation—you’re <b>pathological</b>!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "“Pathos” means “disease.” Pathological often occurs as “pathological liar.” When pathological is used alone to describe a person, the meaning is that the person compulsively lies or hurts others.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "a pathological liar cannot stop, like a disease (path = disease).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "patronizing", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PAY-truh-nize-ing", "also": "patronize (verb)", "d1": "Condescending, having a superior manner, treating as an inferior", "sent": "I’m not surprised that Professor McDougal used to teach kindergarten. His <b>patronizing</b> tone has been driving me crazy. Not only does he assume we can barely add or subtract, he also makes us put our heads down and have a moment of silence.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "As a verb, patronize means to be a patron of—that is, to support with money. You can be a patron of the arts, and restaurants sometimes have signs thanking you for your patronage. This meaning is positive, while being patronizing is negative. What’s the connection? This word (along with patriotic) comes from the Latin “pater,” for “father.” Even in Roman times, fathers had a certain stereotype—they give you money, but they also talk to you like you’re a little kid.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "patronizing: talks down to you like a superior \"patron.\"", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "paucity", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PAW-sit-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Scarcity, the state of being small in number", "sent": "Our school has such a disgraceful <b>paucity</b> of textbooks that the students are sharing, and so cannot even count on being able to take the books home to do homework.", "related": "Dearth (scarcity, lack)", "moreinfo": "Paucity is related to pauper, a very poor person.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "paucity, a pauper's amount (pauc = few): a scarcity, too little.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "peccadillo", "pos": "noun", "pron": "peck-uh-DILL-oh", "also": "", "d1": "Small sin or fault", "sent": "I’m going to propose to Melinda tomorrow—sure, she has her <b>peccadillos</b>, like anyone, but she’s the perfect woman for me.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "“Pecado” is Spanish for “sin.” Adding “-illo” to a Spanish word indicates “small” (a tomatillo resembles a small tomato, for instance). Thus, peccadillo means “small sin.” English speakers pronounce the word like “armadillo,” although of course Spanish speakers would use a “y” sound for the double l.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "a peccadillo: a little \"peck\" of a sin, a minor fault.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pedestrian", "pos": "adj", "pron": "puh-DESS-tree-un", "also": "", "d1": "Ordinary, dull, commonplace", "sent": "It was so amazing when you had us over to dinner and made that rack of lamb! I’m afraid neither of us is a professional chef like you are, though, so we hope you won’t mind that we’ve made something more <b>pedestrian</b>: hot dogs and French fries.", "related": "Prosaic means “relating to prose” or “ordinary, dull, commonplace”—the idea being that prose is not as exciting or noble as poetry.", "moreinfo": "Of course, a pedestrian is also someone who travels on foot (the root for “foot” also appears in pedal, moped, and podiatrist). The connection with “ordinary” is that a distinguished person would travel on horseback—only the common folk would travel on foot.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "pedestrian: plodding along on foot, plain and unremarkable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "penchant", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PEN-chint", "also": "", "d1": "Liking or inclination (usually penchant for)", "sent": "He seems like a mild-mannered accountant, but twice a year he jets off to Australia to satisfy his <b>penchant</b> for adventure sports.", "related": "Predilection, Propensity, Proclivity, and Bent are all words for a preference or inclination (He has an arrogant bent about him, and a propensity to offend others). Partial means biased, or having a special liking for something or someone.", "moreinfo": "From the French penchant (“leaning”) which comes from a Latin root that also gives us pendulum. A person with a penchant leans metaphorically in a certain direction.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "a penchant: a strong leaning or inclination toward something.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "perfidious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "per-FIDD-ee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Disloyal, treacherous, violating one’s trust", "sent": "The <b>perfidious</b> soldier sold out his comrades, giving secrets to the enemy in exchange for money and protection.", "related": "Apostate (person who deserts a party, cause, religion, etc.), Recreant (coward or deserter), Quisling (person who betrays his country by aiding an invader), Infidel (unbeliever, person who does not accept a particular faith)", "moreinfo": "Perfidious contains the root “fid,” for “trust,” also appearing in diffident, fiduciary, infidel, and Fidel Castro’s name.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "per (away) + fid (faith): breaks faith, treacherous.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "peripheral", "pos": "adj", "pron": "puh-RIFF-er-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Relating to or making up an outer boundary or region; not of primary importance, fringe", "sent": "My main goal is to get into a good grad school. Whether it has good fitness facilities is really a <b>peripheral</b> concern.", "related": "Auxiliary (helping or supporting the main thing)", "moreinfo": "“Peri” means “around.” Peripheral vision is what you can see “out of the corner of your eye.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "on the periphery, the outer edge: not central (like PC peripherals).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "permeate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "PER-mee-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Spread or penetrate throughout", "sent": "Under the Emperor Constantine, Christianity began to <b>permeate</b> every sector of public life.", "related": "Pervade is a synonym. Both words use the root “per” for “through.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "per (through) + meate: seep through and spread all the way.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pervasive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "per-VAY-siv", "also": "", "d1": "Tending to spread throughout", "sent": "Poverty is <b>pervasive</b> in our school system; 65% of students receive free or reduced-price lunch.", "related": "Omnipresent and ubiquitous mean existing everywhere.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "per (through) + vas (go): it goes through everything, everywhere.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "philanthropy", "pos": "noun", "pron": "fill-ANN-thruh-pee", "also": "", "d1": "Efforts to improve the well-being of humankind, generally through giving money", "sent": "Many wealthy people turn to <b>philanthropy</b> as a way to create social good, and many others turn to it as a way to hobnob with the rich and famous.", "related": "Altriusm (selflessness, concern for the welfare of others)", "moreinfo": "“Phil” means “love” (a bibliophile loves books) and “anthro” means “humankind.” A misanthropist is a hater of humankind.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "phil (love) + anthro (humans): love of humankind, giving.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "phony", "pos": "adj", "pron": "FOH-nee", "also": "", "d1": "Fake, counterfeit; insincere, not genuine", "sent": "<b>Phony</b> Louis Vuitton bags are easily purchased in Chinatown, but they are illegal—selling them can land a vendor in jail, and the counterfeit bags are often made with child labor.\nShe’s such a <b>phony</b> person, pretending to befriend people and then talking about them behind their backs.", "related": "Faux, apocryphal, and spurious all mean fake or counterfeit. A phony person may possess various affectations (fake behavior, such as in speech or dress, adopted to give a certain impression).", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "phony: fake, counterfeit, a phony baloney.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PIE-uss", "also": "piety (noun)", "d1": "Devout; religiously reverent and dutiful", "sent": "Some matrimonial websites catering to conservative religious groups contain listings for young women that feature testimonials from the woman’s relatives about her piety. Naturally, only similarly <b>pious</b> suitors need apply.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Pious shares a root (“pure”) with purity, puree, and purge.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "pious: full of piety, devoutly religious and reverent.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pith", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PITH", "also": "", "d1": "Core, essence; significance or weight", "sent": "I can only stay at this meeting for a minute—can you get to the <b>pith</b> of the issue now, and discuss the details after I leave?\nThis presentation has no <b>pith</b>—there’s no central point and nothing I didn’t already know.", "related": "Gist means main idea. To distill is to purify (as water) or extract the essential elements of (as an idea).", "moreinfo": "Pith is also the central part of a plant stem, feather, or hair. To pith cattle is to kill it by severing the spinal cord—that is, the central part. A pith helmet (worn in battle) is so called because it was originally made from the pith of the Bengal spongewood tree.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 14", "setNum": 14, "hook": "pith: the core of the fruit, the essential heart of the matter.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "placate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "PLAY-cayt", "also": "", "d1": "Satisfy or calm down (an angry or dissatisfied person), esp. by conciliatory gestures", "sent": "“It’s an interesting business model,” said the flower shop owner. “Outside of Valentine’s Day, our average customer uses our product to <b>placate</b> an angry partner.”", "related": "Appease, Mollify, Conciliate, and Assuage are near-synonyms. Implacable means “not able to be placated.”", "moreinfo": "“Conciliatory gestures” (as in the definition above) could mean giving in, lowering oneself, compromising one’s principles, offering gifts or rebates, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "placate: bring them \"placid\" peace, calm the angry person.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "placid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PLASS-id", "also": "", "d1": "Peaceful, calm, tranquil", "sent": "Famed Spanish tenor <b>Placido</b> Domingo has an unusual name—literally, “Peaceful Sunday.” It’s certainly not a coincidence that <b>Placido</b> looks so much like <b>placid</b>.", "related": "Quiescent (quiet, still)", "moreinfo": "The movie Lake Placid is about a lake containing a man-eating crocodile, which is really NOT placid. Lake Placid is a real place in New York, though, and seems to have been named with much the same thought in mind as the Pacific Ocean (pacific also means peaceful).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "a placid lake: peaceful, calm, tranquil.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "plastic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PLASS-tick", "also": "", "d1": "Able to be shaped or formed; easily influenced", "sent": "A young child’s mind is quite <b>plastic</b>, and exposure to violent movies and video games can have a much greater effect on children than they typically do on adults.", "related": "Tractable, pliable, and malleable can also mean physically bendable, or metaphorically bendable, as in “easily influenced or shaped by others.” Mutable means changeable.", "moreinfo": "The word plastic is much older than the substance we call “plastic.” Plastic surgery is so called because it reshapes parts of the body, not because the substance plastic is involved.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "plastic: moldable, easily shaped or influenced.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "plausible", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PLAW-zib-ull", "also": "plausibility (noun)", "d1": "Believable; having the appearance of truth", "sent": "When three doctors treating crash victims were suddenly stricken with what looked like the effects of nerve gas, hospital officials posited “hysteria” as the culprit—hardly a <b>plausible</b> explanation, as emergency room doctors are accustomed to seeing horrific things every day.", "related": "Credible (believable), Feasible (possible; logical or likely; suitable), Viable (able to live or develop; capable of success, practicable, workable)", "moreinfo": "Plausible shares a Latin root (“approve”) with applause and plaudits.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "plausible, worthy of applause and belief: it seems true.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "plummet", "pos": "verb", "pron": "PLUM-it", "also": "", "d1": "Plunge, fall straight down", "sent": "During the first 60 seconds or so of a skydive, the diver <b>plummets</b> towards Earth in freefall; then, he or she activates a parachute and floats down at what seems like a relatively leisurely pace.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "As a noun, a plummet (or plumb bob) is a weight on the end of a cord. To plumb (or sound) the depths of a body of water is to drop a plummet (or sounding line) and see how much cord is used when the plummet hits the bottom, and therefore how deep the water is. When something plummets, the idea is that it is falling fast and straight down, as though it has been weighted.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "a plumb weight plummets straight down: plunge, fall.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "polarized", "pos": "adj", "pron": "POH-ler-rize-d", "also": "", "d1": "Divided into sharply opposed groups", "sent": "The school board was used to rationally discussing issues, but when it came to the teaching of evolution in schools, the board was <b>polarized</b>, immediately splitting into two camps, with the discussion devolving into a shouting match within minutes.", "related": "Faction (group or clique within a larger organization), Partisan (partial to a particular party, group, etc., esp. in a biased, emotional way), Cabal (a conspiratorial group)", "moreinfo": "In science, polarized objects have positive electric charges in one area and negative electric charges in another.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "polarized to opposite poles: sharply split into two camps.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ponderous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PAHN-der-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Heavy; bulky and unwieldy; dull, labored", "sent": "The book assigned by her professor was a <b>ponderous</b> tome, more a reference book than something you could read straight through. She was so bored she thought she would die.", "related": "Tedium (that which is tiresome due to being too long, dull, or slow, as in The plot moved at a tedious pace)", "moreinfo": "To ponder is simply to contemplate or think deeply about. But ponderous means boring, difficult to make your way through. Both are based on the idea of “heaviness”—considering “heavy” ideas makes you a thoughtful person, but having to listen to a long, boring speech might seem like a heavy weight upon you.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "ponderous, heavy to ponder (pond = weight): bulky, dull, labored.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "posthumous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PAHSS-chum-muss", "also": "", "d1": "Happening or continuing after death", "sent": "Ernest Hemingway died in 1961. His novel The Garden of Eden was published <b>posthumously</b> in 1986.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "“Post” means “after,” course. Posthumous also shares a root with humus—earth or soil, as in the kind a deceased person is buried in. (Not to be confused with hummus, the spread made from chickpeas). Posthumous often describes the publication of books, the granting of awards or military honors, or the birth of a child after the father’s death.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "post (after) + humus (burial ground): happening after death.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "potentate", "pos": "noun", "pron": "POH-tent-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Ruler, person of great power", "sent": "62-year-old Prince Charles has certainly waited long enough to become <b>potentate</b> of England; his mother, Elizabeth II, has been ruling for his entire life.", "related": "Sovereign can be a noun meaning “monarch or supreme ruler,” or an adjective meaning “having supreme power, being above all others.”", "moreinfo": "Potentate shares a root (“power”) with omnipotent (all powerful), impotent (lacking power), and despot (ruler who uses power in a bad way).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "a potentate holds all the potent power: a ruler.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pragmatic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "prag-MAT-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Practical; dealing with actual facts and reality", "sent": "Megan and Dave were in love, but Megan decided to be <b>pragmatic</b>—she doubted they’d stay together through a four-year long distance relationship as they attended different colleges, so she figured they might as well end things now.", "related": "Politic (shrewd, pragmatic; tactful or diplomatic), Expedient (suitable, proper; effective, often at the expense of ethics or other considerations)", "moreinfo": "A pragmatic sanction is a ruler’s declaration having the force of law. A ruler might issue a pragmatic sanction when the ideal situation isn’t possible, so one must be practical and “just get the job done.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pragmatic: practical, dealing with real facts and results.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "preamble", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PREE-am-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Introductory statement, preface", "sent": "The <b>Preamble</b> to the Constitution is a brief introduction that begins, “We the People of the United States, in Order to form a more perfect Union…”\nThe unusual outfit the pop star wore to her first awards ceremony was a mere <b>preamble</b> to a career of full-blown crazy dressing.", "related": "A prologue is an introductory part, usually of a work of literature.", "moreinfo": "Preamble literally means “walk before.” The root for “walk” is also found in amble, ambulance, ambulatory, and circumambulate.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pre (before) + amble (walk): a walk-in intro before the main text.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "precarious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "prick-CAIR-ee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Unstable, insecure, dangerous", "sent": "Recognizing that his position at the company was <b>precarious</b>, Sanjay requested that his bonus structure be formally written down as a contract, rather than dangled over him as a mere verbal promise.", "related": "Parlous is a synonym.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "precarious: teetering on the edge, unstable and dangerous.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "precursor", "pos": "noun", "pron": "prick-KER-ser or PREE-ker-ser", "also": "", "d1": "Something that comes before, esp. something that also announces or suggests something on its way", "sent": "We cannot ignore this warning sign—it is clearly a <b>precursor</b> of larger problems to come.\nThe new CEO decided to do things differently from his <b>precursors</b>.", "related": "Antedate (be older than, precede in time; assign to an earlier date)", "moreinfo": "The “cursor” in precursor is the same as in currency—the root means “run.” A precursor can be thought of as a “before-runner,” or forerunner (a synonym).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pre (before) + cursor (runner): runs ahead, a forerunner.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "predisposed", "pos": "adj", "pron": "pree-diss-POH-zd", "also": "predisposition (noun)", "d1": "Having an inclination or tendency beforehand; susceptible", "sent": "Some autoimmune disorders don’t kill the patient directly, but rather make the patient <b>predisposed</b> to contracting other, potentially fatal illnesses.\nHis defense attorney argued that his abusive childhood <b>predisposed</b> him to a life of crime.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Predisposed certainly is related to the idea of being disposed. While to dispose of something is to throw it away, to be disposed to do something is to be inclined to or willing to do it. For instance, He is disposed to be a good host. preempt or", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pre + disposed: already leaning that way in advance.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pre-empt", "pos": "verb", "pron": "pree-EMPT", "also": "", "d1": "Prevent; take the place of, supplant; take before someone else can", "sent": "The speaker attempted to preempt an excessively long Q&A session by handing out a “Frequently Asked Questions” packet at the beginning of the seminar.", "related": "Obviate (prevent, eliminate, or make unnecessary)", "moreinfo": "The “empt” part of preempt comes from a rare word for “purchase”—to preempt can also mean to settle on public land in order to establish the right to purchase that land before anyone else.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pre + empt (take): grab it first, before anyone else can.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "presumptive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "prizz-ZUMP-tiv", "also": "", "d1": "Based on inference or assumption; providing reasonable grounds for belief", "sent": "The dictator’s favorite nephew is the <b>presumptive</b> heir to power, but anything could happen.\nHe’s the <b>presumptive</b> winner of the election—we haven’t counted all the votes, but at this point it’s almost mathematically impossible for the other guy to win.", "related": "Ostensible or ostensive (professed, evident, or pretended; outwardly appearing in a certain way), Putative (supposed or reputed)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse presumptive with presumptuous, meaning assuming in an arrogant way. He’s presumptively a nice guy, at least from what I’ve heard—my sister said he wasn’t presumptuous at all.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "presumptive: presumed likely, the assumed pick (heir presumptive).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "presumptuous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "prizz-ZUMP-choo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Too bold or forward; going beyond that which is proper", "sent": "I would never date that <b>presumptuous</b> jerk! I mean, I thought he was attractive until he walked up to me and said, “We should go out—I looked up your address on Google and I’ll pick you up at 8.” The nerve!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse presumptuous with presumptive, meaning “based on inference or assumption,” as in a presumptive heir to the throne.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "presumptuous: presumes too much, overstepping and too bold.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pretentious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "prit-TENT-chuss", "also": "", "d1": "Claiming or demanding a position of importance or dignity, esp. when unjustified; showing off, creating a deceptive, false show of worth", "sent": "Josie found her date’s habit of constantly dropping French phrases into conversation to be incredibly <b>pretentious</b>, especially since he knew she didn’t speak French. He sure did sound fancy, though.", "related": "Ostentatious (pretentious, boastfully showy in order to impress others)", "moreinfo": "Pretentious shares an origin with pretend and pretense (pretending or making a false show of something).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pretentious: putting on pretenses, faking importance.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "principled", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PRIN-sip-ull-d", "also": "", "d1": "Having high moral standards", "sent": "Donna was only an intern, but she was also quite <b>principled</b>—although she knew it would kill her shot at a full-time job, she was the one who alerted the authorities when asked to violate federal law via corrupt accounting practices.", "related": "Scrupulous can mean principled (strictly following moral rules), or can mean precise, exact, careful about the details.", "moreinfo": "A principle is a moral rule. A principal is the head of a school, and principal as an adjective means first or most important (the money you owe on student loans, not including the interest, is the principal). Thus, you could say something like My principal principle is never to lie.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "principled: lives by firm principles, upright.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pristine", "pos": "adj", "pron": "priss-TEEN", "also": "", "d1": "In an original, pure state; uncorrupted", "sent": "Having grown up in a rural area, Billy had been in plenty of forests where people regularly left beer cans and shotgun shells behind. So it truly took his breath away, after hiking hours through the Himalayas, to visit a <b>pristine</b> forest, seemingly untouched by humans.", "related": "Unadulterated and unsullied are near-synonyms.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pristine: untouched and spotless, pure as new.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "probity", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PROH-bit-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Honesty, integrity", "sent": "After losing the last election when their candidate’s string of mistresses came to light, this time, the party would only nominate a candidate whose <b>probity</b> was beyond any doubt.", "related": "Scrupulous or principled (having high moral standards), Veracious (honest, true)", "moreinfo": "Normally we think of a “probe” as sort of medically unpleasant, but the real purpose of a probe is to get information—for instance, a government probe might investigate corruption, or probe for the truth.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "probity, tested and \"proven\" honest (prob = prove): integrity.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "prodigal", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PROD-dig-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Wasteful, extravagant; giving abundantly, lavish", "sent": "If you’re going to leave a trust fund for your children, you should raise them not to be <b>prodigal</b>, or they’ll blow through all the cash the minute they get their hands on it.\nThe <b>prodigal</b> land produced larger crops than the people could even consume.", "related": "Profligate (utterly immoral or extremely prodigal), Wastrel or Spendthrift (wasteful person)", "moreinfo": "In the Biblical story of the prodigal son, a son asks his dad if he can have his inheritance now, before the dad dies (rude!), then wastes everything on wild living and comes back all hungry and apologetic, begging for a job as a swineherd (the nice dad takes him back and kills a fattened calf for him). Don’t confuse prodigal with prodigy (very gifted child).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "the prodigal son blew all his money: wasteful, extravagant.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "prodigious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "pruh-DID-juss", "also": "", "d1": "Extraordinarily large, impressive, etc.", "sent": "The Great Wall of China consists of a <b>prodigious</b> series of fortifications stretching over 5,000 miles!\nIf we don’t double our sales with this new product, we will have to declare bankruptcy—we have a <b>prodigious</b> task ahead.", "related": "Prodigious can be good or bad, but Titanic and Olympian specifically mean large or majestic in a manner suitable for the gods.", "moreinfo": "Prodigious shares an origin with prodigy (very gifted child).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "prodigious: a prodigy-sized amount, enormous and impressive.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "profligate", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PRAH-fligg-it", "also": "", "d1": "Completely and shamelessly immoral, or extremely wasteful", "sent": "The billionaire software developer was so disgusted with his <b>profligate</b> daughter’s spending that he cut her off—she had bought champagne for an entire nightclub full of strangers one too many times.", "related": "Prodigal (wasteful, extravagant), Debauched (corrupted morally), Dissolution (sinking into extreme hedonism, vice, and degradation)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "profligate: recklessly blows both money and morals.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "profound", "pos": "adj", "pron": "proh-FOUND", "also": "", "d1": "Very insightful, penetrating deeply into a subject; pervasive, intense, “down to the very bottom”; at the very bottom", "sent": "Certain fish that live in the dark, <b>profound</b> depths of the ocean have long since evolved to have sightless eyes.\nHe was <b>profoundly</b> disappointed when the project he had worked on for fifteen years failed.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Most people know profound in the sense “Whoa, that’s deep, man.” Fewer people know that you can have profound grief or be profoundly corrupt. In both cases, profound means “complete, all the way through.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 15", "setNum": 15, "hook": "pro + found: reaching the deep bottom, deeply insightful.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "profuse", "pos": "adj", "pron": "proh-FYOOSS", "also": "", "d1": "Abundant, extravagant, giving or given freely", "sent": "It didn’t mean anything at all to me, giving my old microwave to the family next door, but the woman’s <b>profuse</b> thanks made me think that maybe the family was having some financial troubles.\nShe came home on Valentine’s Day to an apartment decorated with a <b>profusion</b> of flowers.", "related": "Copious (abundant, plentiful), Lavish (abundant or giving in abundance; marked by excess)", "moreinfo": "Here, “pro” means “forth” and “fuse” comes from the Latin “fundere,” meaning “pour or melt.” “Fuse” is also found in fusion, suffuse, diffusion, transfusion, and effusive.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "profuse, pouring forth (fus = pour): abundant, lavish.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "prohibitive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "proh-HIBB-it-iv", "also": "", "d1": "Tending to forbid something, or serving to prevent something", "sent": "I was admitted to NYU, but it was <b>prohibitively</b> expensive, so I ended up at state school instead.\nMy parents installed a high fence as a <b>prohibitive</b> barrier against the neighborhood hooligans.", "related": "Exorbitant means excessive, especially by being outrageously expensive.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "prohibitive: it prohibits, like prices so high they prevent buying.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "proliferate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "proh-LIFF-er-ayt", "also": "prolific (adj)", "d1": "Increase or spread rapidly or excessively", "sent": "The book alleged that terrorist cells are <b>proliferating</b> across the United States faster than law enforcement can keep up.", "related": "Prolific means producing a lot of something – generally either offspring (rabbits are prolific) or creative work (a prolific songwriter).", "moreinfo": "A phrase associated with the Cold War was nuclear proliferation, the rapid buildup of nuclear weapons. Proliferate shares a Latin root (“offspring, progeny”) with proletariat, meaning the working class, manual laborers, or the poor.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "proli (offspring) + ferate: it breeds and multiplies fast.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "prologue", "pos": "noun", "pron": "PROH-log", "also": "", "d1": "Introductory part to a book, play, etc.", "sent": "The novel’s <b>prologue</b> gives some historical background so the main story can be better understood in context.", "related": "Preamble (introductory statement, preface—such as the Preamble to the Constitution)", "moreinfo": "An epilogue is a concluding portion added after a literary work. The “log” in prologue and epilogue is the same root (“logos,” for “discourse or speech”) as the “lect” in lecture, lectern, and dialect. Both prologue and epilogue can be used metaphorically—“If the blown tire was an unpleasant prologue to the evening, the food poisoning we came down with that night was an even worse epilogue.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "pro (before) + logue (words): the opening words of a work.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pronounced", "pos": "adj", "pron": "proh-NOUN-st", "also": "", "d1": "Distinct, strong, clearly indicated", "sent": "Aunt Shirley claimed we would never know that her “secret recipe” for brownies involved lots of healthy vegetables, but the brownies had a <b>pronounced</b> asparagus flavor.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Of course, we pronounce a word, but pronounce can also mean declare, as in, He pronounced the book the best thing he had ever read.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "pronounced: clearly announced, strong and distinct.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "propriety", "pos": "noun", "pron": "proh-PRY-it-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Conforming to good manners or appropriate behavior; justness", "sent": "The parent questioned the <b>propriety</b> of the punishment meted out to her son—sitting in a corner all day seemed a little harsh for using the pencil sharpener at the wrong time.\nSaying the accounting firm was complicit in “financial impropriety” was a rather polite way to refer to the fraud it committed against its investors.", "related": "Genteel (aristocratic, elegant), Seemly (proper or attractive), Decorous (behaving with propriety and good taste; polite), Mores (customs, manners, or morals of a particular group)", "moreinfo": "This is an easy one to remember—it’s not a coincidence that propriety looks a lot like proper.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "propriety: doing the \"proper\" thing, correct behavior.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "prosaic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "proh-ZAY-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Dull, ordinary", "sent": "Finding his friends’ bar mitzvahs at the local synagogue a bit <b>prosaic</b>, Justin instead asked his dad to rent out the local laser tag center.", "related": "Pedestrian means commonplace, dull, or lacking imagination. Quotidian means daily or commonplace (as daily things tend to be). Middling comes from the idea of being in the “middle” and means medium, average, or mediocre. Something lackluster is unexciting. Humdrum means boring or dull.", "moreinfo": "Prosaic means “relating to prose” or “ordinary, dull, commonplace” —the idea being that prose is not as exciting or noble as poetry.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "prosaic: plain as prose, with no poetry, dull.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "proscribe", "pos": "verb", "pron": "proh-SCRIBE", "also": "", "d1": "Prohibit, outlaw; denounce; exile or banish", "sent": "Plagiarism is <b>proscribed</b> by every college’s code of conduct.", "related": "Censure (strong disapproval or official reprimand), Circumscribe (encircle or confine, set limits)", "moreinfo": "The “scribe” in proscribe means “write” (as in script, scribble, scripture, etc.) The sense is that to proscribe is to publish a record of someone’s punishment—to condemn or sentence that person publicly. Don’t confuse proscribe with prescribe, which means “order, set down as a rule” (like a doctor’s prescription)—the two words are opposites!", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "pro + scribe (write): officially write it off, outlaw it (opposite of prescribe).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "prospective", "pos": "adj", "pron": "proh-SPECK-tiv", "also": "", "d1": "Potential, in the future", "sent": "Everyone had a hard time correctly saying the name of the seminar, “Perspectives for <b>Prospective</b> Doctors.” Even the <b>prospective</b> doctors—college students hoping to be admitted to medical school—were a bit confused.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse with perspective (point of view). Perspective is a noun, which is a good clue that you want prospective in phrases like “prospective student” (usually a person applying to a college but not yet accepted).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "a prospective buyer: a potential one, in the future.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "prudent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "PROO-dent", "also": "prudence (noun)", "d1": "Wise in practical matters, carefully providing for the future", "sent": "Katie’s friends blew all kinds of money on spring break, but Katie <b>prudently</b> kept to her usual spending habits: she’d drink one of whatever was least expensive, and then watch her friends get stupid while she checked her bank balances on her phone and dreamed about the day she’d have a full-time job and a 401K.", "related": "Circumspect (cautious, prudent; careful to consider the circumstances and consequences), Provident (showing foresight, being frugal or careful to provide for the future, thinking ahead in a wise way)", "moreinfo": "The antonym is imprudent.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "prudent: careful and provident, wisely planning ahead.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "pugnacious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "pug-NAY-shuss", "also": "", "d1": "Inclined to fight, combative", "sent": "Amy had hoped to avoid inviting Uncle Ed to the wedding, as he was a <b>pugnacious</b> fellow— and, sure enough, he managed to start a fistfight with the best man.", "related": "Belligerent, bellicose, and truculent are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "Pugilism (boxing, fistfighting) and pugnacious come from the Latin “pugnus,” for “fist.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "pug (fist, as in pugilist): itching for a fistfight, combative.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "qualified", "pos": "adj", "pron": "KWAH-lif-fide", "also": "", "d1": "Modified, limited, conditional on something else", "sent": "The scientist gave her <b>qualified</b> endorsement to the book, pointing out that, while it posed a credible theory, more research was still needed before the theory could be applied.", "related": "Tentative (done in order to test; uncertain), Temper (moderate, soften, tone down)", "moreinfo": "Of course, everyone knows qualified in the sense of qualified for the job. Use context to determine which meaning is intended. A qualified person is suitable or well-prepared for the job; a qualified statement or feeling is held back or limited.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "qualified support: support \"with conditions,\" limited and hedged.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "quandary", "pos": "noun", "pron": "KWAN-duh-ree", "also": "", "d1": "Uncertainty or confusion about what to do, dilemma", "sent": "He knew it sounded like the plot of a cheesy movie, but he really had accidentally asked two girls to the prom, and now he was in quite a <b>quandary</b>.", "related": "Mired (stuck, entangled in a swamp or muddy area or a problem that is hard to get out of).", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "in a quandary: stuck in a puzzling dilemma.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "quibble", "pos": "verb", "pron": "KWIBB-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Make trivial arguments or criticisms, find faults in a petty way, esp. to evade something more important", "sent": "Look, I am telling you some of the serious consequences of global warming, as predicted by the scientific establishment—I think you’re just <b>quibbling</b> to complain that I said “carbon monoxide” when I meant “carbon dioxide.”", "related": "Cavil (synonym), Carp (constantly complain, fret, and find fault), Peevish (annoyed, in a bad mood, stubborn)", "moreinfo": "This word is often associated with lawyers.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "quibble: nitpick over trivial little points.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "quotidian", "pos": "adj", "pron": "kwoh-TIDD-ee-un", "also": "", "d1": "Daily; everyday, ordinary", "sent": "He was so involved in his quest for spiritual enlightenment that he regularly forgot more <b>quotidian</b> concerns—sure, he meditated for six hours today, but he hasn’t done laundry in weeks.", "related": "Mundane is a synonym. Pedestrian and prosaic mean commonplace, dull, or lacking imagination. Diurnal means occurring every day or happening in the daytime.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "quotidian, the daily quota: everyday and ordinary.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ranks", "pos": "noun", "pron": "RANKS", "also": "", "d1": "Personnel; a group of people considered all together", "sent": "Among the <b>ranks</b> of our alumni are two Senators and many famous authors.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Many people know the word rank as “a level or grade,” as in A general has a higher rank than a sergeant. The other use of ranks is also originally related to the military: the ranks or sometimes the rank and file means all the regular soldiers (not the officers). Ranks also refers to soldiers standing in a particular formation, so the expression to break rank means to rebel, disagree, or disrupt a situation in which everyone is doing the same thing, as in The author broke rank with her colleagues in the field of personal development by suggesting that “positive thinking” may be doing more damage than good.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "the ranks: all the people lined up together in a group.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "reap", "pos": "verb", "pron": "REEP", "also": "", "d1": "Harvest, such as by cutting; gather; get as a result of one’s effort", "sent": "He worked night and day in the strange new country, never stopping to rest, for he knew he would <b>reap</b> his reward when his family greeted him as a hero for all the money he had sent back home.", "related": "Reap and sow are used together or separately as metaphors related to farming, and specifically the idea that the seeds that you plant (or sow) determine what you will later harvest (or reap). A common expression is You reap what you sow.", "moreinfo": "The “Grim Reaper” is a fictional figure who uses a scythe (curved blade on a handle) to “cut down” lives as one would cut down grain.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "reap what you sow: harvest and gather the result.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "recluse", "pos": "noun", "pron": "RECK-looss", "also": "", "d1": "Person who lives in seclusion", "sent": "That show about “hoarders” featured a <b>recluse</b> who hadn’t left her house in six years.", "related": "Hermit or Anchorite (person who lives away from society, esp. for religious reasons)", "moreinfo": "Recluse is generally more negative than hermit—it often refers to a person thought by others to be a bit crazy. Recluse shares a root (“claudere,” meaning “to close or shut”) with secluded and exclusive.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "a recluse re-closes the door and lives alone in seclusion.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "refute", "pos": "verb", "pron": "riff-FYOOT", "also": "", "d1": "Prove to be false", "sent": "She’s not a very valuable member of the debate team, actually—she loves making speeches, but she’s not very good at <b>refuting</b> opponents’ arguments.", "related": "Gainsay (deny, refute, oppose), Negate (deny or refute; make void or cause to be ineffective)", "moreinfo": "An opposite of refute is corroborate (confirm).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "refute: shoot the claim down, prove it false.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "relegate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "RELL-igg-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Send or commit to an inferior place, rank, condition, etc.; exile, banish; assign (a task) to someone else", "sent": "After the legal associate offended one of the partners, he found himself <b>relegated</b> to working on minor—even unwinnable—cases.\nThis protest is occurring because we refuse to be <b>relegated</b> to the fringes of society—we demand full inclusion!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Relegate shares a root (“send”) with legate, a deputy or emissary.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "relegate: demote and banish to a lower league or place.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "remedial", "pos": "adj", "pron": "rim-EE-dee-ull", "also": "remediate (verb)", "d1": "Providing a remedy, curative; correcting a deficient skill", "sent": "After harassment occurs in the workplace, it is important that the company take <b>remedial</b> action right away, warning or firing the offender as appropriate, and making sure the complainant’s concerns are addressed.\nFor those who need <b>remedial</b> reading help, we offer a summer school program that aims to help students read at grade level.", "related": "Redress (setting something right after a misdeed)", "moreinfo": "Something irremediable cannot be remediated.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "remedial: providing a remedy, correcting a deficient skill.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "render", "pos": "verb", "pron": "REN-der", "also": "", "d1": "Give, submit, surrender; translate; declare formally; cause to become", "sent": "When you <b>render</b> your past due payments, we will turn your phone back on.\nOnly in her second year of Japanese, she was unable to <b>render</b> the classic poem into English.\nThe judge <b>rendered</b> a verdict that <b>rendered</b> us speechless.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "From an Old French word for “give back.” This word has so many definitions because it is so general. You can even render fat (by melting it); many definitions of render have to do with changing the state of something.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "render aid, render it useless: give it, or cause it to become so.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "replete", "pos": "adj", "pron": "rip-PLEET", "also": "", "d1": "Supplied in abundance, filled, gorged (used with with)", "sent": "This essay is <b>replete</b> with errors—I don’t think you even bothered to use spellcheck, much less carefully edit your work.", "related": "Surfeit, surplus, plethora (excess, overabundance)", "moreinfo": "Replete shares a root (“plenus” for “full”) with plenty and plenary (entire, complete).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "re + plete (full): completely full, gorged with.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "reproach", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "rip-PROH’ch", "also": "", "d1": "Blame, disgrace (noun); criticize, express disappointment in (verb)", "sent": "I’m not really enjoying my foreign study program. My host mom <b>reproached</b> me in Spanish— it sounded really harsh, but I couldn’t really understand her and I have no idea what I did wrong!", "related": "Admonish means scold or mildly criticize. Reprove, upbraid, reprimand, rebuke, excoriate, and castigate are all words for criticizing or scolding more harshly.", "moreinfo": "The expression beyond reproach means “not able to be reproached”—due to being good or perfect.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "reproach: \"approach\" someone with blame, scold them.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "repudiate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "rip-PYOO-dee-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Reject, cast off, deny that something has authority", "sent": "If you receive an erroneous notice from a collections agency, you have 30 days to <b>repudiate</b> the debt by mail.\nAs part of becoming an American citizen, Mr. Lee <b>repudiated</b> his former citizenship.", "related": "Recant (withdraw, retract, or disavow something one has previously said, esp. formally)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 16", "setNum": 16, "hook": "repudiate: push it away and disown it (a repudiated debt).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "requite", "pos": "verb", "pron": "rick-WHITE", "also": "", "d1": "Reciprocate, repay, or revenge", "sent": "Ashley felt that her unrequited love for George would surely kill her. George barely noticed her—he cared about nothing but <b>requiting</b> his father’s death.", "related": "Redress (setting something right after a misdeed; compensation or relief for injury or wrongdoing), Recompense (repay, reward, compensate)", "moreinfo": "Most people only know this word in the phrase unrequited love. But anything you can “get someone back for,” you can requite—kindness, murder, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "re + quite (pay, quit): pay it back, requite love or revenge.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "rescind", "pos": "verb", "pron": "riss-SIND", "also": "", "d1": "Annul, repeal, make void", "sent": "The governor <b>rescinded</b> his proclamation making September 10th “Pastafarian Day” once someone told him it wasn’t a real religion.", "related": "Negate (deny or refute; make void or cause to be ineffective), Nullify (make void or invalid)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "rescind, to cut it back (scind = cut): annul and cancel it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "resolution", "pos": "noun", "pron": "rezz-oh-LOO-shun", "also": "", "d1": "The quality of being firmly determined; resolving to do something; a formal judgment, esp. decided by a vote", "sent": "The city government passed a <b>resolution</b> to support the new monorail.\nA few setbacks did not dampen her <b>resolution</b> to complete her Ph.D.—equipped with her new prosthetic leg, she made her way back into the lab and continued her research.", "related": "Resolve shares the meaning “the quality of being firmly determined” and would also work in the second sentence above.", "moreinfo": "Of course, a New Year’s resolution is a decision to do something or make a change. Most people aren’t resolved enough to keep their resolutions past January.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "resolution: being resolute; or a formal decision by vote.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "resolve", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "rizz-ZAHL’v", "also": "", "d1": "Find a solution to; firmly decide to do something; decide by formal vote (verb); firmness of purpose (noun)", "sent": "She was <b>resolved</b> to find a marrow donor for her son, and led a stunningly successful drive to get people to sign up for a national donor registry. Even when no match was found for her son in the first year, her <b>resolve</b> was undampened.", "related": "Resolute (firmly determined), Unequivocal (clear or decided), Resolution (the quality of being firmly determined; resolving to do something; a formal judgment, esp. decided by a vote)", "moreinfo": "To lose your resolve means to become unsure or to lose your nerve.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "resolve the issue; New Year's resolve: firmness of purpose.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "respectively", "pos": "adverb", "pron": "ree-SPECK-tiv-lee", "also": "", "d1": "In the order given", "sent": "His poems “An Ode to the Blossoms of Sheffield” and “An Entreaty to Ladies All Too Prim” were written in 1756 and 1758, <b>respectively</b>.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Respectively is important in making the meaning clear in some sentences. Lisa and John have a cat and a dog sounds as though the couple jointly owns the pets, whereas Lisa and John have a cat and a dog, respectively makes it clear that the cat is Lisa’s and the dog is John’s.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "respectively: each in the respective order just given.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "restive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "REST-iv", "also": "", "d1": "Impatient or uneasy under the control of another; resisting being controlled", "sent": "The company was purchased by a larger competitor, and the employees grew <b>restive</b> as the new bosses curtailed their freedoms and put a hold on their projects.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse with restless, meaning “lacking rest” or “constantly moving.” You grow restive while waiting for a boss to approve your project; when you can’t sleep, you have a restless night.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "restive: restless and impatient under control, hard to keep still.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "reticent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "RET-iss-sent", "also": "", "d1": "Not talking much; private (of a person), restrained, reserved", "sent": "She figured that, to rise to the top, it was best to be <b>reticent</b> about her personal life; thus, even her closest colleagues were left speculating at the water cooler about whether her growing belly actually indicated a pregnancy she simply declined to mention to anyone.", "related": "Laconic (using few words, concise)", "moreinfo": "Taciturn also means not talking much. The Latin root “tacere” (to be silent) appears in both words.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "reticent: holds back words, reserved and private.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "retrospective", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "ret-truh-SPECK-tiv", "also": "", "d1": "Looking to the past or backward; applying to the past, retroactive (adj); an art exhibit of an artist’s work over a long period of time (noun)", "sent": "The proposed law is <b>retrospective</b>: anyone who violated the law before the law even existed can be prosecuted.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The expression in retrospect is often used to express what the speaker would have done or thought in the past if he knew what he knows now. In retrospect, I should have realized that a Nigerian widow wouldn’t want to leave me a million dollars for no reason, but at the time, I was very excited.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "retro (back) + spect (look): a look back over the past.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "reverent", "pos": "adj", "pron": "REV-er-ent", "also": "", "d1": "Feeling or expressing very deep respect and awe", "sent": "Ayn Rand is a controversial figure, but critical views are not welcome at the local Objectivist Club meeting, where everyone expresses a <b>reverent</b> view of the author.", "related": "Pious (devout; religiously reverent and dutiful)", "moreinfo": "This word comes from the same place as reverend (a minister, as in a church).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "reverent: full of reverence, deep respect and awe.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "rhetoric", "pos": "noun", "pron": "RET-er-ick", "also": "rhetorical (adj)", "d1": "The art or study of persuasion through speaking or writing; language that is elaborate or pretentious but actually empty, meaning little", "sent": "The ancient Greeks used to study <b>rhetoric</b> as a major academic subject. Today, if you want to improve your <b>rhetorical</b> skills, you will probably have to hunt down a public speaking class or join Toastmasters.\nThe politician’s blather is all <b>rhetoric</b> and no substance.", "related": "Oratorical is a synonym. While a lawyer needs good rhetorical skills, sometimes rhetorical and oratorical mean only related to style and effect, and lacking substance.", "moreinfo": "A rhetorical question is one intended for effect, and not intended to be answered, as in “Are you stupid?”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "rhetoric: the art of persuasion, or empty fancy talk.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "rife", "pos": "adj", "pron": "RIFE", "also": "", "d1": "Happening frequently, abundant, currently being reported", "sent": "Reports of financial corruption are <b>rife</b>.", "related": "Replete (supplied in abundance, filled, gorged), Ridden (dominated or burdened by), Teeming (swarming, as in teeming with people)", "moreinfo": "From an Old Norse word for “river”—thus the idea of “flowing freely.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "rife: rampant and widespread, teeming with it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "rudimentary", "pos": "adj", "pron": "roo-dim-MENT-uh-ree", "also": "", "d1": "Elementary, relating to the basics; undeveloped, primitive", "sent": "My knowledge of Chinese is quite <b>rudimentary</b>—I get the idea of characters and I can order food, but I really can’t read this document you’ve just given me.", "related": "Inchoate (just begun, undeveloped, unorganized), Nascent and Incipient (just beginning to exist, or in a very early stage of development)", "moreinfo": "Rudimentary shares a root with rude. Rude originally meant crude or unlearned—that is, lacking rudiments (first principles or early training).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "rudimentary: just the rudiments and basics, undeveloped.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "rustic", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "RUSS-tick", "also": "", "d1": "Relating to country life, unsophisticated; primitive; made of rough wood (adj); a rural or uncultured person (noun)", "sent": "For their honeymoon, they eschewed fancy hotels and instead chose a <b>rustic</b> cabin in the woods.\nGrandpa was a true <b>rustic</b>—I was happy to have him visit, but not so happy to find him urinating outside in a bucket when we have several perfectly nice bathrooms.", "related": "Bumpkin and yokel are also words for an awkward, uncultured, simple person, generally from the country.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "rustic, of the country (rus): rural, rough, and unsophisticated.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sacrosanct", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SACK-roh-sank-t", "also": "", "d1": "Sacred, inviolable, not to be trespassed on or violated; above any criticism", "sent": "In our house, family dinners were <b>sacrosanct</b>—if being in the school play meant you would miss dinner, then you just couldn’t be in the school play.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "From the root for “sacred” that also occurs in sanctify (make holy) and sanctuary (sacred place; refuge).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "sacro + sanct: doubly sacred and saintly, not to be touched.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sagacious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "suh-GAY-shuss", "also": "sage (noun)", "d1": "Wise; showing good judgment and foresight", "sent": "It’s important to choose a mentor who is not only successful, but also <b>sagacious</b>—plenty of people are successful through luck and have little insight about how to attack someone else’s situation.", "related": "Sapient (synonym), Prudent (wise in practical matters, carefully providing for the future), Circumspect (cautious, prudent; careful to consider the circumstances and consequences), Perspicacious (very perceptive, insightful)", "moreinfo": "A sage is a respected wise person. (Sage is also an herb).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "sagacious: wise as a sage, shrewd with foresight.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "salubrious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "suh-LOO-bree-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Healthful, promoting health", "sent": "After spending her twenties smoking and drinking, Jessica recognized the necessity of adopting a more <b>salubrious</b> lifestyle, but found it difficult to cut back.", "related": "Salutary is a synonym.", "moreinfo": "Spanish speakers probably recognized this one – it’s very similar to the Spanish “salud.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "salubrious (salu = health, like salud): healthful, good for you.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sanction", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "SANK-shun", "also": "", "d1": "Permission or approval, something that gives support or authority to something else (noun); to allow, confirm, ratify (verb); OR a legal action by one or more countries against another country to get it to comply (noun); to place sanctions or penalties on (verb)", "sent": "Professional boxers may only fight in <b>sanctioned</b> matches—fighting outside the ring is prohibited.\nAmerica’s <b>sanctions</b> on Cuba mean that it is illegal for Americans to do business with Cuban companies.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "This word can be very confusing—its two definitions seem to be opposites. Allow and penalize? We have to use context to figure out the meaning —since the bad meaning (generally “sanctions,” plural) applies to international actions, most usages of sanction (regarding any other topic) mean “allow.” Sanction comes from the root for holy (also in sanctuary, sanctify, etc.). Imagine an ancient society in which the ruler is also the spiritual leader, and it’s easy to imagine how legal sanctions could be thought of as “holy.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "sanction: officially \"okay\" it, OR impose a penalty on it (both senses).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sanguine", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SANG-gwinn", "also": "", "d1": "Cheerfully optimistic, hopeful; reddish, ruddy (as in rosy-red cheeks indicting health or vitality)", "sent": "She had three papers due in three days, but she maintained her typically <b>sanguine</b> attitude. “Things always just work out for me,” she said, happily.", "related": "Chipper (cheerful, healthy and in good spirits)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse sanguine with sanguinary, which comes from the same root but means “bloodthirsty”! The ancient Greeks thought that people were ruled by the “four humors”: blood, yellow bile, black bile, and phlegm. A person with too much phlegm would be phlegmatic (sluggish, gloomy); a person with too much bile would be bilious (bitter, mean); and a person ruled by blood would be sanguine.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "sanguine, ruddy and red-cheeked (sang = blood): cheerfully optimistic.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sap", "pos": "noun, verb", "pron": "SAP", "also": "", "d1": "The inner fluid of a plant or any essential body fluid; energy, vitality; a person taken advantage of (noun); undermine, weaken, tire out (verb)", "sent": "I really thought that if I clicked on that Facebook ad and entered all my information, I would get a free iPad to test and keep! I feel like such a <b>sap</b>.\nIn order to make maple syrup, you must drain <b>sap</b> from a sugar maple tree—they call this “<b>sapping</b>” the tree, which I can understand, because I feel pretty <b>sapped</b> doing it! Fortunately, it’s just a metaphor.", "related": "Enervate (weaken, tire), Enfeeble (weaken, make feeble)", "moreinfo": "The meaning of sap as a gullible or simple person is thought to be related to calling someone a “sapskull”—the idea being that the person’s head is made of soft, sappy wood from the inside of a tree.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "sap: drain the sap and energy out, weaken and tire.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "satiate or sate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SAY-she-ayt or SAYT", "also": "satiety (noun)", "d1": "To fully satisfy; to go beyond satisfying to the point of excess (possibly inducing disgust, tiredness, etc.)", "sent": "I usually just eat a tiny salad or something while I’m at work, but since I had a half day off, I went to the Indian buffet and stayed for a whole hour! I’ve never been more <b>satiated</b> (or <b>sated</b>) in my life.\nTo maintain a healthy weight, stop eating before you reach the point of satiety.", "related": "Blasé (bored from overindulgence), Ennui (feeling of weariness, boredom, etc. often caused by satiety)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "satiate: fully sated and satisfied, even to excess.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "saturate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SATCH-er-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Soak or imbue thoroughly; cause a substance to unite with the greatest possible amount of another substance", "sent": "We got married on a rainy beach, and my dress was <b>saturated</b>. Although the pictures were a bit dark, the photographer was able to increase the <b>saturation</b> in Photoshop, making our wedding photos ultimately look quite vivid!\nI simply cannot dissolve any more sugar into this iced tea—it’s <b>saturated</b>!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Saturate comes from the root “satus,” (meaning “enough”), which also gives us satisfy, satiate, sated, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "saturate: soak it until it cannot hold any more.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "savor", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SAY-ver", "also": "savory (adj)", "d1": "Appreciate fully, taste or smell with pleasure", "sent": "As a parent, it’s important to take a step back and really <b>savor</b> the special moments—those children will grow up sooner than you think!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Savory means appetizing or enjoyable. It can refer to foods that are pungent, salty, etc. (any flavor but sweet) or to that which is morally respectable (a “dirty” joke could be called unsavory). Although these uses are rarer, you can also savor a food by adding flavor to it, or even use savor as a noun, as in “This soup has a wonderful savor.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "savor the flavor: appreciate and enjoy it fully and slowly.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "scant", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SKANT", "also": "scanty (adj)", "d1": "Not enough or barely enough", "sent": "The new intern was <b>scant</b> help at the conference—he disappeared all day to smoke and didn’t seem to realize that he was there to assist his coworkers.\nThe soldiers were always on the verge of hunger, complaining about their <b>scanty</b> rations.", "related": "A modicum, scintilla, iota, mite, or tad of something is a small amount. A paucity or dearth is scarcity or lack.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "scant, scanty: barely enough, too little.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "scathing", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SKAYTHE-ing", "also": "unscathed (adj)", "d1": "Severe, injurious; bitterly harsh or critical (as a remark)", "sent": "The school superintendant gave a <b>scathing</b> criticism of the education bill, calling it “an attack on our community’s children that will surely go down in infamy.”\nHow is it possible that she flew off her bicycle like that and walked away unscathed?", "related": "", "moreinfo": "As you can see from the use of unscathed to mean “not physically injured,” scathing originally referred to physical injury and now generally refers to injurious comments. A word used in a similar metaphorical way is excoriate, which literally means “to rub the skin off of” but is more often used to mean “to criticize very harshly.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "a scathing review scalds and scars: bitterly harsh.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "secular", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SECK-yoo-ler", "also": "", "d1": "Not religious or holy; pertaining to worldly things", "sent": "Forty years ago, American companies wished their employees “Merry Christmas”—even the employees who didn’t celebrate Christmas. Today, the <b>secular</b> “Happy Holidays” is common.\nWestern governments have grown increasingly <b>secular</b> over the last century; many have laws prohibiting religious expression from being sponsored by the government.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Secular doesn’t mean “atheist”—for instance, a devoutly religious person would describe a church as sacred or sanctified and a library as secular.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 17", "setNum": 17, "hook": "secular: worldly and of this age, not religious.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sedulous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SEDD-joo-luss", "also": "", "d1": "Persevering, persistent, diligent in one’s efforts", "sent": "<b>Sedulous</b> effort is necessary to improve your GRE verbal score—you need to study vocab in a serious way, nearly every day.", "related": "Assiduous is a synonym.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "sedulous: \"sits and does it\" steadily, diligent and persistent.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sentient", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SENT-shent", "also": "", "d1": "Conscious; experiencing sensation or perceiving with the senses", "sent": "Tia became a vegan because she refused to eat any <b>sentient</b> creature.\nLook at the mold in your fridge! Let it grow any more, it might become <b>sentient</b>!", "related": "The antonym insensate can man unconscious or inanimate, lacking reason (that is, “sense”), or “cold-blooded,” merciless, lacking warm human feelings. A rock, a very intoxicated person, and a murderer could all be described as insensate.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "sentient: able to sense and feel, conscious.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "simultaneous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "sye-mull-TAY-nee-uss", "also": "", "d1": "At the same time", "sent": "It is rare in a duel that the two shooters draw their guns <b>simultaneously</b> and actually kill each other in an instant.", "related": "Synchronous and concurrent are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "The root “simul” means “together” and also gives us similar and simulcast, meaning to broadcast simultaneously, such as on television and the Internet at the same time.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "simul (same) + taneous: at the same time.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "skeptic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SKEPP-tick", "also": "", "d1": "Person inclined to doubting or questioning generally accepted beliefs", "sent": "I wish you’d be more of a <b>skeptic</b>—I can’t believe you spent money on a pet psychic so we can “talk” to our dearly departed shih tzu.\nDescartes was a great <b>skeptic</b>, famously declaring that we cannot truly be sure of anything except our own existence—hence, “I think, therefore I am.”", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse skeptical and cynical (thinking the worst of others’ motivations; bitterly pessimistic). In a GRE Reading Comprehension passage, an author might be skeptical (a very appropriate attitude for a scientist, for instance), but would never be cynical.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "a skeptic doubts and questions accepted beliefs.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "skirt", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SKERT", "also": "", "d1": "Border, lie along the edge of, go around; evade", "sent": "Melissa spent all of Thanksgiving <b>skirting</b> the issue of who she was dating and when she might get married and make her mother a grandmother. It was exhausting changing the subject two dozen times!\nThe creek <b>skirts</b> our property on the west, so it’s easy to tell where our farm ends.", "related": "Circumvent (go around, avoid, bypass, such as circumventing the rules)", "moreinfo": "This word is a metaphor related to the clothing item skirt, which passes around a person’s body in a somewhat circular manner.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "skirt the issue: go around its edge, evade it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "slack", "pos": "adj, verb, noun", "pron": "SLACK", "also": "", "d1": "Loose, negligent, lazy, weak (adj); neglect to do one’s duties; loosen up, relax (verb); period of little work (noun)", "sent": "As the product of <b>slack</b> parenting, I never learned good time management skills—Mom and Dad never checked my homework or made me go to bed at a certain time.\nThe holidays represent a lull or <b>slack</b> in work at many companies.", "related": "Lax (not strict; careless, loose, slack), Lull (to calm, or a period of calm or quiet)", "moreinfo": "The slang phrase “slacking off” comes from the word slack. In more formal English, “He is slacking off” might be “He is slacking in his duties.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "slack: loose and limp; a slacker neglects duties.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "slew", "pos": "noun", "pron": "SLOO", "also": "", "d1": "A large number or quantity", "sent": "As soon as we switched software packages, we encountered a whole <b>slew</b> of problems.", "related": "Myriad can also mean a very great number of something (probably bigger than a slew).", "moreinfo": "From the Irish “sluagh,” a crowd or army.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "a whole slew of them: a large number, a big bunch.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "slight", "pos": "adj, verb, noun", "pron": "SLITE", "also": "", "d1": "Small, not very important, slender or delicate (adj); treat as though not very important; snub, ignore (verb); an act of treating in this way, a discourtesy (noun)", "sent": "She was very sensitive, always holding a grudge against her coworkers for a variety of <b>slights</b>, both real and imagined.\nNatalie Portman has always been <b>slight</b>, but she became even thinner to portray a ballerina in Black Swan.\nI felt <b>slighted</b> when my husband told you about his promotion before he told me.", "related": "Nominal (trivial, so small as to be unimportant; in name only, so-called), Inconsequential (insignificant, unimportant)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "slight: tiny and minor; or to slight someone is to snub them.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "solicitous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "suh-LISS-sit-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Concerned or anxious (about another person), expressing care; eager or desirous; very careful", "sent": "A <b>solicitous</b> host, Derek not only asked each person how he or she was doing, but asked by name about everyone’s spouses and kids.\n<b>Solicitous</b> of fame, she would do anything to get near celebrities.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse solicitous with soliciting, which means selling, asking or entreating, or acting as a prostitute. Signs on homes and businesses that say “No Soliciting” are telling salespeople to stay away.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "solicitous: anxiously caring, always asking after you.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "soporific", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "sah-per-RIFF-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Causing sleep; sleepy, drowsy (adj); something that causes sleep (noun)", "sent": "I was excited to take a class with Professor Baria because I had enjoyed her books, but sadly, she is a better writer than speaker—her lectures are <b>soporific</b>.\nI was so distressed after the crash that the doctor gave me a <b>soporific</b>—and, sure enough, I was able to think more clearly after sleeping.", "related": "Somnolent is a synonym.", "moreinfo": "Sopor is a rare word meaning an abnormally deep sleep. A much more common word is the similar sounding stupor, meaning a state of numbness or not making sense (for instance, a drunken stupor).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "soporific \"so-poor-ific\": so boring it puts you to sleep.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sound", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SOUND", "also": "", "d1": "Measure the depth of (usually of water) as with a sounding line; penetrate and discover the meaning of, understand (usually as sound the depths)", "sent": "The psychiatrist appointed by the court felt he would need more time to <b>sound</b> the depths of the defendant’s tortured mind—clearly, she was mentally ill, but did she know on any level that what she was doing was wrong?", "related": "Fathom and Plumb (used as verbs) are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "A sounding line or plumb line is a length of rope with a weight at the bottom; dropping it into water will allow the weight to sink and the depth of the water to be measured.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "sound the depths: measure how deep, or fully grasp it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "spartan", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SPAR-tin", "also": "", "d1": "Very disciplined and stern; frugal, living simply, austere; suggestive of the ancient Spartans", "sent": "A young soldier in the <b>spartan</b> environment of boot camp can really long for a home-cooked meal or even just a comfortable couch to sit on.\nHer apartment was so <b>spartan</b> that she couldn’t even serve us both soup—she only had one bowl and one spoon. Instead, we sat in hard-backed chairs and drank water.", "related": "Ascetic (pertaining to a simple life with no luxuries, such as that of a monk), Austere (severe in manner or appearance; very self-disciplined)", "moreinfo": "The Spartans (ancient Greeks from Sparta) were portrayed in the 2006 movie 300, starring Gerard Butler. The movie isn’t very historically accurate, but the Spartans certainly were fierce warriors known for their harsh training methods.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "spartan, like a Spartan warrior: bare, stern, and disciplined.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "spate", "pos": "noun", "pron": "SPAYT", "also": "", "d1": "Sudden outpouring or rush; flood", "sent": "After a brief <b>spate</b> of post-exam partying, Lola is ready for classes to begin again.\nHe was so furious that a <b>spate</b> of expletives just flew out of his mouth.", "related": "Inundate and deluge are both words for flooding, also used metaphorically to mean an overwhelming influx of something (inundated with work, a deluge of complaints).", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "a spate of storms: a sudden flood or rush of them.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "spearhead", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SPEER-hedd", "also": "", "d1": "Be the leader of", "sent": "Lisa agreed to <b>spearhead</b> the “healthy office” initiative, and was instrumental in installing two treadmills and getting healthy food stocked in the vending machines.", "related": "Avant-garde or vanguard originally referred to the soldiers at the front of an army, and now can mean anyone who innovates or is on the forefront (as in the expression “ahead of their time”).", "moreinfo": "A spearhead can, of course, be the sharp head of a spear. It can also be a person at the front of a military attack, or a leader of anything.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "spearhead: be the tip of the spear, lead the charge.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "specious", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SPEE-shuss", "also": "", "d1": "Seemingly true but actually false; deceptively attractive", "sent": "“All squares are rectangles, all candy bars are rectangles, therefore all squares are candy bars” is clearly a <b>specious</b> argument.", "related": "Fallacious (containing a fallacy, or mistake in logic; logically unsound; deceptive), Dubious (doubtful, questionable, suspect), Meretricious (attractive in a vulgar or flashy way, tawdry; deceptive)", "moreinfo": "Specious is related to the word species, from a Latin root having to do with appearances.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "specious, good on the surface (spec = appear): seems true but is false.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "spectrum", "pos": "noun", "pron": "SPECK-trum", "also": "", "d1": "A broad range of nevertheless related qualities or ideas, esp. those that overlap to create a continuous series (as in a color spectrum, where each color blends into the next in a continuous way)", "sent": "A test showed she was partially colorblind – she did see blues and greens, but was unable to perceive some other colors on the <b>spectrum</b>.\nA “dialect continuum” is a <b>spectrum</b> of dialects of a language where speakers in different dialect groups can understand some, but not all, of the other groups—for instance, people in the west can understand people in the middle, and people in the middle can understand people in the east, but people in the west and the east cannot talk to one another.", "related": "Panoply (splendid, wide-ranging, impressive display or array), Scope (extent or range, such as of knowledge, effectiveness, authority, etc.)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "a spectrum: the full rainbow range of related shades.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "speculate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SPECK-you-layt", "also": "", "d1": "Contemplate; make a guess or educated guess about; engage in a risky business transaction, gamble", "sent": "During the Gold Rush, <b>speculators</b> bought up land, sometimes with borrowed funds, expecting to prospect the land for gold and get rich quickly.\nShe <b>speculated</b> that, in zero gravity, showers would have to exist as closed rooms in which a giant bubble of water formed, and a person got inside it.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Speculate comes from a root (“look at”) also found in spectacle, spectator, inspect, and many others. To speculate is to try to “see” into the future or a situation about which not much is known.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "speculate (spec = look): eye it and guess, or gamble on it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sporadic", "pos": "adj", "pron": "spore-AD-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Occasional, happening irregularly or in scattered locations", "sent": "Her attendance was <b>sporadic</b> at best, so when she flounced into class after a two-session absence, she discovered that not only was everyone working on group projects, but that the professor hadn’t even thought to assign her to a group.", "related": "Erratic (inconsistent, wandering, having no fixed course), Desultory (lacking consistency or order, disconnected, sporadic; going off topic)", "moreinfo": "Sporadic is related to the idea of spores, reproductive structures that come off of plants, mushrooms, etc. and are spread, such as by the wind.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "sporadic: scattered like spores, occasional and irregular.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sportive", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SPORT-iv", "also": "", "d1": "Playful, merry, joking around, done “in sport” (rather than intended seriously)", "sent": "After Will shot a ball entirely off the pool table, knocking a woman’s purse off a bar stool, his friends laughed hysterically and called him “purse-snatcher” all night, but he took it as <b>sportive</b> and bought the next round of drinks.", "related": "Jocular, jocose, and jocund (joking or given to joking all the time; jolly, playful), Waggish (merry, roguish), Risible (laughable, related to laughing)", "moreinfo": "Sportive certainly is related to sports and occasionally means “pertaining to athletics.” Also related is the idea of being a “good sport,” which generally involves having a sense of humor about yourself. Something done “in sport” is meant playfully.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "sportive: done in sport and fun, playful.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "standing", "pos": "noun, adj", "pron": "STAND-ing", "also": "", "d1": "Status, rank, reputation (noun); existing indefinitely, not movable (adj)", "sent": "As he had feared, his divorce greatly reduced his <b>standing</b> as a relationship expert.\nI’m disappointed that you don’t want to leave your current job, but I want you to know that you have a <b>standing</b> invitation—as long as I’m the boss, you have a job here anytime you want one.\nWhile the U.S. has a <b>standing</b> army (that is, an army that is not disbanded in times of peace), Costa Rica’s constitution actually forbids a <b>standing</b> military.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "“Longstanding” and the expressions “of long standing” or “in good standing” are based on standing, as in a longstanding friendship or an account in good standing (generally an account for which all debts are paid).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "standing: your rank and status; a standing offer stays put.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "stark", "pos": "adj", "pron": "STARK", "also": "", "d1": "Complete, total, utter; harsh or grim; extremely simple, severe, blunt, or plain", "sent": "The designer’s work is appreciated for its <b>stark</b> beauty, but most people prefer to live in a cozier, more welcoming home—the kind with carpets and pillows, for instance.\nShe is <b>stark</b> raving mad!\nThe <b>stark</b> reality is that we will have to begin burning our furniture for warmth if we are to survive.", "related": "Austere (severe in manner or appearance; very self-disciplined, ascetic; without luxury or ease; sober or serious)", "moreinfo": "A common expression is “stark naked” (here, stark adds emphasis). In British slang, “starkers” (American: “streakers”) are people who run naked through a public place for amusement. In many cultures, such an act would be a stark violation of public decency.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "stark naked; a stark, bare, harsh, blunt contrast.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "static", "pos": "adj", "pron": "STATT-ick", "also": "", "d1": "Fixed, not moving or changing, lacking vitality", "sent": "The anthropologist studied a society in the Amazon that had been deliberately <b>static</b> for hundreds of years—the fiercely proud people disdained change, and viewed all new ideas as inferior to the way of life they had always practiced.", "related": "Status Quo (existing state or condition), Stasis (equilibrium, a state of balance or inactivity, esp. caused by equal but opposing forces), Standing (existing indefinitely, not movable, as in a standing invitation), Stationary (not moving)", "moreinfo": "The idea of television or radio static is obviously more recent—think of the fact that static keeps whatever you were watching or listening to from moving forward.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "static: stuck still, no motion or change (opposite of dynamic).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "status quo", "pos": "noun", "pron": "STATT-uss or STAY-tuss QUOH", "also": "", "d1": "Existing state or condition", "sent": "Many opposed the establishment of a needle-exchange program, but others reasoned that the plan would be an improvement on the <b>status quo</b>, in which disease spread rapidly through certain communities.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Latin for “state in which.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "status quo: the state in which things currently stand.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "stingy", "pos": "adj", "pron": "STINN-jee", "also": "", "d1": "Not generous with money, reluctant to spend or give", "sent": "Billionaire industrialist J. Paul Getty was so famously <b>stingy</b> that he installed pay phones in his mansion for guests to use. When his grandson was kidnapped, he refused to pay ransom and only changed his mind when the kidnappers cut off the boy’s ear. This famous cheapskate then demanded that his son (the boy’s father) pay him back! What a miser.", "related": "Miser, Cheapskate, Skinflint (stingy person), Frugal (economical, thrifty, not wasteful with money), Stint (to be frugal)", "moreinfo": "A “scrooge” is also a miserly person, especially a wealthy one, after the character of Ebenezer Scrooge in Charles Dickens’ A Christmas Carol.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "stingy: a tightfisted miser, reluctant to spend or give.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "stoic or stoical", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "STOH-ick or STOH-ick-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Indifferent to pleasure or pain, enduring without complaint; person indifferent to pleasure or pain (noun)", "sent": "Della was not only calm but positively <b>stoic</b>, thanking her family for driving her home from chemotherapy, and then simply never mentioning it until it was time to go again.", "related": "Impassive (not having or not showing physical feeling or emotion), Inscrutable (not able to be scrutinized, mysterious), Stolid (showing little emotion)", "moreinfo": "The Stoics were ancient Greek philosophers who believed that self-control could overcome destructive emotions. They compared wicked, emotionally driven people to “a dog tied to a cart,” forced in all directions.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 18", "setNum": 18, "hook": "a stoic takes pain and pleasure alike with calm indifference.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "stolid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "STAH-lid", "also": "", "d1": "Unemotional, showing little emotion, not easily moved", "sent": "Dad is so <b>stolid</b> that we can’t get a rise out of him no matter what we do—Jody got a tattoo, Max declared himself a communist, and Helen won a Rhodes Scholarship. No response! Dad just nods and says “Alright, then.”", "related": "Impassive (not having or not showing physical feeling or emotion), Inscrutable (not able to be scrutinized, mysterious)", "moreinfo": "Stolid has a lot of overlap with stoic (indifferent to pleasure or pain). Stoic is generally stronger and often related to enduring suffering.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "stolid: solid and stone-faced, showing little emotion.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "stymie or stymy", "pos": "verb, noun", "pron": "STY-mee", "also": "", "d1": "Block, hinder, or thwart (verb); an obstacle (noun)", "sent": "Sara feared that her learning disability would <b>stymie</b> her success in college, but the support services offered were excellent and she was fine academically; the thing that really <b>stymied</b> her college career was poor time management.", "related": "Impede, hamper (hold back, obstruct the progress of)", "moreinfo": "This word originates from golf. In golf, a stymie is when an opponent’s ball lies in the way of getting your own ball into the cup.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "stymie: stump and block, throw up an obstacle.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "subjective", "pos": "adj", "pron": "sub-JECK-tiv", "also": "", "d1": "Existing in the mind or relating to one’s own thoughts, opinions, emotions, etc.; personal, individual, based on feelings", "sent": "Naturally, anyone’s experience of a movie is <b>subjective</b>, and some will enjoy this picture despite its flaws; however, it is an objective fact that the cinematography is very bad.\nWe can give names to colors, but we can never quite convey the <b>subjective</b> experience of them—what if my “red” is different from your “red”?", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The opposite of subjective is objective (factual, related to reality or physical objects; not influenced by emotions, unbiased)", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "subjective: about the subject (you), your own personal view.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "subside", "pos": "verb", "pron": "sub-SIDE", "also": "", "d1": "Sink, settle down, become less active; return to a normal level", "sent": "When her terror <b>subsided</b>, she realized that the house wasn’t really haunted.\nIt is a chronic illness—symptoms will flare up and <b>subside</b> over one’s lifetime.", "related": "Abate (reduce, diminish)", "moreinfo": "From the Latin for “sink to the bottom,” subside is related to sediment (material at the bottom of a liquid) and sedentary (not moving around, as a person with an inactive lifestyle).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "sub (down) + side: settle down, the storm subsides.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "substantiate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "sub-STAN-chee-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Support with evidence or proof; give a material existence to", "sent": "You say you were at home when the crime occurred two towns over—is there anyone who can <b>substantiate</b> your claim?\nYour business ideas are interesting, but you never <b>substantiate</b> them—you haven’t put a single plan into action.", "related": "Corroborate (support, add evidence to), Verify (prove true)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "substantiate: give it substance and evidence, back it up.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "succeeding", "pos": "adj", "pron": "suck-SEED-ing", "also": "", "d1": "Coming after or following", "sent": "After the sale of the company, you will receive 5% of the profits from the current year, and 1% in all <b>succeeding</b> years.\nIn 1797, George Washington was succeeded by John Adams.", "related": "Subsequent is a synonym and often appears as the adverb subsequently, as in He took the poison and subsequently fell into a coma.", "moreinfo": "Relatedly, a successor is someone who comes after, usually in holding an office, as in After losing his bid for reelection, the outgoing President handed over the White House to his successor.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "succeeding: coming after, following in succession.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "supersede", "pos": "verb", "pron": "soo-per-SEED", "also": "", "d1": "Replace, take the position of, cause to be disregarded as void or obsolete", "sent": "Of course, electric washing machines <b>superseded</b> hand-powered ones many decades ago, but my great-grandmother used her hand-cranked washer until she died in the 1990s.", "related": "Supplant (take the place of, displace, especially through sneaky tactics), Outstrip (surpass, exceed; be larger or better than; leave behind), Overshadow (cast a shadow over, make to seem less important), Supersede (replace or cause to be set aside), Eclipse (obscure, darken, make less important)", "moreinfo": "“Super” is Latin for “above, beyond” and appears in many words that have a sense of being literally or figuratively on top: superficial, superimpose, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "super + sede (sit): sit above and replace the old one.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "supplicate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "SUPP-lick-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Pray humbly; ask, beg, or seek in a humble way", "sent": "She had been estranged from her wealthy father for years, but when she needed money for her daughter’s medical care, she <b>supplicated</b> the old man for assistance.", "related": "Entreat, Beseech, and Implore are synonyms.", "moreinfo": "Supplicate is related to supple (pliant, flexible). To supplicate is to attempt to “soften” another person or get that person to “bend” to your request.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "supplicate: drop to supple knees and beg or pray humbly.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "surfeit", "pos": "noun", "pron": "SURF-it", "also": "", "d1": "Excess, excessive amount, overindulgence", "sent": "The soup kitchen would like to announce that it has a serious <b>surfeit</b> of those cans of jellied cranberries that no one seems to want, but it could still use at least ten Thanksgiving turkeys.", "related": "Replete with (supplied in abundance, filled, gorged), Glut, Surplus and Plethora (excess, overabundance)", "moreinfo": "“Sur” means “over” and the rest of the word is related to the Latin “facere,” meaning “to make.” The literal meaning is overproduce.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "surfeit, a \"sur-fit\" overload: an excess, overindulgence.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "surmise", "pos": "verb", "pron": "ser-MIZE", "also": "", "d1": "Guess, infer, think or make an opinion with incomplete information", "sent": "Based on your rather sad attempt to figure out the tip on our restaurant bill, I would <b>surmise</b> that you actually have no idea how percents work.", "related": "Conjecture (synonym), Supposition (assumption, hypothesis, something that has been supposed)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "sur (over) + mise: toss a guess over it, infer with little info.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "sycophant", "pos": "noun", "pron": "SICK-uh-fint", "also": "", "d1": "Servile flatterer, parasitic person who fawns in order to get ahead", "sent": "Stop being such a <b>sycophant</b>. I don’t need you to compliment my tie or get me coffee; I just need you to do your job without bothering me.", "related": "Lackey, Toady, and Myrmidon are synonyms. Fawn means to try to please in a submissive way. Obsequious means “servile, very compliant, fawning.” Truckle means to act subserviently and Kowtow means to fawn—or, literally, to bow until one’s forehead touches the floor. In slang, we might call a sycophant a boot-licker, suck-up, etc.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "a \"psycho fan\" fawns and flatters to get ahead.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "synchronous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "SINK-run-nuss", "also": "", "d1": "Happening at the same time; occurring at the same rate and thus happening together repeatedly", "sent": "The sound of that bell is a signal for the dancers to make perfectly <b>synchronous</b> entrances from opposite sides of the stage.\nThe two nearby churches have <b>synchronous</b> church bells—if you stand halfway between the buildings as the bells chime out the hour, it sounds really cool!", "related": "Simultaneous and contemporaneous also mean occurring at the same time. Contemporaneous is appropriate for describing people in the same time period (Charlie Chaplin and Mary Pickford had contemporaneous careers).", "moreinfo": "The root “sym/syn” means “together” and also appears in symbiosis, sympathy, etc.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "syn (together) + chron (time): at the same time, in sync.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "table", "pos": "verb", "pron": "TAY-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Lay aside to discuss later, often as a way to postpone discussion indefinitely", "sent": "I see we’re not going to agree on whether to scrap our entire curriculum and develop a new one, so let’s <b>table</b> that discussion and move on to voting on the budget.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "In American English, to table something means to postpone discussion of it until later, but in British English, to table a bill is the opposite—to submit it for consideration.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "to table it: lay it on the table for later, postpone the discussion.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "tacit", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TASS-it", "also": "", "d1": "Understood without being said; implied, not stated directly; silent", "sent": "Her parents never told her she could smoke, but they gave their <b>tacit</b> consent when they didn’t say anything about the obvious smell coming from her bedroom.", "related": "Implicit (implied, not stated directly; involved in the very essence of something, unquestionable)", "moreinfo": "Tacit is related to taciturn (not talking much, reserved).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "tacit, silent (tac = silent): understood without being said.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "taciturn", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TASS-it-turn", "also": "", "d1": "Not talking much, reserved; silent, holding back in conversation", "sent": "Because he felt self-conscious about his stutter, Mike had always been <b>taciturn</b>, but after some very good speech therapy, soon he was much more voluble.", "related": "Reticent is a synonym. Laconic means “using few words, concise.”", "moreinfo": "Taciturn is related to tacit (understood without being said; implied, not stated directly; silent).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "taciturn: tight-lipped and turned inward, reserved and silent.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "tangential", "pos": "adj", "pron": "tan-JEN-chull", "also": "", "d1": "Only slightly relevant, going off-topic", "sent": "It’s hard to get a quick answer out of Noah—ask him any question, and you’ll get a wide range of <b>tangential</b> remarks before you can find a polite way to move on.", "related": "Penumbra (outer part of a shadow from an eclipse; any surrounding region, fringe, periphery; any area where something “sort of” exists), Digress or Divagate (go off-topic when speaking or writing)", "moreinfo": "In math, a tangent line touches a curve and then continues on, forever—much like many people we wish would stop talking.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "tangential: flying off on a tangent, only slightly relevant.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "temperance", "pos": "noun", "pron": "TEMP-er-enss", "also": "temper (verb)", "d1": "Moderation, self-control, esp. regarding alcohol or other desires or pleasures; total abstinence from alcohol", "sent": "After the end of the Civil War, economic change led to an increase in alcohol problems and the birth of the <b>Temperance</b> Movement, which ultimately led to Prohibition.\nGrandma is a model of <b>temperance</b>—she drinks red wine every night, but only the 1/3 of a glass that she read was conducive to preventing heart attacks.", "related": "Teetotaler (person who doesn’t drink alcohol at all), Abstain (hold back, refrain, esp. from something bad or unhealthy), Sobriety (temperance or the state of being sober; seriousness)", "moreinfo": "To temper is to moderate, soften, or tone down, or to make less intense. Something untempered is not controlled or moderated. Of course, temper as a noun means a person’s state of mind or tendency to anger.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "temperance: tempering your desires, moderation (esp. with alcohol).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "tenuous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TEN-yoo-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Long and thin, slender; flimsy, having little substance", "sent": "Your argument is quite <b>tenuous</b>—it depends on our accepting the results of a 1955 study published in an obscure medical journal not subject to peer review.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "The related attenuate means “weaken or thin out” (for instance, a general who sends too few troops over too large an area has attenuated his army). The related tensile means “relating to tension” or “capable of being stretched.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "tenuous, thin and stretched (ten = stretch): flimsy, a weak link.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "timely", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TIME-lee", "also": "", "d1": "Well-timed, happening at a suitable time", "sent": "Your arrival is quite <b>timely</b>—we were just mulling over a question we’re sure you can answer!\nHis <b>timely</b> departure prevented him from having to do any work.", "related": "Opportune can be a synonym for timely, or can mean “favorable, appropriate.”", "moreinfo": "While timely ends in –ly, it is NOT an adverb. You therefore cannot “do something timely”—you must instead “do it in a timely manner.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "timely: right on time, well-timed.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "timorous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TIM-er-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Fearful, timid", "sent": "An expression describing a <b>timorous</b> person is “quaking in his boots”—that is, a scared person would shake or shiver from fear.", "related": "Intrepid means fearless and did, in fact, come from the rare word trepid, meaning fearful. Craven and pusillanimous mean cowardly.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "timorous, full of timor (fear): timid and easily scared.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "tirade", "pos": "noun", "pron": "tie-RAYD", "also": "", "d1": "Bitter, abusive criticism or verbal attack", "sent": "I hate that television show where that commentator goes on angry <b>tirades</b> about all the liberal conspiracies taking over America. Even my conservative friends find his ranting embarrassing.", "related": "Diatribe, Tirade, Harangue, and Fulmination are all words for bitter, angry speeches or attacks.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "a tirade: a long, bitter, ranting verbal attack.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "torpor", "pos": "noun", "pron": "TORE-pore", "also": "torpid (adj)", "d1": "Sluggishness, lethargy, or apathy; a period of inactivity", "sent": "Sam had hoped to be able to play in the game after having his wisdom teeth out, but the anesthesia left him in such <b>torpor</b> that he obviously couldn’t play soccer.", "related": "Listless (spiritless, lacking interest or energy), Inertia (inactivity, lack of motion or progress), Loafing (idling away the time, lounging)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse torpid with turbid (turbulent), tepid (lukewarm), or turgid or tumid (swollen, bombastic).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "torpor: torpid stillness, like a bear's dull hibernation.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "torrid", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TORE-id", "also": "", "d1": "Very hot, parching, burning; passionate", "sent": "They had a <b>torrid</b> love affair in the ‘80s, but split up because a royal was not permitted to marry a commoner.\nThe wandering refugees were in serious danger in the <b>torrid</b> Sahara.", "related": "Arid (dry, parched, barren)", "moreinfo": "The “torrid zone” is the region of Earth between the tropics.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "torrid: scorching hot (the Torrid Zone), or a torrid romance.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "tractable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TRACK-tib-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Easily controlled or managed, docile; easily shaped or molded", "sent": "He’s a <b>tractable</b> fellow—when I asked if we could see a different movie than the one we’d come to see, he shrugged and said “Cool.”\nThe clay had hardened overnight, but adding water made it <b>tractable</b> again.", "related": "Malleable, Pliable, and Plastic can also mean physically bendable, or metaphorically bendable, as in “easily influenced or shaped by others.” Mutable means changeable. Complaisant means “obliging, eager to please.”", "moreinfo": "“Tract” in Latin means “manage, handle” and also occurs in treat and tractor.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "tractable, easy to pull along (tract = pull): docile, manageable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "transitory", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TRAN-zit-tore-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Temporary, short-lived, not lasting", "sent": "While a few people marry their high school sweethearts, generally, our teenage years are full of <b>transitory</b> crushes.", "related": "Ephemeral, Evanescent and Fugacious are synonyms. Transient can mean “lasting only a short time, temporary” or “staying only a short time,” or can be a noun referring to people who move from place to place.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 19", "setNum": 19, "hook": "transitory: just passing through in transit, short-lived.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "trifling", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TRIFE-ling", "also": "trifle (noun)", "d1": "Trivial, not very important; so small as to be unimportant; frivolous, shallow", "sent": "Luis broke up with Cara because she was always obsessed with some <b>trifling</b> matter—he tried to talk about foreign aid dependency, and she changed the subject to what the actress Katie Holmes dressed her daughter Suri in for a shopping trip.\nThe young heiress was so wealthy that she considered the salary from her internship a mere trifle, and didn’t even notice when her paycheck was days late.", "related": "Frivolous (lacking seriousness, excessively carefree, as in The judge dismissed the lawsuit as frivolous—you can’t sue the government for failing to protect you from birds). Nugatory also means “of little or no importance.”", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "trifling: a mere trifle, trivial and unimportant.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "trite", "pos": "adj", "pron": "TRITE", "also": "", "d1": "Lacking freshness and originality, lacking effectiveness due to overuse, cliché", "sent": "The topic of your speech is “Children are the Future”? That’s pretty <b>trite</b>. Maybe you should think harder and come up with something original.", "related": "Banal, Hackneyed, Inane, and Insipid all mean “lacking freshness and originality, shallow.” Shopworn also means this (or can also literally mean something marred from being handled in a store). Bromide and Platitude are nouns for a cliché or shopworn expression.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "trite: worn down by overuse, a stale cliche.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "ubiquitous", "pos": "adj", "pron": "yoo-BICK-wit-uss", "also": "", "d1": "Existing everywhere at the same time", "sent": "Thirty years ago, women would lie about coloring their hair, considering the admission embarrassing. Today, blatantly fake hair color—not to mention fake tans and unnaturally white teeth— are <b>ubiquitous</b>, not only on starlets, but even on the checkers at the local grocery store.\nMaggie learned in Sunday school that God was <b>ubiquitous</b>, leading to the question nearly all children ask when they hear this: “Does that mean God sees me in the bathroom?”", "related": "Omnipresent (synonym), Pervasive (tending to spread throughout)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "ubiquitous: seemingly everywhere at once (like Starbucks).", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "undermine", "pos": "verb", "pron": "un-der-MINE", "also": "", "d1": "Weaken, cause to collapse by digging away at the foundation (of a building or an argument); injure or attack in a secretive or underhanded way", "sent": "Rather than searching impartially for the truth, these pharmaceutical company “scientists” willfully ignored any evidence that <b>undermined</b> the conclusion they were being paid to produce.\nYou are nice to my face, but you are <b>undermining</b> me behind my back, suggesting to others in the office that I am making mistakes in my work and that you have been fixing them!", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse with undergird (strengthen, add support) and underscore (emphasize). Undermine literally comes from the idea of “mining” underneath—certainly, mining under your house would be likely to make the house fall down.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "undermine: dig a mine underneath, weaken the foundation.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "underscore", "pos": "verb", "pron": "un-der-SCORE", "also": "", "d1": "Emphasize (or, literally, to underline text)", "sent": "“You’re not going to mess with Joey anymore,” said Joey. His new bodyguards stepped forward threatening, as though to <b>underscore</b> Joey’s point.", "related": "Undergird means strengthen, support.", "moreinfo": "Underscore literally can mean to underline, as in a word processing program—of course, we do this for emphasis. Don’t confuse underscore with the antonym undermine, “to weaken or remove the support for; to injure or attack in a sneaky way.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "underscore: underline it, emphasize it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "unearth", "pos": "verb", "pron": "un-EARTH", "also": "", "d1": "Dig up, uncover, expose", "sent": "The ACLU’s Prison Project works tirelessly to <b>unearth</b> evidence from old cases that might exonerate innocent people who have spent years or even decades in prison.\nThe archaeologist <b>unearthed</b> what appears to be the world’s oldest known gravesite, showing that the earliest humans cared for their deceased loved ones.", "related": "Debunk (expose, ridicule, or disprove false or exaggerated claims), Disabuse (free someone from a mistake in thinking)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "un + earth: pull it out of the earth, dig up and expose.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "unequivocal", "pos": "adj", "pron": "un-ee-KWIH-vuh-cull", "also": "", "d1": "Unambiguous, clear, absolute; having only one possible meaning", "sent": "Although Chuck denied he had a problem, his family was <b>unequivocal</b> in demanding that he check into rehab. Chuck knew there was no getting around it this time.\nMark equivocated, going back and forth on the issue, but his brother Ben was <b>unequivocal</b>—he knew what he wanted and went and got it.", "related": "Resolute or Unambivalent (firmly determined), Resolution (the quality of being firmly determined; resolving to do something; a formal judgment, esp. decided by a vote), Patent (obvious, apparent, plain to see), Explicit (direct, clear, fully revealed)", "moreinfo": "The base word equivocal contains the roots for “equal” and “voice”—giving equal voice to all of the options would make it really hard to decide.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "un (not) + equivocal (ambiguous): no double meaning, absolutely clear.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "unprecedented", "pos": "adj", "pron": "un-PRESS-uh-den-ted", "also": "", "d1": "Never before known or seen, without having happened previously", "sent": "When Nixon resigned, American bravado was at an all-time low—the resignation of a sitting President was disgraceful and <b>unprecedented</b>.", "related": "Novel (new, fresh, original)", "moreinfo": "It’s not hard to unpack this word—the base word precedented looks a lot like precede, “to come before.” If something is unprecedented, nothing has come before it. In law, a precedent is a ruling in a similar case that a judge would draw on or reference in writing a decision.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "un + precedent: no precedent, never happened before.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "unseemly", "pos": "adj", "pron": "un-SEEM-lee", "also": "", "d1": "Improper, inappropriate, against the rules of taste or politeness", "sent": "The activist really did want to get the candidate’s support for the equal-rights measure, so she did what the candidate’s aides asked—but she found it very <b>unseemly</b> that they suggested a specific dollar amount for the “donation” she was asked to make in order to get a meeting.", "related": "Indecorous (synonym), Boorish or Churlish (rude, ill-mannered, insensitive), Uncouth (having bad manners, awkward), Gauche (tactless, lacking social grace, awkward, crude)", "moreinfo": "The antonym seemly means suitable, proper, appropriate, or even handsome.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "un + seemly: it does not \"seem\" proper, indecent.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "vacillate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "VASS-ill-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Waver in one’s mind or opinions, be indecisive", "sent": "In need of a good used car, I was <b>vacillating</b> between the Ford and the Hyundai until a recommendation from a friend helped me decide.", "related": "Equivocate (use unclear language to deceive or avoid committing to a position), Ambivalent (uncertain; unable to decide, or wanting to do two contradictory things at once), Waffle (waver, be indecisive), Dither (act irresolutely), Tergiversate (repeatedly change one’s opinions, equivocate) Memory Trick: Vacillate sounds a bit like Vaseline. When you vacillate, your decisions are quite slippery, as though coated in petroleum jelly.", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "vacillate: wobble back and forth, unable to decide.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "venerate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "VENN-er-ayt", "also": "venerable (adj)", "d1": "Revere, regard with deep respect and awe", "sent": "The boys were utterly crushed when the baseball player they <b>venerated</b> saw them waiting and refused to sign an autograph.", "related": "Revere (feel or express very deep respect and awe)", "moreinfo": "Something venerable is worthy of great respect and admiration and is possessed of great dignity usually associated with age or longstanding. That is, you can venerate anything—Mariah Carey, for instance—but if you call her venerable, she might be a bit insulted that you think she’s old. Venerable often appears in the phrase “venerable institution” (said of Harvard, for instance).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "venerate: treat as venerable, revere with deep respect.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "veracity", "pos": "noun", "pron": "ver-RASS-it-ee", "also": "veracious (adj)", "d1": "Truthfulness, accuracy; habitual adherence to the truth", "sent": "I question the <b>veracity</b> of your story—I just don’t think you’ve been to outer space.\nShe was known for her <b>veracity</b> only because she had no choice—she was a terrible liar.", "related": "Probity (honesty, integrity), Verisimilitude (having the appearance of truth), Verity (the quality of being true)", "moreinfo": "Don’t confuse veracious (true) with voracious (hungry, ravenous).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "veracity, the quality of being \"very\" true (ver = truth): truthfulness.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "verbose", "pos": "adj", "pron": "ver-BOH-ss", "also": "", "d1": "Wordy", "sent": "Twitter’s 140 character limit really forces the <b>verbose</b> to go against their natural tendencies and instead write succinctly.", "related": "Loquacious, Prolix, and Voluble mean “talkative, wordy.”", "moreinfo": "The Latin “verbum” means “word” and of course gives us verb, verbal, etc., and also verbiage (excess words or style of expressing something in words). A verbose person could stand to cut down on the verbiage.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "verbose: overflowing with words, wordy.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "viable", "pos": "adj", "pron": "VYE-uh-bull", "also": "", "d1": "Capable of living (or growing, developing, etc.); practical, workable", "sent": "I have three screenplay ideas, but the studio head said only one was commercially <b>viable</b>. Apparently, it’s important to have a human lead character and a satisfying ending.\nDue to leaps forward in technology, premature babies are considered <b>viable</b> earlier and earlier—currently around 24 weeks.", "related": "Feasible (possible; logical or likely; suitable), Plausible (credible, having the appearance of truth)", "moreinfo": "The Latin root for “life” (“vi”) gives us vivid, survive, revive, vivacious, and convivial. And also the Spanish word for “life” and thus the Ricky Martin classic, “Livin’ La Vida Loca.”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "viable, able to live (vita = life): workable, practical.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "vintage", "pos": "adj, noun", "pron": "VIN-tij", "also": "", "d1": "Related to items of high quality from a previous era, old-fashioned, antique (adj); the wine of a particular year (noun)", "sent": "He didn’t want just any <b>vintage</b> Darth Vader action figure—he wanted a particular <b>vintage</b>, the 1978 telescoping lightsaber one.\nShe special-ordered her favorite <b>vintage</b> of the Côtes du Rhône, then turned to her George Clooney-lookalike husband and joked that she liked men of a certain <b>vintage</b> as well.", "related": "Retro is an informal word used to describe the best of earlier times (often related to fashion, design, etc.)", "moreinfo": "Vintage originated in relation to wine, and the same root appears in vine and vintner (winemaker).", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "vintage: a fine old year's wine, high-quality and classic.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "virtual", "pos": "adj", "pron": "VER-choo-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Existing only in the mind or by means of a computer network; existing in results or in essence but not officially or in name", "sent": "The Tamagotchi is a handheld <b>virtual</b> pet made in Japan—you have to “care” for it by performing various actions with the device, but in the end, your “pet” still looks very much like a keychain.", "related": "Nominal can mean “in name only” but not in fact. In this way, it is something of an opposite to virtual, which can mean not in name, but existing in reality (The abused maid was nominally a free person, but kept as a virtual slave). De facto means “in fact, actually” (but not in name, as in “Some allege that Edith Wilson was the de facto—or virtual—President after her husband Woodrow was incapacitated by a stroke”).", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "virtual: in effect but not in name, or existing on a computer.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "vituperate", "pos": "verb", "pron": "vye-TOO-per-ayt", "also": "", "d1": "Verbally abuse, rebuke or criticize harshly", "sent": "All couples fight, but your girlfriend <b>vituperates</b> you so severely that I’m not sure she loves you at all. Verbal abuse is actually a pretty good reason to break up.", "related": "Pejorative (disparaging, derogatory, belittling), Revile (verbally abuse or speak very badly of), Berate (scold angrily and at length)", "moreinfo": "Vituperate comes from the Latin root “vitium,” (“fault”), which also occurs in vice (sin, wickedness) and vitiate (ruin, corrupt). Vice (or vitiating your things) would be one reason for vituperating someone.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "vituperate: spew vitriol, verbally abuse and harshly criticize.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "volatile", "pos": "adj", "pron": "VAH-luh-tile", "also": "", "d1": "Varying, inconstant, fleeting; tending to violence, explosive;", "sent": "Following the sudden revolution, the political environment in the country was so <b>volatile</b> that anything could have started a riot.\nStock prices are by nature <b>volatile</b>—if you want a “safe and steady” investment, try mutual funds.", "related": "Inconstancy (Fickleness, unreliability; the state of changing without good reason), Erratic (inconsistent, wandering, having no fixed course)", "moreinfo": "From chemistry: a volatile substance easily changes states, such as by evaporating.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "volatile, ready to fly off (vol = fly): inconstant or explosive.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "warranted", "pos": "adj", "pron": "WAR-en-ted", "also": "warrant (verb, noun)", "d1": "Justified, authorized (warrant can mean to justify or a justification, but can also mean to vouch for or guarantee)", "sent": "The pundit’s comments don’t even warrant a response from our organization—they were mere name-calling, not suitable for public discourse.\nYour criticism of Anne is unwarranted—as your assistant, she has done everything you’ve asked her to do.\nHe doesn’t have his documents with him, but I’ll warrant that he is indeed a certified forklift operator.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "A warrant can, of course, be a legal justification (as in every cop movie ever)—such as permission given to police by a judge to enter and search a suspect’s home.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "warranted: there is a warrant, a justification, for it.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "wary", "pos": "adj", "pron": "WAIR-ee", "also": "", "d1": "Watchful, motivated by caution, on guard against danger", "sent": "Be <b>wary</b> of anyone who tells you that “anyone” can get rich with some special plan or scheme.", "related": "Chary is a synonym (there are very few rhyming synonyms in English!), Leery (suspicious or wary), Vigilant (keenly watchful or alert in order to guard against danger)", "moreinfo": "", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "wary: beware and stay on guard against danger.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "whereas", "pos": "conjunction", "pron": "wair-AZZ", "also": "", "d1": "While on the contrary, considering that", "sent": "Mr. Katsoulas had always assumed his son would take over the family business, <b>whereas</b> his son had always assumed he would go away to college and never come back.\n<b>Whereas</b> peppers and squash are technically fruits, they are typically considered vegetables for culinary purposes.", "related": "", "moreinfo": "Legal statements often begin with whereas, as in “Whereas the plaintiff was living with the defendant, and whereas she had lent him her car…”", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "whereas: \"where as\" the opposite holds, in contrast.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "whimsical", "pos": "adj", "pron": "WIM-zick-ull", "also": "", "d1": "Marked or motivated by whims (odd, fanciful ideas); erratic, unpredictable", "sent": "Alice in Wonderland is a famously <b>whimsical</b> story in which a little girl falls down a rabbit hole and finds a strange and at times absurd world.\nShe enjoyed a <b>whimsical</b> day at the seashore— no plan, just wandering around and making sand castles as the mood struck.", "related": "Capricious (synonym), Arbitrary (based entirely on one’s discretion; capricious, unreasonable, or having no basis), Lark (merry adventure), Mercurial (changing moods)", "moreinfo": "Whimsical is generally positive, but it depends on context—fanciful behavior that’s fun on a date would not be so fun coming from your boss (Do this! No, that! Let’s have office yoga! Why aren’t you working harder?)", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "whimsical: full of whims, playfully unpredictable.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "wily", "pos": "adj", "pron": "WHILE-ee", "also": "wiles (plural noun)", "d1": "Crafty, cunning, characterized by tricks or artifice", "sent": "The <b>wily</b> criminal stole eight laptops by simply walking into a branch of a large company, introducing himself as the laptop repairman, and waiting for people to hand him their computers.\nShe was quite offended when her coworker suggested that she use her “feminine wiles” to make the sale.", "related": "Artifice (trickery, especially as part of a strategy), Finesse (use tact or diplomacy; employ a deceptive strategy), Duplicity (deceit, double-dealing, acting in two different ways for the purpose of deception)", "moreinfo": "“Feminine wiles” refers to artful or beguiling behavior that a typically attractive woman uses to get what she wants—which could be as innocent as a romance, or something more devious.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "wily: full of wiles, sly as Wile E. Coyote.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "zeal", "pos": "noun", "pron": "ZEEL", "also": "zealot (noun)", "d1": "Great fervor or enthusiasm for a cause, person, etc., tireless diligence in furthering that cause; passion, ardor", "sent": "Whether you agree with their views or not, you have to admit that the employees of PETA have great <b>zeal</b> for animal rights—most work for less than $25,000 a year, and often participate in protests that get them shouted at or even arrested.", "related": "Ardent (very passionate), Fanatical (excessively devoted, enthusiastic, or zealous in an uncritical way). Also Fervent, Fervid, and Perfervid all mean “passionate, fiery, deeply enthusiastic.”", "moreinfo": "The original Zealots were a Jewish sect that fiercely resisted Roman rule.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "zeal: burning, tireless enthusiasm and passion for a cause.", "coll": "Essential"}, {"w": "zenith", "pos": "noun", "pron": "ZEE-nith", "also": "", "d1": "High point, culmination", "sent": "At the <b>zenith</b> of her career, the actress could command $5 million per film. Now, she is mostly seen in made-for-TV movies.", "related": "Acme, Summit, Pinnacle (synonyms), Apex (vertix, tip, point), Apogee (high point, point at which the moon is furthest from the Earth)", "moreinfo": "The opposite of the zenith is the nadir, or lowest point. Both words are terms from astronomy, referring to points directly above and below the observer on an imaginary sphere on which celestial bodies appear to be projected. On the GRE, these words will be used metaphorically —the nadir of one’s struggles, the zenith of one’s success.", "syn": "", "d2": "", "ex": "", "img": "", "set": "Essential 20", "setNum": 20, "hook": "zenith: the peak, the highest point in the sky (opposite of nadir).", "coll": "Essential"}];